var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Analysis-Interfaces","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"Analysis pipelines read a run through its files; nothing couples them to the running simulation in memory. Streaming consumers follow an active run, stationary consumers open a finished one (Two Consumer Classes, below). Consumers may be written in any language, and any number of them may read one run at a time.","title":"Analysis Interfaces"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Design-Principles","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"Consumers are read/copy-only. Analysis processes must never create, modify, move, or delete anything inside a run directory. Data a pipeline needs to own is copied out to consumer-managed storage.\nConcurrent consumers need no locks. The event logs, holes.csv and the metrics profile are append-only; a batch directory appears by an atomic rename and changes afterward only by retention pruning; descriptor.toml is renamed into place whole. The other files are written in place: the mission-start files before the heartbeats appear, the post-processing products before the terminal sentinel. With rule 1 respected no locking protocol is needed.\nThe contract is invariant under speed_up and under the clock mode. A consumer developed against an accelerated run (speed_up = 3600) works unchanged against a real-time run (speed_up = 1.0) or a tick-mode run (simulation.mode = \"tick\"); only the wall-clock arrival cadence differs. Both modes write the heartbeats and the anchor's horizon; a wall run adds its speed-up and nominal wall deadline. Development runs accelerated or on the tick clock (Reproducibility below); rehearsal runs at mission cadence.\nconfig_snapshot.toml is the only source of derived quantities. Sample rates, segment/batch geometry, and session parameters are read from the run's own snapshot, never from the live config.toml.\nThe public surface is this contract, the configuration schema, and the entry-point scripts. Semantic versioning applies to those three: a change that alters what a consumer reads from a run directory, rejects a configuration that was accepted, or changes a script's arguments or products is a major release. A correction that brings a writer into line with this contract, where the contract had recorded the writer's deviation from it, is a defect fix, listed on the Upgrading and Support page. It comes in a minor release when it changes what a consumer reads from the runs of the shipped scenarios on the tick clock, as 3.3.0 did in moving the instants of events_rx.csv to the completion of the transfer, and in a patch release when it leaves them unchanged. The Julia modules and functions are the implementation and may change between minor releases; the API pages document them for contributors. The exception is RunAdapter: its public names are versioned with this contract.","title":"Design Principles"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Two-Consumer-Classes","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"A streaming consumer attaches to an active run and reacts to arrivals as they happen: a sliding-window search that scores every window the moment its data are on the ground, an alert generator, an online estimator that updates a statistic sample by sample. It owns state (what is covered, what is pending, what is a hole), sees the data out of temporal order, and must decide what to do with windows that may never fill.\n\nA stationary consumer opens a finished run as a fixed record: a post-hoc replay of the arrival stream for a latency study, a batch analysis of the delivered series with its gaps declared, a comparison across runs. It may read everything, including the products post-processing derives from the record, and nothing it reads will change.\n\n Streaming (active run) Stationary (finished run)\nSentinel to check RUN_ACTIVE present, heartbeats fresh during the mission phase RUN_COMPLETE or RUN_ABORTED; either may close a truncated record (Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels)\nSource of truth events_rx.csv, tailed, plus the batch directories it names the batch directories, the two event logs, mission_profile.csv, and everything post-processing derived from them\nOrder of the data arrival order: the live front FIFO, the backlog LIFO behind it any order; the record is complete\nWhat can still change new batches, lost and pruned events, a declared gap closing, the terminal sentinel nothing\nGaps holes that may close (undelivered yet) or are permanent (lost, a generation gap) declared intervals with a cause\nState the consumer's: coverage, pending windows, horizon none required; the mask timeline carries the live view at every snapshot\nRead with Streaming Consumption Stationary Analysis","title":"Two Consumer Classes"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#The-Run-Directory-Contract","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"data/runs/<RUN_ID>/ contents, from a consumer's perspective:\n\nArtifact Writer Consumer access\nground/<BATCH>/ receiver Read/copy. The delivery surface (see below).\nlost/<BATCH>/ receiver Read/copy. Retry-exhausted batches, preserved but never ground-available.\nevents_rx.csv receiver (single writer) Tail/read. The authoritative arrival feed.\nholes.csv receiver, emitter, and supervisor through one shared writer; post-processing settles it Tail/read. The declared-hole feed: one row per hole of the delivered record, appended when the producer declares it (Event Feeds).\nevents_tx.csv emitter; the supervisor appends the STREAM gap pair while the emitter is down (one live writer at a time) Tail/read. Generation and transmission milestones.\nmission_profile.csv receiver Tail/read. Link and buffer metrics at a change-driven cadence, columns SimTime, WallTime, Mission_Day, Hours_Elapsed, Bandwidth_Pct, Onboard_Buffer, Link_Buffer, Ground_Total, Ground_Live, Ground_Arch, Nominal_Bandwidth_Pct, Lost_Count, Retry_Count, Disruption_Active.\nmasks/ post-processing Read/copy. Batch-state timeline, batch epochs, and the gap table.\nconfig_snapshot.toml pipeline (at startup) Read. Exact run parameters and the [provenance] section (Snapshot provenance).\nmanifest_snapshot.toml pipeline (at startup) Read. The manifest of the environment the run was resolved on, with the version of every dependency; with the source identity of config_snapshot.toml it fixes the code state (Source identity).\nRUN_ACTIVE / RUN_POSTPROCESSING / RUN_COMPLETE / RUN_ABORTED pipeline Read. Lifecycle sentinels (see below).\nclock_anchor.toml pipeline (at mission start) Read. Persisted mission-clock anchor: mode, wall_epoch, start_sim_time, the mission end horizon_sim, and, for a wall-mode run, speed_up and the nominal wall deadline deadline_wall (anchors of wall runs before 2.8.0 carry no horizon_sim).\ncomponent_events.csv supervisor (single writer) Tail/read. Component lifecycle record, columns SimTime, Component, Event, with Component either emitter or receiver and Event down or restart (runs before 2.8.0 may also hold stalled and recovered).\nemitter_alive / receiver_alive the driver (heartbeats, both modes) Read mtime. Liveness of the mission driver, not of either component: the driver touches both files together, at most once per second, from the start of the mission phase, and deletes them when it ends. Both are absent before the mission phase (setup, pre-population) and after it; a stale mtime while RUN_ACTIVE stands without RUN_POSTPROCESSING means the driver is stalled. A component outage is read from component_events.csv.\nmarkers.csv pipeline (at mission start) Read. Event markers of the run, columns SimTime, Label, PayloadRow (PayloadRow: the payload row of the instant): the instants at which the alert-latency metric is evaluated (alert_latency_markers.csv). Absent when the scenario declares no markers.\nlabel_spans.csv pipeline (at mission start; post-processing regenerates it only when absent) Read. Every labeled interval of the scenario on the payload grid, columns Class, Type, Label, RowFirst, RowLast, ContentStart, ContentEnd, rows as in masks/gaps.csv. Class is label (a [[labels]] span a payload exporter declares in payload rows), blackout or recovery (the phases of a link disruption, Type the event type), generation_gap (a scheduled generation gap as configured, Type the event type; the realized gap of the gap table may differ by the segment-grid snap), contact (Type = pass), low_latency (Type = triggered for a marker's period, scheduled otherwise), or initial_downtime.\ndescriptor.toml post-processing (last) Read/copy. The run's identity for a downstream collection: schema (\"deepspacetelemetry-run/2\" since 3.0, /1 before), run_id, the producer's package_version and source identity (the fields of Source identity), config_sha256, the clock mode, mission_days, start_sim_time, payload_origin, rng_seed, the payload geometry (sample_rate, segment_duration_sec, batch_size, points_per_batch), payload_columns, the external record's external_data_sha256 and external_data_rows, written_at, and under [products] the bytes and sha256 of every table present; RunAdapter.run_descriptor returns it. Written as descriptor.toml.partial and renamed into place (since 3.2.0), so a reader finds the whole file or none.\ndelivery_delay.csv post-processing Read. Measurement-to-ground delay of every generated batch, columns Batch, Live, ContentEnd, AvailableAt, Delay_Hours, LowLatency, Due (AvailableAt and Delay_Hours missing for undelivered batches; LowLatency flags deliveries inside a low-latency period, also where it overlaps a nominal pass; Due, since 3.3.0, whether ContentEnd plus post_processing.delivery_requirement_hours falls at or before the mission end); plots/delivery_delay.png renders the distribution against the requirement.\nalert_latency.csv post-processing Read. Alert-latency curves: the median and quartiles of the ground availability of the look-back data of a live event, for the realized doctrine and the counterfactual FIFO drain, columns Lookback_Hours, N_Alerts, LIFO_Median_Hours, LIFO_Q25_Hours, LIFO_Q75_Hours, FIFO_Median_Hours, FIFO_Q25_Hours, FIFO_Q75_Hours over the windows delivered in full under both doctrines, then, since 3.3.0, N_Alerts_All, N_Censored_LIFO, N_Censored_FIFO, LIFO_KM_Median_Hours, LIFO_KM_Q25_Hours, LIFO_KM_Q75_Hours, FIFO_KM_Median_Hours, FIFO_KM_Q25_Hours, FIFO_KM_Q75_Hours: the Kaplan–Meier estimates over every live event delivered under the realized doctrine, a window still incomplete at the run end censored there and one holding discarded data, or a batch lost for good under the realized doctrine, never complete (NaN where a quantile is not reached; Alert-Latency Metrology). plots/alert_latency.png renders the Kaplan–Meier curves.\nalert_latency_markers.csv post-processing Read. The alert latency at every event marker, columns Label, Marker, Batch, Lookback_Hours, LIFO_Hours, FIFO_Hours.\nproducts.h5 post-processing (hdf5_export) Read/copy. The tabular products, the mask timeline, and the provenance attributes in one HDF5 file (HDF5 Product Export), without the batch payloads, the clock anchor, the sentinels and the logs; regenerable from the CSV products.\nplots/ post-processing Read/copy. Figures mission_summary_global, one session_<stem>_detail per contact (none when post_processing.session_figures is off; at most session_figures_max evenly spaced contacts when that cap is set), alert_latency, delivery_delay, and state_raster (the batch-state timeline as a raster), each as .png and .pdf. Every figure but mission_summary_global is gated by its [post_processing] flag, and a sweep run draws none. scripts/postprocessing/generate_gif.jl adds telemetry_animation.gif (with --web, telemetry_animation_web.gif).\npublication/ post-processing ([post_processing.publication]) Read/copy. Journal-width figure export (the directory is the default of [post_processing.publication] export_dir, which may lie outside the run directory), <stem>__<run_id>.<format>, with a PROVENANCE.toml sidecar ([export]: run_id, run_directory, exported_at, column_width_mm, format, package_version, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty, config_snapshot_sha256, figures).\nmasks/gaps.csv post-processing Read. Every hole of the delivered record as a declared interval with its cause (Stationary Analysis); the first product post-processing writes.\nmasks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv post-processing (generate_mask_timeline) Read/copy. The batch-state timeline: one row per metrics snapshot, one column per batch, states 0–4, described under Stationary Analysis; also the source of the HDF5 masks/timeline group.\nmasks/batch_epochs.csv post-processing (with the mask timeline) Read. Batch → epoch map, columns Batch, GenSimTime, ContentEpoch, PayloadRow: GenSimTime the finalization instant from the event log, ContentEpoch and PayloadRow the first-sample timestamp and payload row from the batch metadata. Written with the mask timeline (post_processing.generate_mask_timeline on, mission_profile.csv non-empty) when the run has gen rows.\nHALT operator The one sanctioned external write: touch HALT stops both components cleanly at their next iteration; the pipeline removes the file once the components have joined, before post-processing.\nemitter.log, receiver.log, supervisor.log logger Read. Diagnostics for a human reader, not a parsed interface: the two component logs and the supervisor's [SUPERVISOR], [POST] and [CONFIG] records of the mission and its post-processing, each rotated to <name>#k.log at retention.log_rotate_mb.\nonboard/, link/ emitter/receiver Off-limits. Internal staging; the emitter counts in-flight batches from the link/ listing, so a slot frees when the receiver moves a batch out.\n\nSeveral files exist only under a condition: events_rx.csv from the receiver's first event, holes.csv from the first declared hole (a run without holes has none), component_events.csv from the first component failure, manifest_snapshot.toml when the active environment has a manifest, alert_latency.csv when a live batch was delivered, alert_latency_markers.csv with it when the run has markers, and delivery_delay.csv when a batch was generated. A product written whole may stand beside #k backups of itself (masks/gaps#1.csv, descriptor#1.toml) once post-processing has been repeated, and descriptor.toml.partial exists while the descriptor is written; neither is part of the contract.\n\nA batch directory contains metadata.json and one seg_<id>.csv per segment. A segment file has one column per payload column, under its name: the one Amplitude column of a synthetic run or of an external run without physics.external_data_columns, otherwise the listed columns of the external file. A synthetic run writes the flag values 0.0 and 1.0, an external run the ingested samples. The column names of a run are payload_columns of descriptor.toml and, for an external run, external_data_columns under [provenance] of the snapshot. Every column shares the payload grid, the batches, and the link, so one hole table covers all of them.\n\nmetadata.json key Content\nbatch_id the global batch index k\nsegment_count the number of segment files\ncontent_epoch mission timestamp of the payload's first sample: the physical epoch the data belong to\npayload_row payload row of that first sample (Batch Identity → Sample Interval)\ncreated_at mission instant at which the batch was finalized and became transmittable: the content end of its last segment, in either clock mode\nmarkers optional: the labels of the event markers the payload holds\n\nThe order of the keys carries no meaning. Segment files carry no timestamps: sample k of a batch lies at content_epoch + (k − 1) / sample_rate and is payload row payload_row + k − 1.\n\nInstants in the CSV tables (DeclaredAt, SimTime, WallTime, GenSimTime, ContentEpoch, ContentStart, ContentEnd, AvailableAt, Marker) are written as yyyy-mm-ddTHH:MM:SS.s, the seconds with one to three decimals (2035-01-04T12:00:36.0, 2026-09-24T02:01:17.051). The package fixes that form on write and the column types on read (TelemetryCore.write_table, TelemetryCore.read_table), independent of the defaults of the CSV library in use. Instants in metadata.json and in the TOML files are written by string(::DateTime), without a fraction at whole seconds (2035-01-04T12:00:36).\n\nBatches are delivered by an atomic same-filesystem mv: a directory visible under ground/ is complete, and afterward only the retention custodian modifies it (Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels).","title":"The Run-Directory Contract"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Snapshot-provenance","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"The [provenance] section of config_snapshot.toml:\n\nKey Content\nconfig_sha256 SHA-256 of the configuration as sorted TOML without [provenance]; its first eight hex digits open a generated run identifier, RUN_cfg=<hash>_pid=<pid>_t=<yyyymmdd_HHMMSS>; a run ID passed to run_full_sim.jl, or a sweep's <prefix>_seed<seed>, carries no hash\npayload_origin content instant of payload row 1, start_sim_time less initial_downtime_days, as an ISO-8601 string (Batch Identity → Sample Interval)\nexternal_data_path, external_data_rows, external_data_sha256, declared_sample_rate, external_data_columns identity of the input record; external data only\n[provenance.platform] package_version; the source identity identity_source, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty, repo_url, repo_rev (Source identity); reader_tree_sha1 (since 3.3.0); hostname, os, cpu_model, logical_cores, total_memory_gb, julia_version, versioninfo, julia_threads, blas_threads","title":"Snapshot provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Provenance-conventions","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"The run directory follows the conventions of DrWatson's provenance tooling without the dependency. Two concurrent components write the layout batch by batch and the provenance is recorded at setup, which the whole-file semantics of tagsave and safesave do not cover. The correspondence:\n\nDrWatson This package\nsavename: parameter-derived identifier TelemetryCore.generate_run_id: RUN_cfg=<8 hex of config_sha256>_pid=<pid>_t=<yyyymmdd_HHMMSS>, key=value fields in savename order\ntagsave: the git commit stored with the result TelemetryCore.setup_run_dir: config_snapshot.toml with [provenance.platform] (source identity, hostname, hardware, Julia and package versions) beside manifest_snapshot.toml\nsafesave: #k backup rotation, never an overwrite TelemetryCore.backup_existing and backup_existing_dir: the same <name>#k rotation for every product written whole and for a batch directory that meets a namesake; a run directory is never reused (setup_run_dir refuses a non-empty one)\nproduce_or_load: cached expensive stages not provided: the mission phase is a live two-component process, and every post-processing product is regenerable from the run directory by Supervisor.post_process! or the function of its stage\ndatadir, projectdir TelemetryCore.run_directory: <data root>/runs/<RUN_ID>, the data root being TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT[] (default: the directory named by DEEPSPACETELEMETRY_DATA when set, else data/ under the package directory)","title":"Provenance conventions"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Source-identity","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"A run records the identity of the package source it was made from in [provenance.platform] of config_snapshot.toml and again in descriptor.toml (since 2.6.1; TelemetryCore.source_identity). The identity is defined for a git checkout and for a copy Pkg installed under ~/.julia/packages, which has no .git.\n\nField Content\nidentity_source How the identity was determined: work_tree (the package directory is the top level of a git checkout, or since 3.3.0 a directory tracked in a clone of this repository, as the headless package), manifest (a copy installed by Pkg, matched by UUID to its entry in the manifest of the active environment), or directory (neither; the content of the directory alone).\ngit_tree_sha1 The git tree hash of the source: HEAD^{tree} of the checkout (HEAD:<path> for a tracked directory), the git-tree-sha1 of the manifest entry, or the hash of the package directory computed as git computes it (leaving out .git, the untracked manifests, and, under a data root inside it, everything but the .gitkeep placeholders the repository tracks). Never empty.\nreader_tree_sha1 The tree hash of the reader package: its tree at HEAD in a clean clone of this repository, otherwise the hash of its directory computed as above. The identity of the configuration and I/O layer where the package identity does not hold it, as for the headless package in a container image (since 3.3.0).\ngit_commit The checkout's HEAD; for an installed copy, the repo-rev of the manifest entry when it is a full 40-digit commit hash, as in a [sources] pin by commit. Empty for a copy pinned by branch or tag, one installed from a registry, and a bare directory.\ngit_dirty The code that ran differs from the recorded identity: uncommitted changes to tracked files of the checkout, or an installed directory whose tree hash differs from its manifest entry's (a [RUN] warning on the console at startup, before supervisor.log exists).\nrepo_url, repo_rev The repo-url and repo-rev of the manifest entry of a copy added by URL; empty otherwise.\n\ngit_tree_sha1 identifies the code: with git_dirty = false it fixes the source, however the package was obtained. Equal content gives an equal value, the git-tree-sha1 Pkg writes into a manifest and the output of git rev-parse <commit>^{tree}. A non-empty git_commit has git_tree_sha1 as its tree and adds the history. repo_url and repo_rev say where an installed copy came from and identify nothing, as a branch or a tag moves. A downstream collection keys a run's producer by git_tree_sha1 and git_dirty and copies git_commit when present. A run with git_dirty = true cannot be reproduced from its recorded identity. Runs made before 2.6.1 record only git_commit and git_dirty, with git_commit empty when made from an installed copy.","title":"Source identity"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Availability-Window-and-Lifecycle-Sentinels","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"With [retention] disabled (the default), no payload is deleted from a run directory: delivered batches persist for the run's lifetime, and only the interactive scripts/maintenance/cleanup.jl deletes them.\n\nWith retention.enabled = true, the receiver's custodian bounds the delivered-payload footprint: a batch's payload is guaranteed readable for retention.grace_hours of mission time after its ingested event, and a pipeline copies what it needs within that window. Beyond it, once the payload tally exceeds retention.high_watermark_gb, the oldest-ingested batches lose their seg_*.csv files. The batch directory remains with its metadata.json and gains a zero-byte PRUNED marker, and a pruned row (state-preserving, Attempt = 0) is appended to events_rx.csv. Consumers must tolerate both the marker and the event value. Event logs, metrics, masks, snapshots and lost/ are not pruned; the replay and the mask products read only those and the batch metadata. Retention is time-based: a consumer registers nothing, as registering would make it a writer.\n\nRun lifecycle is signaled by sentinel files in the run directory. RUN_ACTIVE exists while the pipeline may still write. At the end of the lifecycle RUN_COMPLETE replaces it, also after a component failure, the abort policy or a HALT: the sentinel marks the end of writing, not a full-length record (compare the last SimTime of mission_profile.csv with the anchor's horizon_sim). RUN_ABORTED replaces it instead when an exception ends the pipeline. A consumer may treat either terminal sentinel as the signal to switch from tailing to batch processing.\n\nRUN_POSTPROCESSING stands beside RUN_ACTIVE from the moment the emitter and the receiver have stopped until the lifecycle ends: while it is present the payload, the event logs, and the metrics profile are final, and only derived products (metrology tables, masks, figures, exports) are still written. The gap table, the settled hole feed, and the metrology tables come first; descriptor.toml comes last.\n\nThe terminal sentinels are written by the supervisor process as it leaves. A process killed from outside (by the kernel's out-of-memory handler, by a scheduler at its time limit) leaves RUN_ACTIVE behind with no process to remove it. Such a directory is a stranded run: its heartbeats are stale and nothing writes to it. It is settled with\n\njulia scripts/postprocessing/complete_run.jl <RUN_ID>\n\nwhich runs the post-processing again from the data on disk, with the settings of the run's own snapshot, and replaces RUN_ACTIVE by RUN_COMPLETE when the mission phase had ended, or by RUN_ABORTED when the process died before the horizon and the record is truncated. The mission phase counts as ended when RUN_POSTPROCESSING is present or, failing that, when the SimTime of the last metrics row lies within 1 % of the mission span (at least 1 s) of the anchor's horizon_sim (for an anchor without one, its WallTime against the wall deadline). The script refuses a wall run whose nominal deadline has not passed and, without --force, a run whose top-level files or plots/ were written to within the last minute.","title":"Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Component-Outages-and-Generation-Gaps","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"The two components run under a supervisor ([supervision] in the config): on a component failure the policy aborts the run cleanly, continues one-sided, or restarts the component (bounded attempts). Consumers observe outages through component_events.csv. A receiver outage acts on the record as a ground-station blackout does: the backlog accumulates, then drains. An emitter outage is a generation gap: the restarted instrument resumes at the first segment boundary at or after the current mission time, on the sampling grid of the payload origin.\n\nA gap_start / gap_end row pair in events_tx.csv bounds each of three kinds of generation gap, told apart by Batch:\n\nBatch Gap gap_start gap_end\nSTREAM emitter outage content end of the last batch finalized before the outage: the instant of row payload_row + points_per_batch of the highest payload_row stamp, or the payload origin when no batch exists resumption instant\nSCHEDULED scheduled generation gap (a disruption event with affects = \"generation\") first discarded content instant scheduled end rounded up to the segment grid\nRECORDER recorder overflow (the on-board buffer at telemetry.onboard_capacity_days) first discarded content instant content epoch of the first batch recorded again\n\nThe rows of the partial batch the failed emitter held are lost with it and fall inside the STREAM gap. The pair is written only when a replacement emitter starts; under on_component_failure = \"continue\", or once max_restarts is exhausted, the outage is not recorded as a gap. For a SCHEDULED gap, [gap_start, gap_end) is the content not recorded. During a recorder overflow the instrument keeps generating and the data are dropped on board, so the grid continues without a jump.\n\nNo gap shifts the sampling grid: segment identifiers and payload rows continue from the content epoch, and a batch's rows come from its payload_row stamp (Batch Identity → Sample Interval). The mask replay treats gap events as state-preserving.","title":"Component Outages & Generation Gaps"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Event-Feeds","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"events_rx.csv — columns SimTime, Batch, Event, Attempt; Attempt is populated on every row. The SimTime of an ingested, retry or lost row is the completion instant of the transfer that resolved the batch, at which the row is appended (Packet Loss Channel Models); before 3.3.0 it was the start of the transfer:\n\ningested — the batch reached the ground archive; Attempt is the number of failed attempts before the successful transfer (0 for a batch reconciled at receiver start-up). Ordering guarantee: the payload is moved into ground/ before this row is appended, so a consumer that reads an ingested event may open the batch immediately.\nretry — a transfer attempt was lost; the batch remains on the link and is re-served no earlier than one round-trip light time later, other in-flight batches first (a restarted receiver does not keep the deferral and may retry at once). Attempt counts failed attempts so far.\nlost — retry budget exhausted (Attempt = the resolved retry limit + 1: max_retries + 1 under on_loss = \"retransmit\", 1 under on_loss = \"drop\"); the batch was moved to lost/ (also before the row is appended) and will never become ground-available.\npruned — the retention custodian removed the payload of a delivered batch (Attempt = 0); the PRUNED marker and metadata.json remain (Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels).\n\nevents_tx.csv — columns SimTime, Batch, Event:\n\ngen — the batch was finalized on board.\ntx — the batch was placed on the downlink.\nmarker — SimTime is an event-marker instant and Batch the batch holding it, appended when that batch is finalized; state-preserving.\ngap_start / gap_end — the pair bounding a generation gap (Batch = STREAM, SCHEDULED, or RECORDER, see above); state-preserving.\n\nholes.csv — columns DeclaredAt, Cause, Batch, RowFirst, RowLast, ContentStart, ContentEnd. DeclaredAt is the mission instant of the declaration; the other columns are those of masks/gaps.csv. The file is append-only, with rows in declaration order, and every writer declares at the current mission instant, so DeclaredAt is non-decreasing (since 3.3.0; before, a lost row could precede the row above it by up to one transfer time).\n\nCause Declared by When\nlost, pruned receiver at its lost or pruned row\nscheduled emitter when it skips the gap\nrecorder emitter at the finalization of the first batch recorded again\nstream supervisor before the replacement emitter starts\nundelivered, not_generated, a recorder gap still open post-processing after the mission phase, with the nominal mission end as DeclaredAt\n\nA row is appended after the event row that announces the same hole, so a consumer that reads the feed alone is never ahead of the event logs. On a finished run the feed and the gap table hold the same holes.","title":"Event Feeds"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Batch-Identity-Sample-Interval","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"Batch names are LIVE_batch_<k> (finalized while the link is transmittable: inside a contact window, nominal pass or low-latency period, and outside a full blackout) or ARCH_batch_<k> (finalized otherwise, and every batch of the pre-population); k is the global 1-based batch index.\n\nThe payload is sampled on one grid for the whole run, anchored at the payload origin t₀ ([provenance] payload_origin in config_snapshot.toml): payload row r is the sample at t₀ + (r − 1) / sample_rate. In external mode row r is data row r of the input CSV (header excluded); rows past its end are zeros. With several payload columns, row r holds one sample per column, all at the same instant. A batch with content epoch e starts at the row\n\npayload_row = (e − t₀) · sample_rate + 1\n\nstamped in its metadata.json, and holds the rows payload_row … payload_row + points_per_batch − 1, with\n\npoints_per_batch = round(sample_rate × segment_duration_sec) × batch_size\n\n(all three from config_snapshot.toml [physics]). Segment identifiers are grid positions as well: seg_<id>.csv opens at t₀ + (id − 1) · segment_duration_sec, so identifiers never repeat within a run and a gap skips the identifiers of the segments it suppresses. The mapping is row-exact when sample_rate × segment_duration_sec is an integer. Otherwise validation warns, and the rows of consecutive segments still remain consecutive and disjoint, with payload_row = (id − 1) · round(sample_rate × segment_duration_sec) + 1.\n\nBatch indices stay contiguous across generation gaps while the rows do not: a batch's rows come from its payload_row stamp, which every batch carries, not from its index. On a run whose events_tx.csv has no gap_start row the stamp of batch k is (k − 1) · points_per_batch + 1.","title":"Batch Identity → Sample Interval"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Streaming-Consumption","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"A streaming consumer follows an active run through its arrival feed. The loop has five parts: attach, follow the feed, maintain coverage, treat the holes, and leave when the run ends.\n\nAttach. Check that RUN_ACTIVE and clock_anchor.toml exist: the anchor, markers.csv and label_spans.csv are written once the on-board buffer is pre-populated, as the mission phase starts, and from then on receiver_alive refreshes about once per wall second in both modes. Then read once:\n\nconfig_snapshot.toml: the geometry from [physics] (sample_rate, segment_duration_sec, batch_size, hence points_per_batch), the mission epoch [simulation] start_sim_time, the instant of payload row 1 [provenance] payload_origin (see Batch Identity → Sample Interval), and, for an external payload, the record length external_data_rows, beyond which the producer pads with zeros.\nclock_anchor.toml, if the consumer paces itself against mission time: wall_epoch, start_sim_time and speed_up give the nominal mission instant of a wall instant (a host that lags runs behind it), horizon_sim is the mission end and deadline_wall its nominal wall instant. A tick-mode anchor has no speed_up and no deadline_wall.\nlabel_spans.csv, present from mission start: the labeled intervals of the scenario on the payload grid.\n\nFollow the feed. events_rx.csv is append-only with a single writer. Keep the count of rows consumed and read the rows beyond it on every poll; by the ordering guarantee (Event Feeds), every ingested batch so read is already in ground/. The four event values and the consumer's response:\n\nEvent Meaning Response\ningested the batch is in ground/; Attempt counts the failed transfers before it map the batch to its rows, read its segments, extend the coverage\nretry a transfer was lost; the batch stays on the link and is re-served after one round-trip light time none (state-preserving); it tells the consumer that the batch is coming late\nlost the retry budget is exhausted; the batch is in lost/ and will never be on the ground a permanent hole: discard or gap-handle the windows that cross it, never wait on it\npruned the retention custodian removed the payload of a delivered batch; metadata.json and a PRUNED marker remain the rows stay covered for a consumer that copied the payload within retention.grace_hours; otherwise a hole\n\nThe poll interval follows the cadence of the run: one segment per segment_duration_sec / speed_up wall seconds, a batch every batch_size segments. Polling a few times per batch period is enough; at speed_up = 1 that is minutes. A tick run has no wall mapping: arrivals come as fast as the host processes them, a consumer following one polls as fast as it can, and a consumer is best developed against the finished record. For a long run, tail by byte offset; RunAdapter.FeedCursor does so for events_rx.csv and holes.csv.\n\nMaintain coverage. Map each batch to its rows (Batch Identity → Sample Interval) and keep the delivered rows as a set of disjoint intervals. Arrival is out of temporal order: live batches stream FIFO with priority, and the blind-spot backlog backfills LIFO (newest first) behind them, so coverage grows backward in time from each live front, contiguous behind it, session by session. A window is evaluable when the coverage spans it (fully, or to an acceptable fraction with the holes known). Evaluate it once, when that first becomes true, and do not re-evaluate it when later batches arrive behind it unless the analysis is meant to.\n\nTreat the holes. A row not yet covered is in one of three states, and the feed and the transmission log tell them apart:\n\nPending: the batch was generated (gen row in events_tx.csv) and is on board or on the link. It will arrive, late; a window that needs it waits. The retry rows say how many attempts it has survived.\nPermanent: the batch is lost, or the rows were never generated: a generation gap bounded by a gap_start / gap_end pair of events_tx.csv (Component Outages & Generation Gaps). Declare the hole when those rows appear. holes.csv carries each permanent hole as one row when it is declared, and a consumer may tail it in place of pairing the rows; the pending state is read from the transmission log.\nUnknown: no gen row yet, because mission time has not reached the rows. Beyond the current mission instant nothing is pending.\n\nAn order horizon bounds how long a consumer waits for a pending batch before scoring without it; at the horizon the pending hole becomes a declared one. The realized arrival delays of the shipped scenarios (delivery_delay.csv) are the reference for choosing it.\n\nLeave. Once RUN_POSTPROCESSING stands, the payload, the event logs and mission_profile.csv are final; once RUN_COMPLETE or RUN_ABORTED replaces RUN_ACTIVE, finish as a stationary consumer. A stranded run is settled as Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels describes. The end-of-run rows of holes.csv land during post-processing: read the feed once more after the terminal sentinel.\n\nA minimal consumer in Julia, with the package as a reader (consumers in other languages read the same files):\n\nusing DeepSpaceTelemetry: TelemetryCore\n\nsegment_files(dir) = sort(\n    filter(f -> occursin(r\"^seg_\\d+\\.csv$\", f), readdir(dir));\n    by = f -> parse(Int, match(r\"\\d+\", f).match),\n)\nread_payload(dir) = reduce(vcat, (TelemetryCore.load_segment(joinpath(dir, f)).data for f in segment_files(dir)))  # samples × columns\n\nfunction follow_run(run_id; poll_seconds = 5.0)\n    run_dir = TelemetryCore.run_directory(run_id)\n    cfg = TelemetryCore.load_run_config(run_dir)\n    phys = cfg[\"physics\"]\n    points_per_batch = round(Int, phys[\"sample_rate\"] * phys[\"segment_duration_sec\"]) * phys[\"batch_size\"]\n    covered = UnitRange{Int}[]            # delivered payload rows\n    holes = Pair{UnitRange{Int},Symbol}[] # permanent holes with their cause\n    consumed = 0\n    rx_path = joinpath(run_dir, \"events_rx.csv\")  # absent until the first arrival\n    while true\n        finished = !isfile(joinpath(run_dir, \"RUN_ACTIVE\"))  # tested before the read\n        rx = isfile(rx_path) ? TelemetryCore.read_table(rx_path) : nothing\n        for row in (rx === nothing ? () : eachrow(rx[(consumed + 1):end, :]))\n            consumed += 1\n            row.Event == \"retry\" && continue  # the batch is still under link/\n            stage = row.Event == \"lost\" ? \"lost\" : \"ground\"\n            meta = TelemetryCore.read_batch_metadata(joinpath(run_dir, stage, row.Batch))\n            row_first = meta[\"payload_row\"]\n            rows = row_first:(row_first + points_per_batch - 1)\n            if row.Event == \"ingested\"\n                payload = read_payload(joinpath(run_dir, \"ground\", row.Batch))\n                push!(covered, rows)          # feed `payload` at `rows` to the analysis\n            elseif row.Event == \"lost\"\n                push!(holes, rows => :lost)\n            elseif row.Event == \"pruned\"\n                push!(holes, rows => :pruned)\n            end\n        end\n        finished && break\n        sleep(poll_seconds)\n    end\n    return covered, holes\nend\n\nThe generation gaps enter the same holes list from events_tx.csv: each gap_start row with Batch in STREAM, SCHEDULED, or RECORDER opens a hole at the row of its instant, closed by the matching gap_end (the conversion between an instant and a payload row is in Batch Identity → Sample Interval).\n\nThe run adapter. RunAdapter reads a run directory through the files above. A consumer in Julia calls it; a consumer in another language can take it as the reference reader:\n\nusing DeepSpaceTelemetry: RunAdapter\n\nrun = RunAdapter.open_run(\"RUN_ID\")           # a path or an identifier; checks the producer's version\nRunAdapter.run_geometry(run)                 # sample rate, segment length, batch size, payload origin, record length, clock mode\nRunAdapter.list_batches(run)                 # per batch: name, index, live?, payload rows, content epoch,\n                                             #            state ∈ :ground | :lost | :pruned\nRunAdapter.batch_record(run, \"LIVE_batch_7\") # the same record for one batch, from its metadata alone; nothing if absent\nRunAdapter.read_batch(run, \"LIVE_batch_7\"; column = 1)   # one payload column, Float32, segments in ascending id (read_batch_matrix: all columns)\nRunAdapter.read_batch_matrix(run, \"LIVE_batch_7\"; delivered = true)   # refuses a batch not on the ground (lost, pruned, absent)\nRunAdapter.arrival_events(run; after = n)    # the feed beyond the n rows already consumed, in mission-time order\nRunAdapter.delivered_rows(run; at = t)       # the payload rows on the ground at mission instant t, as disjoint intervals\nRunAdapter.run_state(run)                    # :active | :complete | :aborted, from the sentinels\nRunAdapter.run_descriptor(run)               # descriptor.toml as a Dict (empty until post-processing writes it), schema checked\nRunAdapter.markers(run)                      # the event markers with their payload row\nRunAdapter.label_spans(run)                  # the labeled intervals of the scenario, with their rows\nRunAdapter.declared_holes(run; after = n)    # the producer's hole feed beyond n rows, each with its declaration instant\ncursor = RunAdapter.FeedCursor(run, :arrivals)   # or :holes; each call below reads only the bytes appended since the last\nRunAdapter.arrival_events(cursor)            # (declared_holes(cursor) for a :holes cursor)\n\nopen_run refuses a run of a producer older than 2.6.0, the first with the declared-hole feed and the label-span table, unless told otherwise (minimum_version). declared_holes reads holes.csv: a streaming consumer learns of a permanent hole when the producer declares it, and on a finished run the feed also holds the end-of-run causes of masks/gaps.csv. A consumer following the arrival feed resolves each batch with batch_record, which reads that batch's metadata alone (list_batches reads every batch), and reads its payload with delivered = true; without it, read_batch and read_batch_matrix also serve a lost batch from lost/, for diagnostics. The adapter ships in the reader package DeepSpaceTelemetryReader (Pkg.add(url = …, subdir = \"reader\")), which a consumer adds without the figure stack.\n\nThe adapter's types (RunHandle, RunGeometry, BatchRecord, ArrivalEvent, Hole, FeedCursor), its version floor MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION, and the functions above with batch_rows, record_final, row_time and time_row are declared public and not exported: a consumer qualifies them, as in the example. Principle 5 versions them with this contract; the changelog gives the release of each.\n\nStreaming archetypes. The loop above is what a streaming engine does generically: a coverage set, a window scheduler that emits each window once, conditioning on the delivered stretch, a scorer, and an ordered commit with an order horizon for stateful estimators. Such an engine is best kept behind the adapter, which serves its follow mode on an active run and its replay mode on a finished one. A detector (a classifier, a matched filter) then scores the conditioned windows. An online estimator that requires strictly increasing sample times fits the live FIFO front only; the LIFO backfill behind it is out of order for such a consumer and is better analyzed from the finished record.","title":"Streaming Consumption"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Stationary-Analysis","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"A stationary consumer opens a finished run (RUN_COMPLETE or RUN_ABORTED) and reads a fixed record. A run that run_mission closed with RUN_ABORTED ended before post-processing: it carries no gap table, no settled holes.csv and no descriptor.toml, and complete_run.jl, which settles only a directory still holding RUN_ACTIVE, does not derive them. Three views of the same record serve different questions.\n\nThe delivered series. Concatenate the ground batches on the payload grid: batch rows from payload_row (masks/batch_epochs.csv tabulates Batch, GenSimTime, ContentEpoch, PayloadRow for every generated batch), samples from the segment files, and missing wherever no delivered batch covers a row. Post-processing writes the holes, each with a cause, as masks/gaps.csv: columns Cause, Batch, RowFirst, RowLast, ContentStart, ContentEnd, one row per hole, sorted by RowFirst. RowFirst and RowLast are payload rows, both inclusive; [ContentStart, ContentEnd) is the content interval. holes.csv and label_spans.csv share this row convention. Up to the last row of the last generated batch (for an external payload, up to external_data_rows), the rows of the table and the rows no delivered batch covers are the same set. Rows of a synthetic run after its last generated batch, such as the partial batch at the horizon or the rest of a mission whose emitter outage was not recorded as a gap, appear in neither. The causes and where the record declares each:\n\nCause Where it is declared Rows\nlost events_rx.csv row lost; the batch in lost/ with its metadata its payload_row … + points_per_batch − 1\npruned events_rx.csv row pruned; the PRUNED marker in ground/<BATCH>/ the same\nstream, scheduled, recorder gap_start / gap_end pair in events_tx.csv (STREAM, SCHEDULED, RECORDER); a gap still open at run end closes at the mission end the rows between the two instants\nundelivered a gen row in events_tx.csv with no ingested or lost row; the batch directory under onboard/ or link/ its payload_row … + points_per_batch − 1\nnot_generated external payload only, no gen row: the mission ended before the record was exhausted the rows past the last generated batch, up to external_data_rows\nnone (beyond the record, not a row of the table) external_data_rows in [provenance] (external payload) every row past it holds the producer's zero padding\n\nholes.csv holds the same rows on a finished run, each with its declaration instant (Event Feeds): read the feed for the declaration history, masks/gaps.csv for the table. The table is the reference: post-processing settles the feed from it and reports in the run log any row of the feed the table does not derive (Masks.hole_feed_excess) and alters neither file.\n\nA gap-aware statistic takes the holes as declared intervals rather than inferring them from the spacing of the delivered samples: the spacing cannot tell a lost batch from an undelivered one, and cannot see a gap before the first or after the last delivered sample.\n\nusing DeepSpaceTelemetry: TelemetryCore\n\nrun_dir = TelemetryCore.run_directory(\"RUN_ID\")\ncfg = TelemetryCore.load_run_config(run_dir)\nphys = cfg[\"physics\"]\npoints_per_batch = round(Int, phys[\"sample_rate\"] * phys[\"segment_duration_sec\"]) * phys[\"batch_size\"]\n\nholes = TelemetryCore.read_table(joinpath(run_dir, \"masks\", \"gaps.csv\"))\nrx = TelemetryCore.read_table(joinpath(run_dir, \"events_rx.csv\"))\nfirst_row = TelemetryCore.batch_payload_rows(run_dir)      # every generated batch, all stages\ndelivered = setdiff(Set(rx.Batch[rx.Event .== \"ingested\"]), Set(rx.Batch[rx.Event .== \"pruned\"]))\n\nn_rows = max(\n    maximum(holes.RowLast; init = 0),\n    maximum((first_row[name] + points_per_batch - 1 for name in delivered); init = 0),\n)\n# one column; a multi-column run fills a samples × columns matrix from load_segment(...).data\nseries = Vector{Union{Missing,Float32}}(missing, n_rows)\nfor name in delivered\n    payload = vec(read_payload(joinpath(run_dir, \"ground\", name)))   # as in the streaming example\n    r = first_row[name]\n    series[r:(r + length(payload) - 1)] .= payload\nend\n\nseries is the stationary record and holes its declared gaps with causes, each row the interval RowFirst:RowLast on the same grid. Masks.gap_table(run_dir) computes the same table from the record.\n\nThe arrival stream, replayed. For a latency study, replay events_rx.csv in SimTime order as the arrival stream and drive the same consumer logic as in Streaming Consumption. The framework's own mask products are computed from this event log, so the replayed availability is exactly what a live consumer saw. A streaming analysis is developed and regression-tested this way against recorded runs before it follows a live one, and two routing rules or two link models are compared on the same arrival logic.\n\nThe prepared products. Post-processing derives from the record the views most analyses want and that need no replay of one's own:\n\nmasks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv: one row per snapshot instant (SimTime, one per mission_profile.csv row), columns Batch_<k> for every batch identifier up to the largest generated, values 0 = Future, 1 = Onboard, 2 = Link, 3 = Ground, 4 = Lost. A window anchored at snapshot r may use the batches with state 3 in row r, the live view of a streaming consumer at that instant.\nmasks/gaps.csv: the holes of the delivered record with their causes, as above; masks/batch_epochs.csv: the batch → epoch and payload-row map.\nmission_profile.csv: link capacity, on-board and link buffers, the cumulative ground counts, losses and retries at a change-driven cadence.\ndelivery_delay.csv: the measurement-to-ground delay of every generated batch, missing for the undelivered.\nalert_latency.csv and alert_latency_markers.csv: the completion of the look-back window after a live event, realized against the FIFO counterfactual, and at every marker.\nmarkers.csv and label_spans.csv: the event markers, and the labeled intervals of the scenario with the [[labels]] spans.\nproducts.h5: all of the above in one file (HDF5 Product Export).\n\nReproducibility. config_snapshot.toml (the parameters, the source identity, the platform) and manifest_snapshot.toml fix the code and the environment of a run; the seeded scenario fixes the loss draws. Two runs of the same scenario, mission span and seed on one platform write byte-identical event logs, mask timeline, and batch epochs, and metrics profiles that differ only in the wall-clock column, in either clock mode: pacing changes when a wall run writes its rows, not what they hold.\n\nStationary archetypes. A replay engine consumes a finished run through the same adapter as its streaming counterpart, in replay mode. A batch inference runs on the finished record with its holes declared; a training stage usually wants a gapless record, an inference stage tolerates a gapped one. A gap-aware statistic takes the series and the declared holes and classifies every quantity that spans a hole as exact, gap-crossing, or censored; the holes of the example above are its input.\n\nEnsembles. A stationary study over seeds or parameters starts from a sweep (scripts/run_sweep.jl, the Sweeps module): the same configuration on the tick clock once per seed, and the realized totals of all runs in one table with the sweep's own provenance (Ensembles and Sweeps in the usage page). Sweep runs skip the mask timeline, so they carry no masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv or masks/batch_epochs.csv.","title":"Stationary Analysis"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#HDF5-Product-Export","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"With post_processing.hdf5_export = true (or scripts/postprocessing/export_hdf5.jl [RUN_ID] afterward) the run's products are also written into products.h5, one self-describing file for HDF5 pipelines. The CSV products remain the primary interface; the file is a derived view of them, regenerable at any time.\n\nGroup Content\nroot attributes format_version (\"1\", unchanged at 3.0 when the masks/pointwise group of 2.x files was removed), run_id, start_sim_time, speed_up (NaN for a tick run), exported_at, the platform fingerprint of the run snapshot (hostname, package_version, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, julia_version, …), config_snapshot (the run's configuration as TOML text), and config_source (snapshot; files written by earlier versions may hold fallback, meaning the project config.toml stood in for a missing or corrupt snapshot)\nevents/tx, events/rx, holes the event logs and the declared-hole feed, one dataset per column\nmetrics/mission_profile the metrics profile, one dataset per column\nmasks/timeline states — the batch-state matrix laid out as states[snapshot, batch] for C-order readers (h5py, NumPy; Julia reads the transpose), batch_id, the snapshot instants (SimTime, SimTime_iso), and the attributes state_codes and layout\nmasks/batch_epochs the batch → epoch map, PayloadRow included\nmasks/gaps the gap table, every hole of the delivered record with its cause\nmetrology/alert_latency, metrology/alert_latency_markers, metrology/delivery_delay the metrology tables\nmarkers, label_spans, component_events the event markers, the labeled intervals of the scenario, and the component lifecycle record\n\nColumn conventions:\n\nCSV column HDF5 dataset Missing value\nDateTime Float64 seconds since start_sim_time (attribute unit), with an ISO-8601 twin <name>_iso written by string(::DateTime) NaN; \"\" in the twin\nboolean UInt8 0xff\ninteger Int64, or Float64 when a value is missing NaN\nother number Float64 NaN\nother string \"\"\n\nEach table group carries the attributes source (the CSV it was read from) and rows; masks/timeline carries source, state_codes, and layout.","title":"HDF5 Product Export"},{"category":"section","location":"interfaces/#Real-Time-Operation","page":"Analysis Interfaces","text":"speed_up = 1.0 is a supported configuration (validation requires only speed_up > 0): the mission clock then advances at wall-clock rate, one segment per segment_duration_sec of real time. A run executes in one process without checkpoint or resume, so a real-time rehearsal is planned as a bounded campaign, such as one session or one disruption window at 1×.","title":"Real-Time Operation"},{"category":"section","location":"upgrading/#Upgrading-and-Support","page":"Upgrading and Support","text":"","title":"Upgrading and Support"},{"category":"section","location":"upgrading/#Support","page":"Upgrading and Support","text":"Since 3.0 the package is in maintenance. Patch releases fix defects and keep the dependencies current; minor releases add to the surface below without changing what is there. The file contract, the configuration schema, the entry-point scripts and RunAdapter stay backward compatible within 3.x (Design Principles). Anything removed from that surface first warns through at least one minor release, as everything 3.0 removed did in 2.11. A writer that deviated from the contract as the contract recorded it is corrected in a minor release when the correction changes what a consumer reads from the runs of the shipped scenarios on the tick clock, and in a patch release when it leaves them unchanged; either way the change is listed below.\n\nThe supported Julia versions are 1.12 and the current stable release; the continuous integration runs the current release on Linux, macOS and Windows and 1.12 on Linux. Defects are reported as issues of the repository, with the run's descriptor.toml where a run is involved.","title":"Support"},{"category":"section","location":"upgrading/#From-3.2-to-3.3","page":"Upgrading and Support","text":"The simulator is now the package DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless (Headless Package and Containers). The main package re-exports its modules under the same names, so code that loads DeepSpaceTelemetry reaches them as before. What changes for a user of 3.2:\n\n3.2 3.3\nMetrology.plot_alert_latency, Metrology.plot_delivery_delay MetrologyFigures.plot_alert_latency, MetrologyFigures.plot_delivery_delay; the tables stay in Metrology\nthe HDF5 export loads with the package an extension of the headless package, loaded with HDF5; the main package loads it, and Export.hdf5_available() tells whether it is there\nevents_rx.csv: the SimTime of ingested, retry and lost is the start of the transfer the completion instant of the transfer; delivery delays and alert latencies grow by about one transfer time, more where a transfer spans the end of a pass\nalert_latency.csv: a row where a window completed under both doctrines a row wherever a live event's window lies within the run; on the added rows N_Alerts = 0 and the complete-case quantiles are NaN; the Kaplan–Meier columns follow the existing ones\nExport.HDF5, Export.ensure_group, Export.write_column!, Export.write_table!, Export.write_mask_timeline!, Export.write_provenance! moved into the extension DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadlessHDF5Ext; Export.export_hdf5 is the interface\nReceiver.ServicePacer, Receiver.serve_slot! removed\n\nThe configuration schema and RunAdapter are unchanged; a configuration of 3.2 runs as it is.","title":"From 3.2 to 3.3"},{"category":"section","location":"upgrading/#From-2.x-to-3.0","page":"Upgrading and Support","text":"When 3.0 rejects a configuration, the [CONFIG] error names the key and its remedy. The changes:\n\n2.x 3.0\npost_processing.expand_to_pointwise_masks, target_event_rows, masks/pointwise_mask_*.csv, the HDF5 group masks/pointwise/, scripts/postprocessing/apply_telemetry_mask.jl, standalone_mask_expander.jl, Masks.expand_pointwise_mask removed; RunAdapter.delivered_rows(run; at) returns the delivered payload rows at any mission instant as row intervals, exact across generation gaps, where the masks placed batches by position\nRunAdapter.GapTracker, declare!, open_gaps removed; RunAdapter.declared_holes reads holes.csv, and RunAdapter.FeedCursor(run, :holes) follows it by byte offset\nRunAdapter.MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION = 2.1.0 2.6.0: runs of earlier producers, without holes.csv and label_spans.csv, open only with open_run(run; minimum_version) lowered, and the readers no longer derive their missing parts; a batch without a payload_row stamp (producers before 2.1.0) is an error\nstorage.onboard_capacity_days telemetry.onboard_capacity_days; the snapshot of an earlier run is still read\nsupervision.watchdog_sec removed (no effect since 2.8.0)\nstorage.bytes_* removed; [storage] holds max_storage_gb, max_file_count and max_ram_gb\nkeys retired at 1.0.0 (simulation.test_duration_sec, simulation.max_storage_gb, physics.signal_injection_probability, post_processing.generate_batch_matrix, the [disaster] section) no longer recognized: validate_config warns that they are ignored\ndescriptor.toml schema = \"deepspacetelemetry-run/1\" \"deepspacetelemetry-run/2\": the point-wise masks are gone from the run directory\n\nThe shipped scenarios took this form in 2.11, so a configuration derived from one of them since 2.11 needs no change. The products of a run are otherwise those of 2.11.","title":"From 2.x to 3.0"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Usage-and-Configuration","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"","title":"Usage & Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#The-Configuration-File-(config.toml)","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"Mission parameters are set in a TOML file, and config.toml documents the safe interval of each key inline. validate_config enforces them at startup: code-breaking values abort with a precise message, suspicious ones warn. Type mismatches (quoted numbers, float counts) and malformed TOML abort with a [CONFIG] message rather than a stack trace.\n\nThe keys the sections below do not discuss, with the value that applies when a key is absent:\n\nKey Default Admissible\nsimulation.initial_downtime_days 0 ≥ 0 [d]\nsimulation.rng_seed 0 integer\nstorage.max_storage_gb, max_ram_gb 5, 8 > 0 [GiB]\nstorage.max_file_count 1 000 000 integer > 0\nretention.grace_hours, log_rotate_mb 24, 64 > 0 [h], [MiB]\nretention.high_watermark_gb 0.75 × max_storage_gb (0, max_storage_gb] [GiB]\ntelemetry.session_start, session_duration_hours required HH:MM:SS; (0, 24] [h]\ntelemetry.downlink_kbps, onboard_data_rate_kbps required without max_batches_per_hour > 0 [kbit/s], both or neither\ntelemetry.max_batches_per_hour required without the rate pair > 0; excludes the rate pair\ntelemetry.bandwidth_profile \"sine\" \"sine\", \"sigmoid\", \"gaussian\", \"flat\"\ntelemetry.sigmoid_steepness, gaussian_sigma 10, 0.15 > 0\ntelemetry.max_inflight_batches 5 integer ≥ 1\ntelemetry.min_link_factor 0.05 [0, 1)\ntelemetry.range_million_km 0 (no light-time delay) ≥ 0 [10⁶ km]\ncontacts.season_period_days 365.25 > 0 [d]\ncontacts.season_peak_day_of_year 172 [1, 366]\ncontacts.low_latency_capacity_fraction 1 (0, 1]\npacket_loss.enabled, model, p_loss false, \"bernoulli\", 0.05 boolean; \"bernoulli\", \"gilbert_elliott\"; [0, 1]\npost_processing.alert_latency, delivery_delay true boolean\npost_processing.alert_lookback_hours, delivery_requirement_hours 72, 24 > 0 [h]\ndashboard.open_live_viewer, open_receiver_log, open_emitter_log true boolean\nsupervision.on_component_failure \"abort\" \"abort\", \"continue\", \"restart\"\nsupervision.max_restarts 3 integer ≥ 0\n\nStorage is checked before the run: check_storage_limits prints a per-artifact estimate derived from the configuration (payload, metadata, event logs, metrics, masks, plots, logs, file count) and enforces the [storage] budgets. With [retention] disabled, a projected overrun aborts before any directory is created. With the retention custodian enabled, the run proceeds with a warning while the steady-state footprint fits the budget: the non-prunable classes plus the delivered payload capped at high_watermark_gb. The custodian prunes delivered ground/<batch>/seg_*.csv payload copies, oldest ingested first and each at least grace_hours of mission time after its ingestion, and records each pruning as a pruned event. Event logs, metrics, masks, snapshots, and lost/ are kept. Log files rotate at log_rotate_mb whether or not retention is enabled.","title":"The Configuration File (config.toml)"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Mission-Clock","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"simulation.mode selects the clock the mission runs on (Mission Clocks: Wall and Tick). \"wall\", the default, paces mission time against the wall clock and reads speed_up and mission_wall_seconds; \"tick\" advances mission time logically from one component wakeup to the next and reads mission_days instead. The two key sets are disjoint: a speed_up or mission_wall_seconds in tick mode, or a mission_days in wall mode, is a configuration error naming the mode, and tick mode also rejects dashboard.receiver_status_panel = true. scenarios/deterministic_1d.toml is the library's tick scenario, one mission day. Any wall scenario runs on the tick clock with its span unchanged through --tick on the command line (TelemetryCore.tick_configuration in a session); the figures of this manual are made that way.","title":"Mission Clock"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Scenario-Library","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"The repository ships a library of complete configurations under scenarios/, each runnable by path; scenarios/README.md carries the coverage matrix and the expected regime of every file. config.toml at the package root is the default entry point and is a copy of scenarios/recovery_12h_seasonal.toml. The TOML comments give each key's meaning, admissible choices, and safe interval; the scenario rationale is in the library README and below.\n\nThe reference scenario, recovery at the seasonal peak, spans 7 mission days from 30 March 2035 in about 3 min of wall time. It runs the physical link, 230 kbit/s downlink against 75 kbit/s production, flat within the pass (the Definition Study Report's sustained rate). The 4-hour seasonal extension, evaluated on the date of each pass, widens the daily 8-hour window to 12-hour passes: 30 March is the peak day, and the extension stays within minutes of its maximum over the week. The mission opens with a 2-day blind-spot backlog (288 batches at the shipped physics: 60 s segments, 10 segments per batch → 144 batches/day) served by 12-hour passes of about 210 batches each over a bursty Gilbert–Elliott channel (sticky BAD state, 50 % loss while BAD). Three scheduled events stress the mission: a 15-minute antenna repointing on day 1.5 (a generation gap), a day-2.5 full blackout after a solar particle event (18 h, then a 12 h linear recovery ramp with 5× elevated loss), and a day-5.0 partial DSN outage (severity 0.8, 12 h, 6 h ramp, 3× loss). An event marker at 14:00 on day 4 triggers, six hours later, a 3-hour low-latency period at half capacity.\n\nscenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml runs the same disruption timeline on plain 8-hour passes with the seasonal term disabled: the link delivers 138 batches per day against 144 produced, and the backlog grows. scenarios/nominal_8h.toml is the balanced case without backlog or disruptions, and scenarios/abstraction_gaussian_peak.toml the peak-capacity abstraction, a 60 batches/h peak under a Gaussian profile. The library also covers a drop policy, an explicit pass schedule, a 30-day seasonal mission reaching the recorder ceiling, the coarse-batch regime of 2400 s segments, external ingestion, and one mission day on the tick clock.\n\nUnder the physical rate pair and 8-hour passes only the flat profile sustains production. A day's capacity is the link rate times the pass length times the profile mean: flat 1.0, sigmoid with k = 10: 0.93, sine 0.5, Gaussian with σ = 0.15: 0.38. The shaped profiles are stress abstractions of a partially usable pass. validate_config warns when the rate pair meets a shaped profile, stating the profile mean and the capacity of one nominal pass against the daily production (TelemetryCore.capacity_balance), and the mission banner prints the same balance as its Capacity: line for every run.\n\nEvery scenario without a seasonal term starts on 1 January 2035, the calendar epoch of the ground-segment conventions, where the date labels the time axis and does not act on the passes. In the two seasonal scenarios the start date sets the phase of the extension, which is then evaluated on the calendar date of each pass: a mission spanning months sees its passes lengthen and shorten with the season (Contact Schedule and Low-Latency Periods).\n\nThe arguments are optional and order-independent. One ending in .toml selects the configuration (a relative path resolves against the current directory, then the package root), --tick runs it on the tick clock, and any other argument sets the run ID:\n\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl MY_RUN scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml\n\nEach run archives the configuration it used as config_snapshot.toml, and post-processing reads that snapshot.","title":"Scenario Library"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Packet-Loss-and-Disruptions","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"[packet_loss]\nenabled = true\nmodel = \"gilbert_elliott\"   # or \"bernoulli\" (then set p_loss)\np_good_to_bad = 0.02\np_bad_to_good = 0.30\np_loss_good = 0.005\np_loss_bad = 0.40\non_loss = \"retransmit\"      # or \"drop\" (= lose on first failure)\nmax_retries = 3\n\n[[disruption.events]]\ntype = \"link_disruption\"    # free string\nlabel = \"ground outage\"     # optional display name on the dashboard status line\nstart_day = 10.0            # mission days after start_sim_time\nduration_hours = 60.0       # full-blackout phase\nseverity = 1.0              # 1.0 = zero capacity; 0.4 = 60% capacity retained\nrecovery_hours = 12.0       # linear ramp back to nominal\nloss_multiplier = 8.0       # stochastic loss × 8 during blackout + ramp\n\n[[disruption.events]]\ntype = \"antenna_repointing\" # affects = \"generation\" by default for this type\nstart_day = 1.5\nduration_hours = 0.25       # no data produced for 15 min; severity/recovery/loss ignored\n\nThe on-board recorder is bounded by [telemetry] onboard_capacity_days (default 14): once the buffer holds that many days of production, new batches are discarded until room returns (no eviction), and the loss is recorded as a RECORDER gap in the transmit event log.\n\nsimulation.rng_seed seeds the packet-loss channel, the run's only random stream; the synthetic payload is declared by the event markers and involves no draw. The same seed gives the same record on either clock (Mission Clocks: Wall and Tick).","title":"Packet Loss & Disruptions"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Contact-Schedule","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"[contacts] shapes the daily window of [telemetry]; every key is optional.\n\n[contacts]\nseasonal_extension_hours = 4.0      # evaluated on each pass's date: 8 h at the trough, 12 h at the peak\nseason_peak_day_of_year = 89.0\nlow_latency_enabled = true          # set to false for the counterfactual run\n\n[[contacts.exceptions]]             # the window of one date, as given\ndate = \"2035-01-04\"\nduration_hours = 0.0                # 0 = missed pass; omit start to keep session_start\n\n[[contacts.low_latency_periods]]\nstart = \"2035-01-05T20:00:00\"\nduration_hours = 3.0\ncapacity_fraction = 0.5             # station availability as a fraction of peak\nlabel = \"transient follow-up\"\n\nA planned pass list replaces the daily generator (exceptions are then not admissible), either inline\n\n[[contacts.passes]]\nstart = \"2035-01-02T08:00:00\"\nduration_hours = 6.0\n\nor as schedule_csv = \"path/to/passes.csv\" with the columns Start, DurationHours (relative paths resolve against the current directory, then the package root). Passes may cross midnight and must not overlap.","title":"Contact Schedule"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Event-Markers","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"[[events.markers]] declare the instants the alert-latency metric is evaluated at; a marker may trigger a low-latency period after a reaction delay, and [ground] holds the processing budget added to every latency.\n\n[ground]\nprocessing_latency_hours = 1.0\n\n[[events.markers]]\ntime = \"2035-01-05T14:00:00\"\nlabel = \"transient candidate\"\nlow_latency_after_hours = 6.0       # optional: triggered period, 6 h after the marker\nlow_latency_duration_hours = 3.0\nlow_latency_capacity_fraction = 0.5","title":"Event Markers"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Label-Spans","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"[[labels]] entries declare labeled spans of payload rows, such as the samples of an injected event that a payload exporter records beside the series it writes. The run writes them to label_spans.csv with the other labeled intervals of the scenario (disruption phases, generation gaps, contact passes, low-latency periods, the initial downtime), where a consumer reads them. A [payload] section, the provenance a payload exporter records with its scenario fragment, is carried into the snapshot unchecked.\n\n[[labels]]\nlabel = \"mbhb_1\"\nlabel_start_index = 100   # payload rows, inclusive; start > end declares an empty span\nlabel_end_index = 2500","title":"Label Spans"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Synthetic-Payload","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"With data_source = \"synthetic\" the payload is a binary flag series: a segment is 1 throughout when its content span holds the instant of an [[events.markers]] entry and 0 throughout otherwise. segment_duration_sec must be a whole number of milliseconds and hold at least one sample at sample_rate.","title":"Synthetic Payload"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#External-Data-Ingestion","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"An external high-frequency CSV time series replaces the synthetic payload with:\n\n[physics]\ndata_source = \"external\"\nexternal_data_path = \"path/to/data.csv\"\nexternal_data_columns = []    # or [\"A\", \"E\"]: the file's columns to ingest, in order\nsample_rate = 1024.0\nsegment_duration_sec = 60.0\nbatch_size = 15\n\nRow r of the series is the sample at start_sim_time − initial_downtime_days · 86 400 s + (r − 1) / sample_rate: the first rows fill the pre-existing blind-spot backlog, a generation gap skips the rows of its interval, and past the last row the payload is zero. Each batch records the row of its first sample (payload_row, Batch Identity → Sample Interval).\n\nA file may carry several columns. external_data_columns names those to ingest, in that order; left empty, the Amplitude column is taken, or else the first column, under the name Amplitude. The ingested columns form one payload and share each batch, transfer and loss draw. Each segment file carries one column per payload column under its name. The names are stamped into the run's provenance and descriptor, and the adapter reads one column (read_batch) or all of them (read_batch_matrix). A listed column absent from the file's header is rejected at validation.","title":"External Data Ingestion"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Running-the-Simulation","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"The emitter and the receiver run as cooperative tasks on one thread in both modes, so Julia needs no thread option.\n\nInteractive Dashboard:\n\njulia scripts/launch_dashboard.jl\n\nThis spawns the log terminals and the UnicodePlots live viewer, which redraws only on a state change, for each [dashboard] flag open_* set to true; the shipped configurations set them false.\n\nHeadless Mode:\n\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl --tick MY_RUN scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml\n\nThe arguments follow the rule given under Scenario Library. The receiver's console status panel (a text panel redrawn on every receiver iteration, which clears the terminal) is off by default; dashboard.receiver_status_panel = true enables it for a headless run whose terminal shows nothing else.","title":"Running the Simulation"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Ensembles-and-Sweeps","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"A sweep runs one configuration on the tick clock once per seed, with optional overrides of any key, and gathers the realized totals of every run into one table. Any run of the table can be regenerated exactly from its seed and the sweep's provenance:\n\njulia scripts/run_sweep.jl scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml MY_SWEEP --seeds 1:8 --workers 4 --set packet_loss.p_loss_bad=0.6\n\n--seeds takes a range or a comma-separated list. --workers N runs N members at once as tasks on N Julia threads (serial without it); the script restarts itself with --threads=N when Julia was started with fewer. On the tick clock the runs are independent of the host's load and share no state, so parallel runs reproduce the serial ones exactly. Every --set section.key=value applies to all runs; the value is read as a boolean, an integer, a float, or else a string (quotes optional). The runs land under data/runs/<PREFIX>_seed<seed>/, and their post-processing writes the gap table and the metrology tables but no figures and no mask timeline. data/sweeps/<PREFIX>/ receives summary.csv, one row per run with the seed, the generated and delivered counts, live and archive, lost, failed transfers (column retries), the buffer and link at the end, the share within the delivery requirement, the alert-latency medians at 24 h and 72 h under the realized doctrine and the FIFO drain, and the hole count. Beside it, sweep.toml records the sweep's provenance: the base configuration's hash, the overrides, the seeds, the commit and version, the date. In a session, Sweeps.run_sweep(cfg; prefix, seeds, overrides, workers) returns the table and the directory, and Sweeps.summarize_run(run_dir) reads one run's totals.","title":"Ensembles and Sweeps"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Post-Processing-and-Masks","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"After a run, post-processing writes masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv, a 2D matrix of the state of every batch (0=Future, 1=Onboard, 2=Link, 3=Ground, 4=Lost) at every telemetry event. The reconstruction is exact: the emitter and receiver append every batch milestone to events_tx.csv / events_rx.csv (generation, transmission, ingest, retry, loss), and post-processing replays those ground-truth logs.\n\nA run whose config_snapshot.toml is missing or unparsable is refused with a [CONFIG] error.\n\nBefore any figure, post-processing writes the gap table masks/gaps.csv. It lists every hole of the delivered record as a payload-row interval with its cause: a lost or pruned batch, a batch undelivered at run end, a generation gap by type (stream, scheduled, recorder), or the unreached tail of an external record. A gap-aware analysis takes it as its declared input (Stationary Analysis), and Masks.gap_table(run_dir) computes it for a run made before the product existed. The declared-hole feed holes.csv is appended during the run as each hole is declared. Post-processing settles it from the gap table, appending the end-of-run holes with the nominal mission end as their declaration instant, after which the feed and the table agree. A row of the feed that the table does not derive is reported as a warning in the run log (Masks.hole_feed_excess); the table is the reference. The label-span table label_spans.csv is written at mission start and regenerated here only when a run lacks it. Last, post-processing writes descriptor.toml, the run's identity for a downstream collection: producer version and source identity (tree hash and commit, also for a copy installed by Pkg), configuration hash, seed, clock mode, payload geometry, and the size and SHA-256 of every table present.\n\nWith hdf5_export = true in [post_processing] every table product (event logs, hole feed, metrics, mask timeline, batch epochs, gap table, metrology tables, markers, label spans, component events) is additionally written to products.h5 with the run's provenance as attributes (HDF5 Product Export); the same file can be produced afterward for any run:\n\njulia scripts/postprocessing/export_hdf5.jl [RUN_ID]","title":"Post-Processing & Masks"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Publication-Figures","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"[post_processing.publication] re-renders the figures of a run (mission summary, session figures, alert latency, delivery delay, batch-state raster) at a declared printed width in a vector format, into one directory with a PROVENANCE.toml sidecar (run ID, package version, git commit, snapshot hash, file list), each file named <stem>__<run_id>.<format>:\n\n[post_processing.publication]\nenabled = true\nformat = \"pdf\"              # or \"svg\"\ncolumn_width_mm = 120.0     # printed width [mm], 100 to 400; default 178\nexport_dir = \"figures_export\"     # \"\" = <run_dir>/publication; relative to the current directory\n\nEvery figure is composed at one standard layout (1200 Makie units wide, 26-unit type) and the export scales it as a whole, so a narrower figure is a miniature of the standard one: at 178 mm the axis labels print at about 11 pt and the tick labels at about 9 pt, at the 100 mm floor at about 6 pt and 5 pt. Figure heights and legend rows follow from Makie's own layout measurements at the standard layout. In-axis annotations move to whichever end of the axis the disruption rules and event markers leave free, and the delivery-delay requirement rule stops above its annotation block. The same export runs afterward for any run with the settings of its snapshot:\n\njulia scripts/postprocessing/export_publication_figures.jl [RUN_ID]","title":"Publication Figures"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Session-Figures-of-Long-Missions","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"Each contact window, nominal pass or low-latency period, receives a session figure (session_day<kk>_detail.png, session_day<kk>_low_latency_detail.png), so their number grows with the mission: a year of daily passes is 365 figures, about a second and 0.6 MB each. Two keys bound the stage:\n\n[post_processing]\nsession_figures = true      # false: the mission summary only\nsession_figures_max = 0     # at most this many, evenly spaced over the contacts; 0 = no cap\n\nUnder a cap the windows are spread evenly over the mission, the first and the last included. The keys are read from the run's own snapshot, so the publication export and a later call of MissionFigures.generate_mission_plots select the same windows.\n\nThe stage holds one figure in memory at a time: each figure is drawn in a theme scope of its own and released after it is saved, so its memory does not grow with the number of windows. The start-up estimate prints it as Figure RAM and checks it against storage.max_ram_gb, beside the replay RAM of the mask timeline.\n\nAbove 120 mission days the capacity panel of the mission summary shows the mean capacity of each mission day, and its legend says so; passes drawn one by one would merge into a solid block. An 8-hour pass then reads as a third of the day, a missed pass as zero, and the seasonal extension as the slow rise and fall of that level. Count axes that reach 10⁴ batches carry tick labels with one common power of ten (10⁴, 2×10⁴, …).\n\nThe post-processing writes its products in order of cost: the gap table, the settled hole feed and the label spans, the metrology tables, then the alert-latency and delivery-delay figures, the mask timeline and the raster drawn from it, the mission summary and the session figures, the exports, and the run descriptor last. A run whose supervisor process was killed during that stage is completed with scripts/postprocessing/complete_run.jl (Availability Window & Lifecycle Sentinels).","title":"Session Figures of Long Missions"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#Batch-State-Raster","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"One further figure follows every run, behind its own flag:\n\n[post_processing]\nstate_raster = true         # the batch-state timeline as a raster\n\nstate_raster.png draws masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv with one column per batch, one row per recorded event, and one color per state, in the live and archive colors of every other figure. The Scenario Gallery shows how to read it. It needs generate_mask_timeline, and is skipped without it.","title":"Batch-State Raster"},{"category":"section","location":"usage/#GIF-Animation","page":"Usage & Configuration","text":"To visualize the LIFO/FIFO routing physics of a completed run:\n\njulia scripts/postprocessing/generate_gif.jl [--web] [RUN_ID]\n\nEach frame is one row of mission_profile.csv and states the mission clock, the link state, and the buffer counters. The rows follow a change-driven cadence, so the animation is not linear in mission time. Two rendering profiles exist: the default archive profile (1400 units wide, two raster units each, at most 800 frames) and --web, which writes the size the README and the manual carry (900 px, at most 240 frames, a few megabytes) as telemetry_animation_web.gif.","title":"GIF Animation"},{"category":"section","location":"api/channeleffects/#ChannelEffects","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"","title":"ChannelEffects"},{"category":"module","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"ChannelEffects\n\nDownlink channel models: stochastic packet loss (NoLoss, BernoulliLoss, two-state GilbertElliottLoss with its analytic stationary rate), scheduled disruption events (blackout, linear recovery ramp, elevated loss), and the composite LinkModel (visibility × disruption) gating both pipeline loops.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.LossModel","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"LossModel\n\nAbstract supertype for stochastic downlink packet-loss models. Concrete models implement sample_loss!, which is drawn once per batch transfer attempt on the receiver side. The random models carry their own RNG, a Xoshiro seeded by build_loss_model: the variates of the k-th attempt depend on the seed alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.LossModel"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.NoLoss","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"NoLoss()\n\nThe trivial loss model: every transfer attempt succeeds. Used when [packet_loss] enabled = false.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.NoLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.BernoulliLoss","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"BernoulliLoss(p, rng)\n\nMemoryless i.i.d. packet loss: each batch transfer attempt fails independently with probability p ∈ [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.BernoulliLoss"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.GilbertElliottLoss","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"GilbertElliottLoss(p_good_to_bad, p_bad_to_good, p_loss_good, p_loss_bad, in_bad_state, rng)\n\nTwo-state Markov (Gilbert–Elliott) bursty-channel model of correlated packet loss (Gilbert 1960, doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1960.tb03959.x; Elliott 1963, doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1963.tb00955.x). The channel alternates between a GOOD state with loss probability p_loss_good and a BAD state with loss probability p_loss_bad; state transitions occur once per transfer attempt with probabilities p_good_to_bad / p_bad_to_good. All four parameters must lie in [0, 1].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.GilbertElliottLoss"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.sample_loss!","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"sample_loss!(model::LossModel; multiplier::Float64=1.0) -> Bool\n\nDraws one loss realization for a single batch transfer attempt. Returns true if the transfer is lost. multiplier scales the instantaneous loss probability (clamped to [0, 1]) and is used to elevate loss rates during disruption events (see disruption_loss_multiplier). Mutates internal channel state for stateful models (Gilbert–Elliott).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.sample_loss!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.stationary_loss_rate","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"stationary_loss_rate(model::LossModel) -> Float64\n\nAnalytic long-run loss probability of the model (used for physics-style validation of the sampled stream). For Gilbert–Elliott this is π_bad·p_loss_bad + π_good·p_loss_good with the stationary state occupancies π_bad = p_good_to_bad / (p_good_to_bad + p_bad_to_good).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> ChannelEffects.stationary_loss_rate(ChannelEffects.BernoulliLoss(0.1, Xoshiro(1)))\n0.1\n\njulia> ge = ChannelEffects.GilbertElliottLoss(0.03, 0.25, 0.01, 0.5, false, Xoshiro(1));\n\njulia> round(ChannelEffects.stationary_loss_rate(ge); digits = 4)\n0.0625\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.stationary_loss_rate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.DisruptionEvent","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"DisruptionEvent\n\nA scheduled link-degrading event (a spacecraft safe mode after a solar particle event, a ground-station outage, …). Between start_time and blackout_end the link capacity is multiplied by 1 - severity (severity = 1 is a full blackout); between blackout_end and recovery_end capacity ramps linearly back to nominal. Throughout the whole event (blackout + recovery) the stochastic loss probability is scaled by loss_multiplier. label is an optional free-text display name shown on the dashboard while the event is active.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.DisruptionEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.DisruptionTimeline","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"DisruptionTimeline(events::Vector{DisruptionEvent})\n\nChronologically sorted collection of DisruptionEvents. Overlapping events compose conservatively: the minimum capacity factor and the maximum loss multiplier apply. DisruptionTimeline() builds the empty timeline of a disruption-free link.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.DisruptionTimeline"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.disruption_factor","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"disruption_factor(tl::DisruptionTimeline, t::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nMultiplicative link-capacity factor in [0, 1] at simulation time t: 1.0 outside all events, 1 - severity during a blackout, and a linear ramp from 1 - severity back to 1.0 during the recovery phase.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.disruption_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.disruption_loss_multiplier","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"disruption_loss_multiplier(tl::DisruptionTimeline, t::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nMultiplier (≥ 1) applied to the stochastic loss probability at time t. Active from event start through the end of the recovery ramp.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> cfg = Dict{String,Any}(\n           \"disruption\" => Dict{String,Any}(\n               \"events\" => [Dict{String,Any}(\n                   \"start_day\" => 2.0,\n                   \"duration_hours\" => 12.0,\n                   \"recovery_hours\" => 6.0,\n                   \"severity\" => 1.0,\n                   \"loss_multiplier\" => 4.0,\n               )],\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> tl = ChannelEffects.build_disruption_timeline(cfg, DateTime(2035, 1, 1));\n\njulia> ChannelEffects.disruption_loss_multiplier(tl, DateTime(2035, 1, 3, 6))\n4.0\n\njulia> ChannelEffects.disruption_loss_multiplier(tl, DateTime(2035, 1, 4))\n1.0\n\n\n\n\n\ndisruption_loss_multiplier(tl::DisruptionTimeline, segments::AbstractVector{Tuple{DateTime,DateTime}}) -> Float64\n\nThe largest loss multiplier (≥ 1) of the events active at some instant of the intervals [a, b) in segments: the multiplier of a transfer that transmitted during them (transfer_completion records them). An event confined to an interruption of the transfer, when no frame is sent, does not count.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.disruption_loss_multiplier"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.active_disruption_label","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"active_disruption_label(tl::DisruptionTimeline, t::DateTime) -> String\n\nThe label of the earliest-starting disruption event active at time t that carries a non-empty label, or \"\" when no active event is labeled. Used by the dashboard status line to name the ongoing event.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.active_disruption_label"},{"category":"type","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.LinkModel","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"LinkModel(visibility::TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel, disruptions::DisruptionTimeline)\n\nThe complete downlink channel: daily DSN visibility windows composed with the disruption timeline. LinkModel(vis) builds a disruption-free link.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.LinkModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.effective_bandwidth","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"effective_bandwidth(link::LinkModel, t::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nEffective link capacity in [0, 1] at simulation time t: the visibility bandwidth profile multiplied by the disruption capacity factor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.effective_bandwidth"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.is_transmittable","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"is_transmittable(link::LinkModel, t::DateTime) -> Bool\n\nWhether the satellite can place batches on the downlink at time t: geometrically visible and not inside a full blackout.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.is_transmittable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.transfer_completion","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"transfer_completion(link::LinkModel, t0::DateTime, work_sec::Real,\n                    horizon::DateTime; step_sec::Real = 60.0, segments = nothing)\n    -> Union{DateTime,Nothing}\n\nThe instant at which a transfer that starts at t0 and needs work_sec seconds at full capacity completes: the first τ with ∫_{t0}^{τ} c(t) dt = work_sec, c the effective capacity (effective_bandwidth), which is zero outside the contact windows. A transfer interrupted by the end of a contact or by a blackout resumes at the next contact. The integral is evaluated by the midpoint rule on sub-intervals of at most step_sec seconds, split at the window boundaries and at the breakpoints of the disruption timeline so that the capacity is smooth on each; within the sub-interval that completes the transfer the capacity is interpolated linearly between its ends and the integral solved for τ (linear_fill_time). Exact for a capacity that is piecewise linear between the breakpoints. Returns nothing when the transfer does not complete before horizon; the result is rounded to the millisecond. With a vector segments, the intervals during which the transfer transmitted (positive capacity) are appended to it, abutting ones joined, for disruption_loss_multiplier.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.transfer_completion"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.linear_fill_time","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"linear_fill_time(c0::Float64, c1::Float64, h::Float64, r::Float64) -> Float64\n\nThe time x ∈ (0, h] at which a capacity interpolated linearly from c0 at the start of a step of h seconds to c1 at its end has delivered r seconds of full-capacity transfer: c0·x + (c1 − c0)·x²/(2h) = r, solved as x = 2r / (c0 + √(c0² + 2r(c1 − c0)/h)), which stays accurate when c1 ≈ c0 and is exact for a linear capacity. h when the interpolant does not reach r within the step.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.linear_fill_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_loss_model","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"build_loss_model(cfg::AbstractDict, seed::Integer) -> LossModel\n\nConstructs the stochastic loss model declared in the [packet_loss] config section, with its own RNG seeded from seed. Returns NoLoss when the section is absent or enabled = false. An unknown model string or a probability outside [0, 1] raises the [CONFIG] error of TelemetryCore.loss_channel_settings (validated upstream by TelemetryCore.validate_config as well).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_loss_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_disruption_timeline","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"build_disruption_timeline(cfg::AbstractDict, start_sim::DateTime) -> DisruptionTimeline\n\nConstructs the link-disruption timeline from the validated TelemetryCore.disruption_event_settings; events with affects = \"generation\" belong to generation_gaps instead. Event start_day values are mission days relative to start_sim.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_disruption_timeline"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.generation_gaps","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"generation_gaps(cfg::AbstractDict, start_sim::DateTime) -> Vector{Tuple{DateTime,DateTime}}\n\nThe scheduled generation gaps — [[disruption.events]] with affects = \"generation\" — as (start, stop) intervals of duration_hours from start_day, sorted by start. The emitter produces no data inside them (Emitter.skip_generation_gaps!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.generation_gaps"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_link_model","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"build_link_model(cfg::AbstractDict) -> LinkModel\n\nConstructs the full LinkModel from a parsed configuration: visibility from TelemetryCore.visibility_model, disruptions from [disruption] anchored at simulation.start_sim_time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.build_link_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/channeleffects/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.loss_retry_limit","page":"ChannelEffects","text":"loss_retry_limit(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Int\n\nResolves the retry policy from [packet_loss]: on_loss = \"drop\" maps to 0 retries (immediate loss), \"retransmit\" (default) to max_retries (default 3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.ChannelEffects.loss_retry_limit"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"Overview","text":"One page per module:\n\nTelemetryCore\nChannelEffects\nVirtualInstrument\nEmitter\nReceiver\nMasks\nMissionFigures\nMetrology\nMetrologyFigures\nExport\nPublication\nSupervisor\nRunAdapter\nSweeps\nPlotTheme\n\nThe public names of RunAdapter are versioned with the run-directory contract (Analysis Interfaces); the pages of the other modules document the implementation. TelemetryCore and RunAdapter live in the reader package, which a consumer may add on its own:\n\nThe simulator without the figures is the headless package (Headless Package and Containers):","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#DeepSpaceTelemetry","page":"Overview","text":"DeepSpaceTelemetry\n\nSimulation framework for the telemetry environment of deep-space science missions: duty-cycled ground-station contact windows, a physical downlink with stochastic loss and scheduled disruptions, live-FIFO/archive-LIFO routing, and provenance-tracked data products. The shipped scenarios model the LISA mission; the telemetry, channel, and queuing layers are mission-agnostic. The simulator is the package DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless, whose modules are re-exported here under the same names; this package adds the figures and loads HDF5, which enables the HDF5 export. Submodules: TelemetryCore and RunAdapter (from the reader package DeepSpaceTelemetryReader), ChannelEffects, VirtualInstrument, Emitter, Receiver, Masks, Metrology, Export, Supervisor, Sweeps (from DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless), and PlotTheme, MissionFigures, MetrologyFigures, Publication.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader","page":"Overview","text":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader\n\nThe reader half of DeepSpaceTelemetry: TelemetryCore (configuration, run layout, batch and table I/O, provenance, the mission clock) and RunAdapter (typed access to a run directory), without the figure stack and the HDF5 export. A consumer that only reads run directories adds this package alone, by URL with subdir = \"reader\"; DeepSpaceTelemetry depends on it and re-exports both modules. The two packages are versioned together.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PACKAGE_ROOT","page":"Overview","text":"Absolute path of this package's root: the directory of config.toml, scenarios/ and data/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PACKAGE_ROOT"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless","page":"Overview","text":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless\n\nThe simulator of DeepSpaceTelemetry without its figures: the channel and instrument models, the emitter and the receiver on the tick driver, the post-processing tables, the mission supervisor and sweeps. Every package of its dependency closure carries a permissive licence (test/licences.toml), so it can be installed in a container or bundled in a system image without the terms of the figure stack. DeepSpaceTelemetry re-exports these modules and adds the figures; loaded alone, a mission writes every table and no figure. The HDF5 export is an extension, loaded with the HDF5 package. Submodules: TelemetryCore and RunAdapter (from the reader package), ChannelEffects, VirtualInstrument, Emitter, Receiver, Masks, Metrology, Export, Supervisor, Sweeps.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.PACKAGE_ROOT","page":"Overview","text":"Absolute path of this package's root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.PACKAGE_ROOT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/masks/#Masks","page":"Masks","text":"","title":"Masks"},{"category":"module","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks","page":"Masks","text":"Masks\n\nThe batch-state products of a run: the replay of every batch's state (future, onboard, link, ground, lost) from the event logs, the 2D mask timeline it writes, the batch epoch map, the gap table of the delivered record, and the label-span table of the scenario's labeled intervals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks"},{"category":"type","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.BatchStates","page":"Masks","text":"BatchStates\n\nAlias for the per-snapshot batch-location record of reconstruct_batch_states, read by the GIF animation: batch-ID vectors for every (stage × stream) bucket plus the terminal lost bucket.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.BatchStates"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.reconstruct_batch_states","page":"Masks","text":"reconstruct_batch_states(run_dir::String, df::DataFrame; rows = 1:nrow(df))\n\nExact replay of every batch's location from the ground-truth event logs: the gen/tx milestones of events_tx.csv (emitter) and the ingested/lost milestones of events_rx.csv (receiver); the state-preserving retry and pruned rows are skipped. Returns one BatchStates record per requested mission_profile.csv row, in the order of rows, evaluated at that row's SimTime; the identifiers of each bucket are sorted ascending (order within a bucket is not part of the contract). Each loss is attributed to its batch ID, which gives mask state 4 = Lost. Runs of producers before 2.8.0 can hold milestones whose timestamps invert within a batch; the replay enforces per-batch causal order (gen → tx → terminal) with later stages absorbing, keeping every batch's state sequence monotone. Events at the same instant are applied in causal order. The streaming form, which keeps no record per row, is replay_batch_states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.reconstruct_batch_states"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.replay_batch_states","page":"Masks","text":"replay_batch_states(f, run_dir::String, df::DataFrame) -> Int\n\nStreams the exact event-log replay over the metrics frame df. For every row i, once the events up to df.SimTime[i] are applied, f(i, codes, live) is called with codes::Vector{Int8}, the state of every batch identifier (0 future, 1 onboard, 2 link, 3 ground, 4 lost), and live::BitVector, whether the batch is live; both are indexed by batch identifier up to the largest identifier among the events the replay applies (those not later than the last SimTime of df), which is returned, and both are reused between calls, so f copies what it keeps. Events of unparsable batch names (identifier 0) are ignored. The event ordering and the per-batch causal rule are those described at reconstruct_batch_states.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.replay_batch_states"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.batch_states","page":"Masks","text":"batch_states(run_dir::String, df::DataFrame; rows = 1:nrow(df)) -> Vector{BatchStates}\n\nBatch-location history of a run: the exact event-log replay (reconstruct_batch_states) over the metrics frame df, for the metrics rows rows. Runs without events_tx.csv (pre-0.9 layouts) are not supported and raise an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.batch_states"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.generate_telemetry_masks","page":"Masks","text":"generate_telemetry_masks(run_dir::String)\n\nWrites the replay of the LIFO/FIFO transmission state machine as the 2D matrix telemetry_mask_timeline.csv: rows are time steps, columns the Batch_IDs, entries the batch's location (0=Future, 1=Onboard, 2=Link, 3=Ground, 4=Lost). The rows are streamed from replay_batch_states to disk, so the matrix is never held in memory. Also writes the batch-epoch map masks/batch_epochs.csv; returns without output, with a warning, when mission_profile.csv is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.generate_telemetry_masks"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.GAP_CAUSES","page":"Masks","text":"GAP_CAUSES\n\nThe values of the Cause column of gap_table: lost (retry budget exhausted), pruned (payload removed by the retention custodian), undelivered (generated, on board or on the link at run end), stream (emitter outage), scheduled (a scheduled generation gap), recorder (on-board recorder overflow), and not_generated (an external record not exhausted by run end).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.GAP_CAUSES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.gap_table","page":"Masks","text":"gap_table(run_dir::String) -> DataFrame\n\nEvery hole of the delivered record of a run, as declared intervals with their cause: columns Cause (GAP_CAUSES), Batch (the batch of a batch-level hole, missing for a generation gap or the unreached tail), RowFirst and RowLast (payload rows, inclusive), and ContentStart, ContentEnd (the content interval [ContentStart, ContentEnd)), sorted by RowFirst. Batch rows come from the payload_row stamp of the batch metadata; generation gaps from the gap_start / gap_end pairs of events_tx.csv, a gap left open at run end closed at the mission's end; the unreached tail from external_data_rows of the snapshot. Up to the last generated row (for an external record, up to external_data_rows), the rows of the table are the rows no delivered batch covers. On a finished run the declared-hole feed holes.csv, settled by settle_hole_feed!, holds the same holes with the instant each was declared at.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.gap_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.write_gap_table","page":"Masks","text":"write_gap_table(run_dir::String; table = gap_table(run_dir)) -> String\n\nWrites gap_table of a run to <run_dir>/masks/gaps.csv (rotated, never overwritten) and returns the path. table is the gap table when the caller has derived it already.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.write_gap_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.settle_hole_feed!","page":"Masks","text":"settle_hole_feed!(run_dir::String; table = gap_table(run_dir)) -> Int\n\nAppends to holes.csv, the declared-hole feed, every row of gap_table it does not yet hold, with the nominal mission end (TelemetryCore.mission_end) as DeclaredAt: the end-of-run causes a feed cannot announce during the run (undelivered, not_generated) and a recorder gap still open at the mission's end. On a finished run the feed then holds every hole of masks/gaps.csv, and hole_feed_excess lists any row it holds beyond them. Idempotent: a second call appends nothing. Returns the number of rows appended. table is the gap table when the caller has derived it already.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.settle_hole_feed!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.hole_feed_excess","page":"Masks","text":"hole_feed_excess(run_dir::String; table = gap_table(run_dir)) -> DataFrame\n\nThe rows of holes.csv beyond the holes of gap_table: every row whose (Cause, Batch, RowFirst, RowLast) the table does not derive, and every repeat of a hole the feed already holds, in feed order with the feed's columns. Empty on a sound finished run, where the settled feed and the table hold the same holes; the table is the reference. Post-processing reports a non-empty excess as a warning in the run log and alters neither file. table is the gap table when the caller has derived it already.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.hole_feed_excess"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.LABEL_SPAN_CLASSES","page":"Masks","text":"LABEL_SPAN_CLASSES\n\nThe values of the Class column of label_span_table: initial_downtime (the blind spot before the mission epoch), label (a [[labels]] span of the scenario), blackout and recovery (the two phases of a link disruption), generation_gap (a scheduled generation gap as configured), contact (a scheduled contact pass), and low_latency (a low-latency period, scheduled or triggered by a marker).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.LABEL_SPAN_CLASSES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.label_span_table","page":"Masks","text":"label_span_table(run_dir::String) -> DataFrame\n\nEvery labeled interval the scenario of a run declares, on the payload grid of gap_table: columns Class (LABEL_SPAN_CLASSES), Type, Label, RowFirst, RowLast (payload rows, inclusive), ContentStart, ContentEnd (the interval [ContentStart, ContentEnd)), sorted by RowFirst, RowLast, Class, Label. The table is derived from the run's configuration snapshot alone. Time intervals are taken as half-open and clipped to the mission record, rows 1 to the row before the mission end; [[labels]] spans keep their configured rows verbatim. Type is the disruption type for blackout, recovery, and generation_gap, pass for a contact, triggered or scheduled for a low-latency period, and empty otherwise; Label is the configured label. A generation gap is the configured span from start_day over duration_hours; the realized gap of masks/gaps.csv may differ from it by the snap of the emitter to the segment grid. Intervals that cover no payload row are omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.label_span_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/masks/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.write_label_spans","page":"Masks","text":"write_label_spans(run_dir::String) -> String\n\nWrites label_span_table of a run to <run_dir>/label_spans.csv (rotated, never overwritten) and returns the path. The file is written even when the table is empty, as a header without rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.write_label_spans"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrology/#Metrology","page":"Metrology","text":"","title":"Metrology"},{"category":"module","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology","page":"Metrology","text":"Metrology\n\nAnalysis metrics derived from a run's ground-truth event logs. The alert-latency curve gives, for a transient caught in a live batch, the time after the event at which the look-back window of length δ before it is on the ground, under the realized live-FIFO / archive-LIFO doctrine and under a counterfactual first-in, first-out drain of the same service completions. The delivery delay compares the measurement-to-ground delay of every batch with a delivery requirement (the Definition Study Report's 24 hours; Colpi et al. 2024, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2402.07571). See alert_latency_table and delivery_delay_table; the figures are drawn by DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology"},{"category":"type","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.BatchDelivery","page":"Metrology","text":"BatchDelivery\n\nOne batch in the delivery schedule: identity, content interval, generation (finalization) instant, realized ground availability (nothing when the batch never reached the ground), and the counterfactual FIFO availability (nothing when the FIFO drain never reaches it within the mission).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.BatchDelivery"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_schedule","page":"Metrology","text":"delivery_schedule(run_dir::String) -> Vector{BatchDelivery}\n\nPer-batch delivery bookkeeping from events_tx.csv, events_rx.csv, and the batch metadata, sorted by content epoch. The realized availability is the first ingested record of the batch. The counterfactual FIFO availability re-assigns the realized service completions (the sorted ingested instants) to the batches in content order: each completion goes to the oldest batch that had been generated by then and is still undelivered under FIFO, on the run's own link and slots without live priority. Batches without a recorded content epoch (pre-content_epoch metadata) take generation − batch span.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_schedule"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.batch_containing","page":"Metrology","text":"batch_containing(schedule, t::DateTime) -> Union{Nothing,BatchDelivery}\n\nThe batch whose content interval [content_epoch, content_end) contains t, or nothing (before the first batch, inside a generation gap, or after the last).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.batch_containing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.completion_curves","page":"Metrology","text":"completion_curves(schedule, alert_idx, t_m, steps, span, first_epoch) -> (real, fifo)\n\nWindow-completeness latencies of the alert schedule[alert_idx], with event instant t_m, on the look-back grid δ = 0, span, …, steps·span: for each δ, the instant (hours after t_m) at which every batch overlapping [t_m − δ, t_m) plus the alert batch itself has reached the ground, under the realized deliveries (real) and the counterfactual FIFO drain (fifo). Entries are nothing once the window contains a batch that never arrived under that doctrine (each curve independently); the vectors stop where the window would reach before first_epoch, and are empty when the alert batch reached the ground under neither doctrine.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.completion_curves"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.marker_latency_table","page":"Metrology","text":"marker_latency_table(run_dir::String; lookback_hours = 72.0) -> DataFrame\n\nWindow-completeness latency of every event marker of the run (markers.csv): Label, Marker (the instant), Batch (the batch whose content span holds it; empty when none does), Lookback_Hours, and LIFO_Hours / FIFO_Hours, completion_curves with the marker instant as t_m. Missing values mark an undelivered alert batch or a window containing a batch that never arrived; one row with Lookback_Hours = 0 and missing latencies records a marker outside every batch. Empty when the run has no markers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.marker_latency_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.alert_latency_table","page":"Metrology","text":"alert_latency_table(run_dir::String; lookback_hours = 72.0) -> DataFrame\n\nWindow-completeness latency curves over the look-back grid δ = 0, D, 2D, … (D = the batch content span) up to lookback_hours. A live batch defines an alert whose event instant t_m is the batch's content end (the moment the transient's samples exist). The look-back window [t_m − δ, t_m), together with the alert batch itself, is complete on the ground once every batch overlapping it has arrived; the latency is that completion instant minus t_m, for the realized doctrine and for the counterfactual FIFO drain (delivery_schedule). For δ ≤ D it is the delivery delay of the live batch itself, and it is non-decreasing in δ. At each δ an alert is excluded when its window reaches before the first recorded batch.\n\nTwo populations enter the table. The complete-case columns, N_Alerts and the median and 25 % / 75 % quantiles of the latency in hours for LIFO (realized) and FIFO (LIFO_Median_Hours, …, FIFO_Q75_Hours), are conditional on windows delivered in full under both doctrines: their alerts are the live batches delivered under both, and at each δ an alert whose window holds a batch that never arrived under one of the two is left out. The Kaplan–Meier columns use every alert, a live batch delivered under the realized doctrine (N_Alerts_All counts them at each δ). Under each doctrine a window that holds an undelivered batch is censored at the run end, T_end − t_m with T_end the mission end, and a window that holds data discarded at the on-board recorder (recorder_spans) never completes, nor, under the realized doctrine, one that holds a batch lost after its last retransmission (the counterfactual drain reassigns the transfers and may deliver it); N_Censored_LIFO and N_Censored_FIFO count both kinds, and LIFO_KM_Median_Hours, LIFO_KM_Q25_Hours, LIFO_KM_Q75_Hours and their FIFO counterparts are quantiles of the product-limit estimate (km_quantile). The estimator assumes censoring independent of the latency, which a backlog at the run end violates in part (late alerts are censored early), so the Kaplan–Meier median is an estimate, and \"not reached\" (NaN) is itself a result. One row per δ with N_Alerts_All > 0; on rows with N_Alerts = 0 the complete-case quantiles are NaN. Empty when the run has no alert.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.alert_latency_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_delay_table","page":"Metrology","text":"delivery_delay_table(run_dir::String) -> DataFrame\n\nMeasurement-to-ground delay of every generated batch: Batch, Live, ContentEnd, AvailableAt (missing when the batch never reached the ground), Delay_Hours = availability minus content end (missing when undelivered), LowLatency — whether the batch reached the ground inside a low-latency period of the run's contact model, also where the period overlaps a nominal pass — and Due: whether content_end + delivery_requirement_hours falls at or before the mission end, so that the requirement could be checked within the run. Built on delivery_schedule; rows sorted by content epoch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_delay_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_compliance","page":"Metrology","text":"delivery_compliance(table::DataFrame, requirement_hours::Float64; discarded::Int = 0) -> NamedTuple\n\nSummary of a delivery_delay_table against a delivery requirement: generated, delivered, within (delivered within requirement_hours of measurement), via_low_latency (delivered inside a low-latency period), fraction_within (of all generated batches — an undelivered batch is non-compliant), median_hours, and p95_hours of the delivered delays (NaN when nothing was delivered); due (batches with Due), pending (undelivered and not yet due), discarded (the keyword, the batches discarded at the on-board recorder, discarded_batches), and fraction_within_resolved (NaN when its denominator is 0). due, pending, and fraction_within_resolved are missing for a table without the Due column (an older run). fraction_within counts every generated batch, so a batch generated less than the requirement before the end of the run counts as non-compliant although it is not yet due. fraction_within_resolved counts the batches that were due or delivered by the end of the run and the discarded ones: within / (generated − pending + discarded).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.delivery_compliance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.km_quantile","page":"Metrology","text":"km_quantile(times::AbstractVector{<:Real}, observed::AbstractVector{Bool}, p::Real) -> Float64\n\nThe p-quantile of the Kaplan–Meier product-limit estimate of the distribution of times, where observed[i] is false for a time censored at times[i] (the true value exceeds it): the smallest observed time t with Ŝ(t) ≤ 1 − p, Ŝ the estimated survival function. At an equal time events precede censorings. NaN when Ŝ stays above 1 − p (the quantile is not reached within the follow-up) or when times is empty. Kaplan & Meier (1958), doi:10.1080/01621459.1958.10501452.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.km_quantile"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.recorder_spans","page":"Metrology","text":"recorder_spans(run_dir::String) -> Vector{Tuple{DateTime,DateTime}}\n\nThe content intervals [start, stop) the on-board recorder discarded at its ceiling, from the RECORDER gap rows of events_tx.csv; a span still open at the end of the log closes at the mission end. The parts of a span that a scheduled generation gap (SCHEDULED rows) covers held no data and are left out. A stream gap of an emitter outage is not: it starts at the content end of the last batch, which during an overflow is where the recorder began to discard.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.recorder_spans"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.discarded_batches","page":"Metrology","text":"discarded_batches(run_dir::String) -> Int\n\nThe number of whole batches the on-board recorder discarded at its ceiling: the length of the recorder_spans over the batch content span.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.discarded_batches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.write_metrology_tables","page":"Metrology","text":"write_metrology_tables(run_dir::String; lookback_hours::Float64 = 72.0) -> Nothing\n\nWrites the metrology tables of a run: alert_latency.csv and, when the run has event markers with an alert, alert_latency_markers.csv (alert_latency_table, marker_latency_table), and delivery_delay.csv (delivery_delay_table). A table that would be empty (no delivered live batch, no generated batch) is not written and is announced with a [POST] warning. The tables are run products independent of the figure flags; the metrology figures of the main package (MetrologyFigures) write none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.write_metrology_tables"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrology/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.quantile_sorted","page":"Metrology","text":"quantile_sorted(values::AbstractVector{<:Real}, p::Real) -> Float64\n\nQuantile p ∈ [0, 1] of an ascending-sorted vector with linear interpolation between order statistics (the default definition of Statistics.quantile). NaN for an empty vector.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Metrology.quantile_sorted"},{"category":"section","location":"api/missionfigures/#MissionFigures","page":"MissionFigures","text":"","title":"MissionFigures"},{"category":"module","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures","page":"MissionFigures","text":"MissionFigures\n\nThe figure products of a run: the mission summary, the per-contact session figures, and the batch-state raster, composed at the standard layout of PlotTheme from the metrics profile, the event logs, and the batch-state timeline of the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.generate_mission_plots","page":"MissionFigures","text":"generate_mission_plots(run_dir::String; style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> Vector{String}\n\nReads mission_profile.csv and renders the mission summary (plot_mission_summary) and one session figure per contact window (plot_session) into plots_dir (default <run_dir>/plots) in formats (default PNG + PDF) with the file-name suffix, under the telemetry theme of style (PlotTheme.PlotStyle); returns the paths written. Windows come from the contact model (nominal daily passes, scheduled or generated, and low-latency periods), not from the effective bandwidth, so a blacked-out day still gets its zero-throughput figure and file names follow the summary's 0-based days. Each figure is drawn in its own PlotTheme.figure_scope. post_processing.session_figures switches the session figures off and post_processing.session_figures_max caps them at that many windows, evenly spaced over the mission (evenly_spaced).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.generate_mission_plots"},{"category":"type","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.PlotContext","page":"MissionFigures","text":"PlotContext\n\nPer-run inputs shared by the mission summary and the session figures: the metrics frame with its elapsed-hour axis, the mission epoch, the visibility and link models, the disruption, component-outage, low-latency and event-marker spans in plot coordinates, and the loss-panel policy. Built once by plot_context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.PlotContext"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_context","page":"MissionFigures","text":"plot_context(run_dir::String, df::DataFrame, cfg::AbstractDict) -> PlotContext\n\nAssembles the PlotContext of a run from its metrics frame and its configuration snapshot. The time axis is anchored at start_sim_time, so day ticks and disruption shading sit on mission-day boundaries; a snapshot without a parsable start_sim_time falls back to the first metrics row. A malformed disruption section warns and yields an empty timeline, and the post-processing continues.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_context"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_mission_summary","page":"MissionFigures","text":"plot_mission_summary(ctx::PlotContext) -> String\n\nRenders the mission summary — capacity with the onboard buffer on a twin axis, cumulative received batches (total and archive share), and, when the loss channel was active, the Lost strip — to <run_dir>/plots/mission_summary_global.png with a vector PDF twin. Above SUMMARY_DAILY_MEAN_DAYS days the capacity is drawn as its mean over each mission day (daily_mean_capacity). Count axes that reach 10⁴ carry power-of-ten tick labels (PlotTheme.count_tickformat). Must run inside the telemetry theme. Returns the PNG path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_mission_summary"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.RASTER_PX_PER_UNIT","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Pixel density of the batch-state raster in a vector export, equal to the PNG twin's.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.RASTER_PX_PER_UNIT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.RASTER_STATES","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Cell states of the batch-state raster in legend order: the code drawn, the legend label, and the color. Codes 0–4 are those of masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv; plot_state_raster redraws the ground state of a blind-spot archive batch as code 5, so delivered batches carry the live and archive colors of every other figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.RASTER_STATES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.archive_batch_ids","page":"MissionFigures","text":"archive_batch_ids(run_dir::String) -> Set{Int}\n\nIDs of the batches generated as blind-spot archive, from the gen rows of events_tx.csv (TelemetryCore.is_archive_batch). Empty when the run carries no emitter log.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.archive_batch_ids"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_state_raster","page":"MissionFigures","text":"plot_state_raster(run_dir::String; style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRenders masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv as a raster, one column per batch, one row per recorded event, and one color per state, to <plots_dir>/state_raster.png with a vector twin, and returns its path; nothing when the timeline is absent, so a run that skipped DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Masks.generate_telemetry_masks is not an error. Delivered batches take the live or the archive color by the batch family recorded in events_tx.csv. Opens its own telemetry theme scope.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_state_raster"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.raster_figure","page":"MissionFigures","text":"raster_figure(states, hours, style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> String\n\nThe raster itself, once plot_state_raster has read the timeline: states is one row per recorded event and one column per batch, hours the mission hour of each row. states carries the display codes of RASTER_STATES; the legend lists the states present. Must run inside the telemetry theme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.raster_figure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.session_figure_stems","page":"MissionFigures","text":"session_figure_stems(model, t_start::DateTime, t_end::DateTime) -> Vector{Tuple{String,ContactWindow}}\n\nFile-name stems of the session figures of every contact window of model overlapping [t_start, t_end] (TelemetryCore.contact_windows): day<kk> from the 0-based mission day of the window start, _low_latency appended for low-latency periods, and a letter suffix (b, c, …) when several windows of the same kind start on the same day.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.session_figure_stems"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.evenly_spaced","page":"MissionFigures","text":"evenly_spaced(n::Integer, cap::Integer) -> Vector{Int}\n\nIndices of at most cap of n items, evenly spaced with the first and the last included; all of them when cap is zero (no cap) or not below n. Throws an ArgumentError for a negative cap.\n\njulia> DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.evenly_spaced(10, 4)\n4-element Vector{Int64}:\n  1\n  4\n  7\n 10\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.evenly_spaced"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.session_figure_selection","page":"MissionFigures","text":"session_figure_selection(cfg::AbstractDict) -> (enabled::Bool, cap::Int)\n\nThe post_processing.session_figures flag and the post_processing.session_figures_max cap of a run configuration (TelemetryCore.post_processing_settings). A snapshot whose [post_processing] section cannot be read, as that of a run made by an earlier version may be, warns and yields every session figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.session_figure_selection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_session","page":"MissionFigures","text":"plot_session(ctx::PlotContext, window::TelemetryCore.ContactWindow, stem::String; style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRenders the session figure of one contact window — a nominal pass or a low-latency period: smooth nominal and effective capacity with the onboard buffer on a twin axis, and the batches received within the window (total and archive share) with ✕ pins and a count badge for any losses — to <run_dir>/plots/session_<stem>_detail.png with a vector PDF twin (session_figure_stems names the stems). Returns nothing when the window lies outside the recorded span or holds fewer than two metrics rows. Must run inside the telemetry theme.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.plot_session"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.hours_since","page":"MissionFigures","text":"hours_since(t::DateTime, t0::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nElapsed mission hours from t0 to t, the time coordinate of the figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.hours_since"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.component_outage_spans","page":"MissionFigures","text":"component_outage_spans(run_dir, t_start, x_end) -> Vector{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\nComponent-outage windows from the supervisor's component_events.csv: each down opens a window closed by the next restart of the same component, or by the mission end x_end [h]. Empty when the record is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.component_outage_spans"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SPAN_COALESCE_FRACTION","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Fraction of the plotted time range below which two recorder-full spans are drawn as one band (coalesce_spans).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SPAN_COALESCE_FRACTION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SPAN_MIN_VISIBLE_FRACTION","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Fraction of the plotted time range below which a shaded span is widened about its center to that width (shade_spans!): a 15-minute generation gap on a seven-day axis would otherwise collapse to a line, unlike the band its legend entry shows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SPAN_MIN_VISIBLE_FRACTION"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.coalesce_spans","page":"MissionFigures","text":"coalesce_spans(spans, min_gap::Real) -> Vector{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\nspans ((start, stop) pairs in hours, any order) sorted by start and merged wherever the interval from one span's stop to the next span's start is below min_gap. A recorder toggling at capacity on a weak link opens a gap per transmitted batch; at mission scale the boundary lines of those gaps would tile the panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.coalesce_spans"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.generation_gap_spans","page":"MissionFigures","text":"generation_gap_spans(run_dir, t_start, x_end, tag) -> Vector{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\nGeneration-gap windows from the emitter's events_tx.csv: gap_start / gap_end pairs whose Batch column equals tag (SCHEDULED for planned gaps, RECORDER for recorder overflows, STREAM for outages recorded by the supervisor), in hours since t_start; an unclosed gap ends at x_end.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.generation_gap_spans"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.low_latency_spans","page":"MissionFigures","text":"low_latency_spans(model::TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel, t_start, x_end) -> Vector{NTuple{2,Float64}}\n\nLow-latency periods of the run, scheduled ones and those triggered by an event marker, as (start, stop) pairs in hours since t_start for the periods overlapping [0, x_end], unclipped (the shading clamps them). Empty when contacts.low_latency_enabled is unset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.low_latency_spans"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.marker_times","page":"MissionFigures","text":"marker_times(run_dir::String, t_start::DateTime) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nDeclared event markers of the run, in hours since t_start, from its markers.csv (TelemetryCore.load_markers). Empty when the run declared none or predates the marker record.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.marker_times"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.spans_overlap","page":"MissionFigures","text":"spans_overlap(spans, x_lo, x_hi, lo, hi) -> Bool\n\ntrue when any span's [s[lo], s[hi]] phase intersects the plotted window [x_lo, x_hi]. Legends must only advertise what their own figure draws, so blackout (lo = 1, hi = 2) and recovery-ramp (lo = 2, hi = 3) phases are gated independently.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.spans_overlap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_disruptions!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"shade_disruptions!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, disruption_spans; style)\n\nShades every disruption event onto ax, clamped to the plotted range: a uniform wash over the blackout, fading linearly to zero alpha across the recovery ramp as the capacity does, and dashed same-hue lines at the guide line width of style at event start and full recovery. The shading lies behind the data on every panel but above z = -100, where the white background of a twin axis would cover it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_disruptions!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shading_patch","page":"MissionFigures","text":"shading_patch(fill, edge, linestyle::Symbol, style::PlotTheme.PlotStyle) -> Vector\n\nLegend entry of a shaded event window: the fill patch under a line in the color and line style of the window's edge lines, at the guide line width of style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shading_patch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_spans!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"shade_spans!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, spans; color, edgecolor, linestyle, style)\n\nShades (start, stop) spans onto ax, clamped to the plotted range: a wash of color with same-hue edge lines (edgecolor, linestyle) at the guide line width of style, pushed behind the data. A span narrower than SPAN_MIN_VISIBLE_FRACTION of the plotted range is widened about its center to that width so it remains a visible band. The defaults are the component-outage styling (PlotTheme.COLOR_OUTAGE, dotted edges); the generation-gap, recorder-full, and low-latency washes pass their own.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_spans!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_generation_gaps!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"shade_generation_gaps!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, ctx::PlotContext; style)\n\nScheduled generation gaps (onboard-family color, dash-dot edges) and recorder overflows (loss color, dash-dot edges) behind the data of ax, edge lines at the guide line width of style. Recorder overflows closer than SPAN_COALESCE_FRACTION of the plotted range are drawn as one band (coalesce_spans).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_generation_gaps!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_low_latency!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"shade_low_latency!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, ctx::PlotContext; style)\n\nLow-latency periods behind the data of ax: a low-alpha wash in the capacity color with dash-dot-dot edges of the same hue. A period is a contact at a fraction of peak capacity, so the wash sits under the capacity curve.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.shade_low_latency!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.mark_events!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"mark_events!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, times; style)\n\nDraws one upright rule per declared event marker within the plotted range onto ax, in PlotTheme.COLOR_MARKER at the guide line width of style, behind the data. The rule is solid, a style no shaded window uses for its edges, so a marker does not read as an event boundary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.mark_events!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.decorate_axis!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"decorate_axis!(ax, x_lo, x_hi, ctx::PlotContext; style, low_latency::Bool)\n\nThe event layers of a time axis over [x_lo, x_hi], in drawing order: the disruptions (shade_disruptions!), the component outages (shade_spans!), the generation gaps (shade_generation_gaps!), with low_latency the low-latency periods (shade_low_latency!), and the event markers (mark_events!).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.decorate_axis!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.figure_legend_entries","page":"MissionFigures","text":"figure_legend_entries(; degraded, blackout, ramp, lost, outage = false, …) -> Vector{Tuple{String,Vector{Any},Vector{String}}}\n\nThe Link, Received, and Events families of a figure's legend, each a (title, elements, labels) tuple for PlotTheme.figure_legend!, holding only what the figure draws. degraded swaps the capacity entry for the nominal/effective pair; blackout, ramp, outage, scheduled_gap, recorder, low_latency, and marker gate their entries; daily_mean marks the capacity labels as daily means; lost is :strip (summary stairs and marks), :marks (session ✕ pins), or :none. Band+stair series get composite fill+edge patches, and labels name the series only (Total (live + archive) under Received).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.figure_legend_entries"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.add_figure_legend!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"add_figure_legend!(fig; degraded, blackout, ramp, lost, outage = false, …) -> fig\n\nOne frameless horizontal legend strip above the panels of fig, carrying the entry families figure_legend_entries builds from the same keywords, placed and fitted to the figure width by PlotTheme.figure_legend!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.add_figure_legend!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.upright_rules","page":"MissionFigures","text":"upright_rules(ctx::PlotContext) -> Vector{Float64}\n\nEvery x position at which a figure of ctx draws an upright rule: the boundaries of the disruption, outage, generation-gap and low-latency windows, and the event markers. In-axis annotations are placed against this list (PlotTheme.annotation_side, PlotTheme.annotation_anchor) to keep the rules clear of the text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.upright_rules"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.TICK_LABEL_SPACE","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Width reserved for the y tick labels of every stacked panel at the standard layout [Makie units], four digits at the tick size, so the y-labels of stacked panels align.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.TICK_LABEL_SPACE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.summary_tick_step_hours","page":"MissionFigures","text":"summary_tick_step_hours(total_days) -> Float64\n\nDay-tick spacing of the mission summary [h]: the smallest step of 1, 2, 5, 10, 20, 30, or 60 days that places at most eleven Day n labels on the axis (the labels touch beyond that at the design width), 120 days beyond.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.summary_tick_step_hours"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.mission_time_ticks","page":"MissionFigures","text":"mission_time_ticks(span_hours::Float64) -> (values, labels, axis_label)\n\nTick positions [h], tick labels, and axis label of a mission-time axis spanning span_hours: Day n labels at the spacing of summary_tick_step_hours from two days on; below that, where a day axis would carry one or two labels, whole hours at a step of 6 h (3 h up to one day, 1 h up to 8 h) with the unit in the axis label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.mission_time_ticks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.count_tick_step","page":"MissionFigures","text":"count_tick_step(y_top::Real) -> Int\n\nTick step of a batch-count axis reaching y_top: the smallest of 1, 2 and 5 times a power of ten that fits at most three steps below y_top, so the labels read as counts (0, 50, 100 rather than 0, 36, 72).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.count_tick_step"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SUMMARY_CAPACITY_SAMPLES","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Number of equidistant instants on which the mission summary evaluates the capacity curves (capacity_curves): about one per raster column of the standard PNG, so a contact edge is drawn within a pixel of its instant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SUMMARY_CAPACITY_SAMPLES"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SESSION_CAPACITY_SAMPLES","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Number of equidistant instants on which a session figure evaluates the capacity curves (capacity_curves).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SESSION_CAPACITY_SAMPLES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.capacity_curves","page":"MissionFigures","text":"capacity_curves(vis_model, link_model, t_start, x_lo, x_hi, samples) -> NamedTuple\n\nNominal and effective link capacity [%] on samples equidistant instants spanning [x_lo, x_hi] hours since t_start, as (hours, nominal, effective): the visibility model alone (TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor) and the link model with its disruptions (ChannelEffects.effective_bandwidth). The Bandwidth_Pct column of the metrics profile is written on change only; joined row to row, it would slope across intervals without a row, such as the start-up before the first pass or a contact gap with the recorder full. Throws an ArgumentError for fewer than two samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.capacity_curves"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SUMMARY_DAILY_MEAN_DAYS","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Mission span [days] above which the mission summary draws the capacity as its mean over each mission day (daily_mean_capacity). At the standard layout a day is then narrower than three line widths, and the passes drawn one by one merge into a solid block.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SUMMARY_DAILY_MEAN_DAYS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.DAILY_MEAN_SAMPLES","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Instants per mission day on which daily_mean_capacity evaluates the capacity: one per minute, so that a pass whose length drifts by minutes from day to day gives a mean that drifts with it instead of jumping by quarter hours.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.DAILY_MEAN_SAMPLES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.daily_mean_capacity","page":"MissionFigures","text":"daily_mean_capacity(vis_model, link_model, t_start, x_hi) -> NamedTuple\n\nNominal and effective link capacity [%] averaged over each mission day of [0, x_hi] hours since t_start, as (hours, nominal, effective) for a staircase with step = :post: hours are the day boundaries 0, 24, … closed by x_hi, and the value at a boundary is the mean over the day that begins there (the last value repeats the one before it). A day's mean times 24 h is the contact time of that day at full capacity, which sets the daily balance against production.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.daily_mean_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.step_vertices","page":"MissionFigures","text":"step_vertices(x, y) -> Tuple{Vector{Float64},Vector{Float64}}\n\nVertices of the staircase stairs! draws through (x, y) with step = :pre, each value holding on the interval that ends at its abscissa: (x₁, y₁), (x₁, y₂), (x₂, y₂), (x₂, y₃), …, (xₙ, yₙ). A metrics row written at an ingestion carries the count before it, so this is the convention under which the cumulative counts step at the ingestion instants. Throws a DimensionMismatch when the lengths differ.\n\njulia> DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.step_vertices([0.0, 1.0, 3.0], [5.0, 6.0, 8.0])\n([0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 1.0, 3.0], [5.0, 6.0, 6.0, 8.0, 8.0])\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.step_vertices"},{"category":"function","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.filled_stairs!","page":"MissionFigures","text":"filled_stairs!(ax, x, y; color, style)\n\nDraws the cumulative count y over x onto ax as a staircase (step = :pre) at the edge line width of style over an area fill of the same hue at PlotTheme.FILL_ALPHA. The fill is bounded by the vertices of the staircase (step_vertices), not by the straight segments between the samples, so the edge is the boundary of the area at every sampling density.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.filled_stairs!"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/missionfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SESSION_PIN_HEIGHT","page":"MissionFigures","text":"Relative height of the lost-batch pins on the received panel of the session figure: below the top row, which the loss count and the low-latency note occupy, and above the data, whose maximum sits at 1/1.2 of the axis.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MissionFigures.SESSION_PIN_HEIGHT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/runadapter/#RunAdapter","page":"RunAdapter","text":"","title":"RunAdapter"},{"category":"section","location":"api/runadapter/#Public-surface","page":"RunAdapter","text":"","title":"Public surface"},{"category":"section","location":"api/runadapter/#Internals","page":"RunAdapter","text":"","title":"Internals"},{"category":"module","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter","page":"RunAdapter","text":"RunAdapter\n\nRun adapter for consumers. It reads the geometry, batches, payload, arrivals, state, labeled intervals and identity of a run directory from the file contract alone, for an active run as for a finished one. open_run checks the producer's version against MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION; declared_holes reads the holes of the delivered record from the producer's feed, holes.csv, and delivered_rows the rows on the ground at any instant. The contract is the Analysis Interfaces chapter of the manual; a consumer in another language reads the same files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION","page":"RunAdapter","text":"MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION\n\nThe oldest producer whose runs the adapter opens by default: 2.6.0, the first to write the declared-hole feed holes.csv and label_spans.csv beside the payload_row stamps of the batch metadata (2.1.0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.RunGeometry","page":"RunAdapter","text":"RunGeometry\n\nThe geometry of a run as a consumer needs it: sample_rate [Hz], segment_duration_sec, batch_size (segments per batch), points_per_batch, the mission epoch start_sim_time, the payload_origin (the instant of payload row 1), payload_rows (the length of an external record, nothing for a synthetic payload), the producer's package_version, the clock mode (\"wall\" or \"tick\"), and the names columns of the payload columns, from provenance.external_data_columns of the configuration snapshot when present, else the one column TelemetryCore.PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.RunGeometry"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.BatchRecord","page":"RunAdapter","text":"BatchRecord\n\nOne batch of the run: name (LIVE_batch_<k> or ARCH_batch_<k>), its global index k, whether it is live, the payload rows it covers, its content_epoch (the mission instant of its first sample, missing for a batch written before the key existed), and its state — :ground (payload readable), :pruned (delivered, payload removed by the retention custodian), or :lost (retry budget exhausted, payload in lost/).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.BatchRecord"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.ArrivalEvent","page":"RunAdapter","text":"ArrivalEvent\n\nOne row of events_rx.csv: the mission instant sim_time, the batch, the event (:ingested, :retry, :lost, :pruned), and the attempt count the row carries.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.ArrivalEvent"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.RunHandle","page":"RunAdapter","text":"RunHandle\n\nAn opened run: its directory, its configuration snapshot, and its RunGeometry. Made by open_run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.RunHandle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.open_run","page":"RunAdapter","text":"open_run(run::AbstractString; minimum_version = MINIMUM_PRODUCER_VERSION) -> RunHandle\n\nOpens a run by directory path or by run identifier (resolved under the data root). Throws an ArgumentError when the directory is not a run of this package (no config_snapshot.toml, no lifecycle sentinel), or when the producer's recorded version is older than minimum_version; a run that records no version is opened with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.open_run"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_geometry","page":"RunAdapter","text":"run_geometry(run::RunHandle) -> RunGeometry\n\nThe run's RunGeometry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_geometry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_state","page":"RunAdapter","text":"run_state(run::RunHandle) -> Symbol\n\nThe lifecycle state, from the sentinels: :complete, :aborted, or :active (the pipeline may still write).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_state"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.record_final","page":"RunAdapter","text":"record_final(run::RunHandle) -> Bool\n\nWhether the payload, the event logs, and the metrics profile are final: the run has ended, or RUN_POSTPROCESSING stands beside RUN_ACTIVE and only derived products are still being written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.record_final"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_descriptor","page":"RunAdapter","text":"run_descriptor(run::RunHandle) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe run's identity from descriptor.toml, as TelemetryCore.write_run_descriptor writes it: schema, run_id, the producer's package_version and source identity (git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty, …), config_sha256, the clock mode, the payload geometry, payload_columns, and under \"products\" the bytes and sha256 of every table present. Empty while the file is absent. Post-processing writes it last, so it is final once the run has ended (run_state not :active); a run that ended before post-processing has none. The producer renames the file into place, so a read during post-processing finds the whole descriptor or none. Throws an ArgumentError when the file declares no schema or one other than \"deepspacetelemetry-run/1\" (producers 2.5.0 to 2.11.0) and \"deepspacetelemetry-run/2\" (3.0.0 and later).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.run_descriptor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.row_time","page":"RunAdapter","text":"row_time(geometry::RunGeometry, row::Integer) -> DateTime\n\nThe mission instant of payload row row: payload_origin + (row − 1) / sample_rate, on the millisecond grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.row_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.time_row","page":"RunAdapter","text":"time_row(geometry::RunGeometry, t::DateTime) -> Int\n\nThe payload row at the mission instant t, the inverse of row_time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.time_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.batch_rows","page":"RunAdapter","text":"batch_rows(run::RunHandle, stage::AbstractString, name::AbstractString) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nThe payload rows of the batch name under <run_dir>/<stage>/, from the payload_row stamp of its metadata. stage is one of the stage directories \"onboard\", \"link\", \"ground\", \"lost\". Throws an ArgumentError for another stage, for a name that is not a batch name, and for a batch absent from that stage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.batch_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.list_batches","page":"RunAdapter","text":"list_batches(run::RunHandle) -> Vector{BatchRecord}\n\nEvery batch under ground/ and lost/, sorted by index, with its rows, content epoch, and state (a delivered batch whose payload the custodian removed is :pruned).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.list_batches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.batch_record","page":"RunAdapter","text":"batch_record(run::RunHandle, name::AbstractString) -> Union{BatchRecord,Nothing}\n\nThe BatchRecord of the batch name, by the record and state rule of list_batches (:ground, :pruned, :lost), from the metadata of that batch alone; nothing when it is in neither ground/ nor lost/ (not yet finalized, or still on the link). It reads one metadata.json and needs no stage directory. Throws an ArgumentError for a name that is not a batch name (LIVE_batch_<k> or ARCH_batch_<k>).\n\narrivals = RunAdapter.FeedCursor(run, :arrivals)\nfor e in RunAdapter.arrival_events(arrivals)\n    e.event === :ingested || continue\n    record = RunAdapter.batch_record(run, e.batch)\n    record.state === :ground || continue   # pruned since its arrival\n    samples = RunAdapter.read_batch_matrix(run, e.batch; delivered = true)\n    # consume samples at record.rows\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.batch_record"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.read_batch","page":"RunAdapter","text":"read_batch(run::RunHandle, name::AbstractString;\n           column::Union{Integer,AbstractString} = 1,\n           delivered::Bool = false) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nColumn column (an index or a name) of read_batch_matrix, which receives delivered; for a one-column run the default is the whole payload. Throws the errors of read_batch_matrix, and an ArgumentError for a column that is not one of run.geometry.columns.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.read_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.read_batch_matrix","page":"RunAdapter","text":"read_batch_matrix(run::RunHandle, name::AbstractString;\n                  delivered::Bool = false) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nThe samples of one batch, one row per sample and one column per payload column of run.geometry.columns, its segments concatenated in ascending segment identifier, from ground/ or, for a lost batch, from lost/. With delivered = true only a batch on the ground is read. Throws an ArgumentError for a batch that is absent or pruned (or, with delivered = true, lost), for a segment whose columns differ from those of the geometry, and for a batch whose segments do not hold points_per_batch samples.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.read_batch_matrix"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.arrival_events","page":"RunAdapter","text":"arrival_events(run::RunHandle; after::Int = 0) -> Vector{ArrivalEvent}\n\nThe rows of events_rx.csv in file order, which is mission-time order, beyond the first after rows. The whole file is parsed, a last line still being written included; a streaming consumer follows the feed with a FeedCursor. A batch's payload is in ground/ before its ingested row is appended.\n\n\n\n\n\narrival_events(cursor::FeedCursor) -> Vector{ArrivalEvent}\n\nThe rows of events_rx.csv appended since the previous call on an :arrivals cursor (FeedCursor), advancing it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.arrival_events"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.delivered_rows","page":"RunAdapter","text":"delivered_rows(run::RunHandle; at::Union{DateTime,Nothing} = nothing)\n    -> Vector{UnitRange{Int}}\n\nThe payload rows on the ground at mission instant at: the rows of every batch with an ingested row in events_rx.csv at or before at (every one when at is nothing), merged into sorted, disjoint intervals. The rows come from the batches' payload_row stamps (batch_rows), so they are exact across generation gaps and emitter restarts. A pruned batch counts as delivered, as its delivery happened; list_batches tells which payloads are still readable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.delivered_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.markers","page":"RunAdapter","text":"markers(run::RunHandle) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe event markers of the run, (label, time, row), from markers.csv. Empty when the run declares none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.markers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.label_spans","page":"RunAdapter","text":"label_spans(run::RunHandle) -> Vector{NamedTuple}\n\nThe labeled intervals of the run's scenario from label_spans.csv, (class, type, label, rows, content_start, content_end) with rows a UnitRange{Int} of payload rows: the scenario's [[labels]] spans, the blackout and recovery phases of the link disruptions, the scheduled generation gaps as configured, the contact passes and low-latency periods, and the initial downtime. Empty when the producer wrote no table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.label_spans"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.Hole","page":"RunAdapter","text":"Hole\n\nA declared hole of the delivered record: its cause (:lost, :pruned, :stream, :scheduled, :recorder, and in the settled feed also :undelivered, :not_generated), the batch for a batch-level hole (missing for a generation gap or the unreached tail), its payload rows, the content interval [content_start, content_end), and declared_at, the mission instant the producer declared the hole at. Hole(cause, batch, rows, content_start, content_end) builds a hole without a declaration instant (missing).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.Hole"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.declared_holes","page":"RunAdapter","text":"declared_holes(run::RunHandle; after::Integer = 0) -> Vector{Hole}\n\nThe declared-hole feed holes.csv beyond its first after rows, each row a Hole with its declared_at, appended as the producer declares it; on a finished run the feed holds the holes of masks/gaps.csv. Empty while no hole has been declared (the file appears with the first).\n\n\n\n\n\ndeclared_holes(cursor::FeedCursor) -> Vector{Hole}\n\nThe rows of holes.csv appended since the previous call on a :holes cursor (FeedCursor), advancing it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.declared_holes"},{"category":"type","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.FeedCursor","page":"RunAdapter","text":"FeedCursor(run::RunHandle, feed::Symbol)\n\nA position in one of the append-only feeds of a run, :arrivals (events_rx.csv) or :holes (holes.csv): the byte offset past the last complete line consumed and the count of rows read. Each call of arrival_events or declared_holes on the cursor reads only the bytes appended since the previous call, so following a feed costs O(new rows) per poll, where the after forms re-read the whole file. A line still being written (no terminating newline yet) is left for the next call. No file handle is held between calls, and an absent file yields no rows until it appears.\n\narrivals = RunAdapter.FeedCursor(run, :arrivals)\nwhile RunAdapter.run_state(run) === :active\n    for e in RunAdapter.arrival_events(arrivals)\n        # consume e\n    end\n    sleep(poll_interval)\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.FeedCursor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/runadapter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.segment_files","page":"RunAdapter","text":"segment_files(run::RunHandle, name::AbstractString; delivered::Bool = false)\n    -> Tuple{String,Vector{String}}\n\nThe directory of the batch name, under ground/ or, for a lost batch, under lost/ (never with delivered = true), and its segment file names in ascending segment identifier. Throws an ArgumentError for a batch that is absent, pruned, or holds no segment file, and with delivered = true for a lost one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.RunAdapter.segment_files"},{"category":"section","location":"api/supervisor/#Supervisor","page":"Supervisor","text":"","title":"Supervisor"},{"category":"module","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor","page":"Supervisor","text":"Supervisor\n\nThe mission orchestration layer: assembles a validated MissionPlan from the configuration, runs the emitter and the receiver as supervised tasks on the tick driver (abort / continue / restart policies, the single-writer component_events.csv), keeps the lifecycle sentinels consistent on every exit path, and drives the failure-isolated post-processing stages. The headless entry point scripts/run_full_sim.jl is argument parsing plus one call to run_mission. The figures are drawn by a figure backend that adds methods to TelemetryCore.render_figure_product (figure_backend_loaded): DeepSpaceTelemetry does; the headless package alone writes the tables only.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor"},{"category":"type","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.CleanFileLogger","page":"Supervisor","text":"CleanFileLogger(path::String, rotate_bytes::Int)\n\nComponent log sink: one [Level] message line per record, structured keyword values on indented lines, exceptions rendered compactly, no └ @ Module file:line suffix, and ANSI escape sequences stripped. Each record opens the file anew (a few lines per batch), so the file can rotate to name#k.log at retention.log_rotate_mb between records. A record whose rotation or write fails is dropped with a line on stderr, since a throwing logger would terminate the task it logs for.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.CleanFileLogger"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.with_supervisor_log","page":"Supervisor","text":"with_supervisor_log(f, run_dir::String, rotate_bytes::Int; append::Bool = false)\n\nRuns f() with the current logger teed into <run_dir>/supervisor.log (CleanFileLogger, rotated at rotate_bytes), so the [SUPERVISOR], [POST], and [CONFIG] records of the mission and its post-processing are on disk beside the component logs, where the console output of a detached run is not. The file is truncated at entry unless append is set, in which case the new records follow the existing ones.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.with_supervisor_log"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.strip_ansi","page":"Supervisor","text":"strip_ansi(text::AbstractString) -> String\n\nRemoves ANSI escape sequences (colors, cursor motion) from a log message.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.strip_ansi"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.render_log_value","page":"Supervisor","text":"render_log_value(v) -> String\n\nText of one structured log value for CleanFileLogger: exceptions (also the exception of an (exception, backtrace) tuple) through showerror, everything else through string; a value whose rendering throws yields a <unprintable T> placeholder, so the logger itself never throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.render_log_value"},{"category":"type","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.MissionPlan","page":"Supervisor","text":"MissionPlan\n\nEverything derived from a validated configuration before the mission clock starts: the run ID, the [simulation] scalars, the payload origin (Emitter.instrument_epoch), the typed [telemetry], [physics], [supervision], [post_processing], [post_processing.publication], [ground], [dashboard], and [contacts] settings, the composite link model, the loss channel (seeded with simulation.rng_seed), the retry limit, and the retention policy. Built by mission_plan; nothing on disk depends on it until run_mission creates the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.MissionPlan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.mission_plan","page":"Supervisor","text":"mission_plan(cfg::Dict{String,Any}; run_id = \"\") -> MissionPlan\n\nValidates cfg (safe intervals, then the storage budget), builds the channel models, and fixes the run ID (generated when empty); the loss channel, the run's only random stream, is seeded with simulation.rng_seed. Writes nothing to disk and leaves cfg unmodified: the plan carries a shallow copy, which becomes the run's configuration snapshot and receives in cfg[\"provenance\"] the payload origin (payload_origin, the content instant of payload row 1 as an ISO-8601 string) and, when physics.data_source = \"external\", the external-input provenance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.mission_plan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.stamp_external_provenance!","page":"Supervisor","text":"stamp_external_provenance!(cfg, physics, needed_days)\n\nAdds external_data_path, external_data_rows, external_data_sha256, declared_sample_rate, and external_data_columns (the names of the payload columns, [\"Amplitude\"] when physics.external_data_columns is empty) to cfg[\"provenance\"], and logs whether the input covers needed_days. Row count and SHA-256 are computed before any directory exists: exhaustion is reported at startup, and the snapshot pins the input by its bytes alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.stamp_external_provenance!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.record_generation_gap!","page":"Supervisor","text":"record_generation_gap!(run_dir, clock, resume; origin, sample_rate,\n                       points_per_batch, hole_feed = nothing)\n\nBounds an emitter outage in events_tx.csv (Batch = STREAM): gap_start at the content end of the last finalized batch, gap_end at resume, the content instant at which the replacement instrument resumes. The content end is the instant of row payload_row + points_per_batch of the highest payload_row stamp, on the payload grid of origin and sample_rate, or origin when no batch has been finalized. The gap thus covers the rows of the partial batch the failed emitter held. With a hole_feed, the same interval is appended to the declared-hole feed as a stream hole declared at the current mission time. Called before the replacement emitter is spawned, while no events_tx writer is live.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.record_generation_gap!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.log_component_event!","page":"Supervisor","text":"log_component_event!(run_dir, clock, component, event)\n\nAppends one row to the single-writer component_events.csv (SimTime,Component,Event; events down and restart) through TelemetryCore.write_table, the header with the first row. Instants are written in the form of TelemetryCore.timestamp_text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.log_component_event!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.apply_failure_policy!","page":"Supervisor","text":"apply_failure_policy!(name, task, run_dir, clock, stop_flag, policy, restart_counts, spawners, on_restart) -> Union{Task,Nothing}\n\nThe supervisor's response to a component task that ended with an exception, applied by supervise_ticks!. The failure is reported and recorded as down; policy.on_component_failure then decides. \"restart\" calls on_restart(name, attempt), relaunches through spawners[name], and returns the replacement task, up to policy.max_restarts times; an exhausted budget is treated as \"abort\". \"continue\" leaves the partner running one-sided. \"abort\" raises stop_flag so the partner stops cooperatively. Returns nothing when the component stays down.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.apply_failure_policy!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.supervise_ticks!","page":"Supervisor","text":"supervise_ticks!(spawners, run_dir, clock::TelemetryCore.TickClock, stop_flag, policy;\n                 on_restart = (name, attempt) -> nothing,\n                 heartbeats = Dict{Symbol,String}(),\n                 halt_path = joinpath(run_dir, \"HALT\")) -> Dict{Symbol,Int}\n\nRuns the components until all of them have finished and returns the restart count per component; drives both modes, the paced wall mode and the unpaced tick mode, through the pace of clock. spawners[name](attempt) returns the component's task, created through TelemetryCore.tick_spawn (attempt 0 is the primary launch, attempt ≥ 1 a supervised restart). The tasks are driven by TelemetryCore.run_ticks! on the calling thread; a task that ends with an exception meets policy.on_component_failure through apply_failure_policy!, a restart calling on_restart(name, attempt) first, up to policy.max_restarts times. A task that never parks holds the clock. The driver touches the files of heartbeats while it releases wakeups and removes them when it returns. A paced driver stops sleeping once stop_flag is raised or halt_path exists. Lifecycle transitions are appended to component_events.csv; failures, policy decisions, and restarts are logged as [SUPERVISOR] records through the logger of the calling task (the global logger under run_mission; the component tasks log to their own files).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.supervise_ticks!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.build_instrument","page":"Supervisor","text":"build_instrument(physics::NamedTuple, origin::DateTime, markers) -> VirtualInstrument.InstrumentState\n\nThe payload instrument of [physics] with payload origin origin, the start of the initial blind spot (Emitter.instrument_epoch), where the pre-population begins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.build_instrument"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.component_spawners","page":"Supervisor","text":"component_spawners(plan, run_dir, clock, stop_flag, instrument,\n                   pending_segments, emitter_logger, receiver_logger,\n                   orig_stdout; spawn, hole_feed = nothing)\n    -> Dict{Symbol,Function}\n\nThe emitter and receiver launchers consumed by supervise_ticks!. spawn(name, f) turns a component body into its task on the mission's tick clock (TelemetryCore.tick_spawn). Attempt 0 continues the pre-populated instrument and partial batch. A restarted emitter (attempt ≥ 1) starts without a partial batch, on the instrument VirtualInstrument.resumed from its predecessor at the current mission time; the generation gap this leaves is bounded in events_tx.csv by record_generation_gap! at the resumption instant, before the replacement task starts. hole_feed is the run's declared-hole feed (TelemetryCore.HoleFeed), shared by the emitter, the receiver, and the outage record; nothing writes no feed. Both components share one on-board count, seeded from onboard/ here and rebuilt from its census by every emitter, restarted or not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.component_spawners"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.figure_backend_loaded","page":"Supervisor","text":"figure_backend_loaded() -> Bool\n\nWhether a figure backend is loaded: a package that draws the mission summary through a method of TelemetryCore.render_figure_product for Val(:mission_summary), as DeepSpaceTelemetry does. Without one, post_process! writes the tables and skips the figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.figure_backend_loaded"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.TIMELINE_PRODUCTS","page":"Supervisor","text":"TIMELINE_PRODUCTS\n\nFlags of the figure products drawn from the mask timeline; post_process! renders them after it and the other products before it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.TIMELINE_PRODUCTS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.render_products!","page":"Supervisor","text":"render_products!(plan, run_dir::String, orig_stdout::IO, selected)\n\nRenders the flagged figure products of TelemetryCore.FIGURE_PRODUCTS for which selected(product) holds, in table order, each failure-isolated. A stage of post_process!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.render_products!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.post_process!","page":"Supervisor","text":"post_process!(plan, run_dir; orig_stdout = stdout, figures = true)\n\nThe derived products after both components have finished. Each stage is failure-isolated: the simulation data is already on disk, so an error is reported but does not abort the remaining stages, and every product can be regenerated by calling this function or its stage again. In order:\n\nthe gap table (Masks.gap_table, derived once and written by Masks.write_gap_table), the declared-hole feed holes.csv settled from it (Masks.settle_hole_feed!), and a warning for any feed row the table does not derive (Masks.hole_feed_excess);\nthe label-span table (Masks.write_label_spans), only when absent (it is written at mission start);\nthe metrology tables (Metrology.write_metrology_tables), whatever the figure flags;\nthe flagged figure products of TelemetryCore.FIGURE_PRODUCTS that do not read the mask timeline (alert latency and delivery delay);\nthe 2D batch-state timeline (post_processing.generate_mask_timeline) and the flagged products drawn from it (TIMELINE_PRODUCTS, the batch-state raster);\nthe mission summary and session figures (the figure backend's method for Val(:mission_summary));\nthe HDF5 product export (hdf5_export) and the publication figure export ([post_processing.publication]);\nthe run descriptor (TelemetryCore.write_run_descriptor), last because its hashes cover the products above.\n\nThe figure stages (4, the products of 5, and 6) and the publication export run only when figures is set; the tables, the mask timeline, the HDF5 export, and the descriptor do not depend on it. The cheap tables come first, so a failure of the mask replay or of a figure stage leaves them written.\n\nplan is a MissionPlan or any object with the properties read here: post_processing, ground, publication, physics, mission_days, and initial_downtime_days, such as the result of post_processing_plan.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.post_process!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.post_processing_plan","page":"Supervisor","text":"post_processing_plan(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nWhat post_process! reads, taken from a configuration or a run snapshot without building the mission models: post_processing, ground, publication, physics, and the [simulation] scalars mode, mission_days (the span in either mode), and initial_downtime_days. The retention policy rides along for the log rotation size of complete_run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.post_processing_plan"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.STRANDED_IDLE_SEC","page":"Supervisor","text":"STRANDED_IDLE_SEC\n\nSeconds without a write to the run directory after which complete_run takes a stranded run for abandoned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.STRANDED_IDLE_SEC"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.mission_ended","page":"Supervisor","text":"mission_ended(run_dir::String, anchor) -> Bool\n\nWhether the record of a run reaches the end of its mission, anchor being the result of TelemetryCore.load_clock_anchor, or nothing. The last row of mission_profile.csv must lie within one second, or one percent of the respective span if that is longer, of the end: its SimTime against the horizon, or, for an anchor without one (wall runs before 2.8.0), its WallTime against deadline_wall. false without an anchor or a profile, and for an anchor with neither a horizon nor a wall deadline. A profile that stops earlier belongs to a process that died during the mission.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.mission_ended"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.complete_run","page":"Supervisor","text":"complete_run(run_id::AbstractString; force = false, orig_stdout = stdout) -> String\n\nCompletes a stranded run: a run directory that holds RUN_ACTIVE and neither RUN_COMPLETE nor RUN_ABORTED because its supervisor process was killed. The post-processing is run again from the data on disk (post_process!, with the settings of the run's own snapshot) and the sentinels are settled: RUN_COMPLETE when the mission phase had ended, RUN_ABORTED when the process died before the end of its mission and the record is truncated. The mission phase counts as ended when RUN_POSTPROCESSING is present or, failing that, when mission_ended holds for clock_anchor.toml: the metrics profile reaches the horizon (or, for a wall anchor written before 2.8.0, the wall deadline) within its tolerance.\n\nRefuses, with an ArgumentError, a directory that is not stranded, a paced run whose nominal wall deadline (deadline_wall) has not passed, and, unless force is set, a directory written to within the last STRANDED_IDLE_SEC seconds, where a supervisor may still be post-processing. Returns the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.complete_run"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.contact_summary","page":"Supervisor","text":"contact_summary(plan::MissionPlan) -> String\n\nOne-line description of the contact schedule for the mission banner: the daily window with its seasonal extension and exception count, or the explicit pass count, plus the low-latency periods and whether they are enabled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.contact_summary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.print_banner","page":"Supervisor","text":"print_banner(io::IO, plan::MissionPlan, run_dir::String)\n\nWrites the mission-start banner to io: run ID, mission span and speed-up, link capacity and the daily capacity balance, the contact schedule (contact_summary), markers, generation gaps, the on-board recorder ceiling, and the log location.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.print_banner"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.execute_mission!","page":"Supervisor","text":"execute_mission!(plan, run_dir, orig_stdout; figures = true)\n\nThe mission proper inside an existing run directory: fresh component logs, the sequential pre-population of the onboard buffer, the mission phase on the tick clock, and the post-processing stages (post_process!, which receives figures). Both modes run one path: a TelemetryCore.TickClock spanning the mission (plan.mission_days in tick mode, speed_up · mission_wall_seconds in wall mode), with its persisted anchor, and the cooperative component tasks driven on this thread (supervise_ticks!), with the heartbeat files emitter_alive and receiver_alive. Touches RUN_POSTPROCESSING at the end of the mission phase; the other lifecycle sentinels belong to run_mission, its caller.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.execute_mission!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/supervisor/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.run_mission","page":"Supervisor","text":"run_mission(cfg::Dict{String,Any}; run_id = \"\", orig_stdout = stdout,\n            figures = true, data_root = TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT[]) -> String\n\nThe headless pipeline end to end: mission_plan (validation, storage gate, models, provenance), the run directory <data_root>/runs/<run_id> with its configuration snapshot, and execute_mission! under with_supervisor_log. Returns the run directory. figures = false skips every figure stage and the publication export (the sweep driver uses it); the tables are written regardless.\n\nLifecycle sentinels: RUN_ACTIVE while the pipeline may still write, with RUN_POSTPROCESSING beside it from the end of the mission phase to the end of the lifecycle; then RUN_COMPLETE at lifecycle end or RUN_ABORTED on any escaping exception. RUN_COMPLETE marks the end of the lifecycle, not success: a failed component still reaches it after the failure-isolated post-processing. A directory left with RUN_ACTIVE and no process is settled by complete_run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Supervisor.run_mission"},{"category":"section","location":"api/telemetrycore/#TelemetryCore","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"","title":"TelemetryCore"},{"category":"module","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"TelemetryCore\n\nShared infrastructure of the simulation: configuration loading, schema-aware validation, storage governance (artifact estimation, budgets, retention settings), the mission clock and its persisted anchor, run provenance (event logs, snapshots, safesave rotation), and batch/segment I/O. Routing is implemented in Emitter and Receiver. The source is split by concern into the files of src/TelemetryCore/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.C_LIGHT","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"C_LIGHT\n\nSpeed of light in vacuum [m s⁻¹].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.C_LIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DATA_ROOT\n\nDefault base directory for run storage (a Ref; default default_data_root of the package root): the data root of the entry points (Supervisor.run_mission, the scripts, RunAdapter.open_run by run ID) when none is given. The components take their run directory explicitly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT_VARIABLE","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DATA_ROOT_VARIABLE\n\nThe environment variable that names the default data root (default_data_root): DEEPSPACETELEMETRY_DATA.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT_VARIABLE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.default_data_root","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"default_data_root(root::AbstractString) -> String\n\nThe data root a package rooted at root uses by default: the directory named by the environment variable DATA_ROOT_VARIABLE when it is set and not empty (made absolute), else <root>/data. A container or a cluster job sets the variable to a mounted volume.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.default_data_root"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PROJECT_ROOT","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"PROJECT_ROOT\n\nAbsolute path of the package root (a Ref), which holds config.toml, scenarios/ and data/. physics.external_data_path resolves against it; contacts.schedule_csv and the config file resolve against the current directory first, then against it. A run records the root's source identity. DeepSpaceTelemetry sets it to its own root when it loads; on its own, the reader package points it at the reader's root.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PROJECT_ROOT"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.run_directory","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"run_directory(run_id::String, data_root::String = DATA_ROOT[]) -> String\n\nRun-directory path <data_root>/runs/<run_id>.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.run_directory"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.runs_root","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"runs_root() -> String\n\nDirectory holding every run directory (<DATA_ROOT>/runs).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.runs_root"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_run_dir","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"checked_run_dir(run_dir::String) -> String\n\nrun_dir when it is an absolute path, else an ArgumentError: the components address a run by its directory, and a bare run ID resolves through run_directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_run_dir"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.latest_run_id","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"latest_run_id() -> Union{Nothing, String}\n\nID of the most recently modified run directory under runs_root that carries a config_snapshot.toml, or nothing when no run exists. Used by the post-processing scripts when no run ID is given on the command line.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.latest_run_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_name","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_name(id::Integer, live::Bool) -> String\n\nDirectory name of batch id: LIVE_batch_<id> for a batch finalized while the link was transmittable, ARCH_batch_<id> otherwise.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.batch_name(42, true)\n\"LIVE_batch_42\"\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.batch_name(42, false)\n\"ARCH_batch_42\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_name"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_id","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_id(name::AbstractString) -> Int\n\nNumeric ID parsed from a batch directory name (the trailing _<id> field); 0 for a name that does not carry one, such as a stray directory entry.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.batch_id(\"LIVE_batch_42\")\n42\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.batch_id(\"stray_entry\")\n0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_live_batch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"is_live_batch(name::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\ntrue for a LIVE_batch_<id> directory name (finalized while the link was transmittable).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.is_live_batch(\"LIVE_batch_7\"), TelemetryCore.is_live_batch(\"ARCH_batch_7\")\n(true, false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_live_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_archive_batch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"is_archive_batch(name::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\ntrue for an ARCH_batch_<id> directory name (blind-spot or blackout generation, delivered by the LIFO backfill).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_archive_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_batch_name","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"is_batch_name(name::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\ntrue for either batch class; false for any other directory entry.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_batch_name"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_config","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_config(path::String = \"\") -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe parsed TOML configuration at path. A relative path resolves against the current directory first, then the project root; an empty path reads config.toml by the same rule. A missing or unparsable file raises a [CONFIG] ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.csv_header","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"csv_header(path::AbstractString) -> Vector{String}\n\nThe column names of a CSV file from its first line: split on commas, surrounding whitespace and double quotes removed. Empty for an empty file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.csv_header"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_run_config_with_source","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_run_config_with_source(run_dir::String) -> Tuple{Dict{String,Any},String}\n\nThe configuration that produced a run, parsed from the run's own config_snapshot.toml, and its source, always \"snapshot\". The second value is kept for the config_source attribute of the HDF5 export. A missing or unparsable snapshot raises a [CONFIG] ArgumentError: the directory is not a run of this package.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_run_config_with_source"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_run_config","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_run_config(run_dir::String)\n\nThe configuration that produced a given run, read from its own config_snapshot.toml (load_run_config_with_source without the source). Post-processing reads this and not load_config: an edit of config.toml would re-parametrize the analysis of old runs (disruption windows, session times, physics rates).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_run_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mask_timeline_path","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mask_timeline_path(run_dir::String) -> String\n\nPath of the batch-state timeline masks/telemetry_mask_timeline.csv of a run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mask_timeline_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_mask_timeline","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"read_mask_timeline(run_dir::String) -> DataFrame\n\nThe batch-state timeline of a run as a table, one wide row per event snapshot. Read on a single task: CSV.jl's multithreaded chunking logs a failure on that shape before falling back to one task anyway.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_mask_timeline"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_number","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"checked_number(v, name::String) -> Float64\n\nCoerces a config value to Float64, aborting with a [CONFIG] error naming the key when the TOML value is not numeric (e.g. a quoted \"3600\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_number"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_integer","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"checked_integer(v, name::String) -> Int\n\nCoerces a config value to Int with a [CONFIG] error naming the key on non-integer TOML values (strings, floats, booleans).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_integer"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_string","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"checked_string(v, name::String) -> String\n\nCoerces a config value to String with a [CONFIG] error naming the key when the TOML value is not a string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_string"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_flag","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"checked_flag(v, name::String) -> Bool\n\nCoerces a config value to Bool with a [CONFIG] error naming the key when the TOML value is not a boolean (e.g. a quoted \"true\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.checked_flag"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.dashboard_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"dashboard_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [dashboard] flags, each a boolean: open_live_viewer, open_receiver_log, and open_emitter_log (the terminals the dashboard launcher opens; default true) and receiver_status_panel (the receiver's in-console status panel; default false). A non-boolean value is rejected with a [CONFIG] error.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.dashboard_settings(Dict{String,Any}()).receiver_status_panel\nfalse\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.dashboard_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.config_error","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"config_error(msg::AbstractString)\n\nRejects a configuration with an ArgumentError carrying the [CONFIG] message. The validator and the coercions reject through this function.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.config_error"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.required_value","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"required_value(section::AbstractDict, sec_name::String, key::String)\n\nFetches a required configuration key; an absent key raises a [CONFIG] error naming sec_name.key.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.required_value"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.reject_removed_key","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"reject_removed_key(section::AbstractDict, sec_name::String, key::String)\n\nRaises a [CONFIG] ArgumentError naming the replacement when section still carries key, a retired configuration key (REMOVED_CONFIG_KEYS); returns nothing otherwise. Called by validate_config.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.reject_removed_key"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_wall_seconds","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mission_wall_seconds(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Float64\n\nValidated wall-clock mission span simulation.mission_wall_seconds (> 0).\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.mission_wall_seconds(Dict{String,Any}(\"simulation\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"mission_wall_seconds\" => 168.0)))\n168.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_wall_seconds"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.SIMULATION_MODES","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"SIMULATION_MODES\n\nThe values of simulation.mode: \"wall\", the operational mode, on the TickClock paced against the wall clock at speed_up for mission_wall_seconds, and \"tick\", the analysis mode on the unpaced logical clock spanning mission_days.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.SIMULATION_MODES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.simulation_mode","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"simulation_mode(cfg::AbstractDict) -> String\n\nValidated simulation.mode, one of SIMULATION_MODES; \"wall\" when absent.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.simulation_mode(Dict{String,Any}())\n\"wall\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.simulation_mode"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_days_setting","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mission_days_setting(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Float64\n\nValidated simulation.mission_days (> 0), the span of a mission in simulation.mode = \"tick\"; missing is a configuration error naming the mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_days_setting"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_span_days","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mission_span_days(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Float64\n\nThe mission span in days in either mode: mission_wall_seconds × speed_up / 86 400 in wall mode, mission_days in tick mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_span_days"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.simulation_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"simulation_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [simulation] scalars: mode (simulation_mode), start_sim_time (mission_epoch), mission_days, the span in either mode (mission_span_days), speed_up > 0 and mission_wall_seconds in wall mode (NaN in tick mode, where they have no value), initial_downtime_days ≥ 0 (default 0), and the integer rng_seed (default 0).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.simulation_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.tick_configuration","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"tick_configuration(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nA copy of a configuration switched to simulation.mode = \"tick\" with the same mission span: mission_days set from mission_span_days, speed_up and mission_wall_seconds removed, and the console status panel off. A wall scenario runs deterministically through it; a configuration already in tick mode comes back unchanged.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> cfg = Dict{String,Any}(\"simulation\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"speed_up\" => 3600.0, \"mission_wall_seconds\" => 168.0, \"start_sim_time\" => \"2035-01-01T06:00:00\"));\n\njulia> sim = TelemetryCore.tick_configuration(cfg)[\"simulation\"];\n\njulia> sim[\"mode\"], sim[\"mission_days\"], haskey(sim, \"speed_up\")\n(\"tick\", 7.0, false)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.tick_configuration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.telemetry_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"telemetry_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [telemetry] parameters: session_start::Time, session_duration::Second (session_duration_hours ∈ (0, 24]: consecutive daily windows do not overlap), bandwidth_profile::String (one of BANDWIDTH_PROFILES), sigmoid_steepness, gaussian_sigma, max_batches_per_hour, max_inflight_batches::Int, min_link_factor, and range_million_km ≥ 0 (spacecraft–Earth range; 0 disables the light-time delay) with the derived round_trip_light_time_sec = 2 · range / c.\n\nThe link capacity is given either as max_batches_per_hour or as the rate pair downlink_kbps and onboard_data_rate_kbps (both > 0); the two forms are mutually exclusive, and the pair fixes the capacity in batches per hour through the batch content span of [physics]. nominal_batch_transfer_sec is the transfer time of one batch at full capacity; catch_up_ratio (downlink over production rate) and onboard_data_rate_kbps are NaN in the batches-per-hour form.\n\nBounds raise [CONFIG] errors. Absent keys take their defaults, so the snapshot of an older run still reads; validate_config applies the required-key policy of a live configuration.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> cfg = Dict{String,Any}(\n           \"telemetry\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"downlink_kbps\" => 230.0, \"onboard_data_rate_kbps\" => 75.0),\n           \"physics\" => Dict{String,Any}(\n               \"data_source\" => \"synthetic\",\n               \"sample_rate\" => 4.0,\n               \"segment_duration_sec\" => 60.0,\n               \"batch_size\" => 10,\n           ),\n       );\n\njulia> tel = TelemetryCore.telemetry_settings(cfg);\n\njulia> round(tel.nominal_batch_transfer_sec; digits = 1), round(tel.max_batches_per_hour; digits = 1), round(tel.catch_up_ratio; digits = 2)\n(195.7, 18.4, 3.07)\n\njulia> tel = TelemetryCore.telemetry_settings(Dict{String,Any}(\"telemetry\" => Dict{String,Any}(\"max_batches_per_hour\" => 20.0)));\n\njulia> tel.nominal_batch_transfer_sec, isnan(tel.catch_up_ratio)\n(180.0, true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.telemetry_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.samples_per_segment","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"samples_per_segment(sample_rate, segment_duration_sec) -> Int\n\nNumber of samples in one segment, sample_rate × segment_duration_sec rounded to the nearest integer. ArgumentError when the product is below one: the sample period would exceed the segment.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.samples_per_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.points_per_batch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"points_per_batch(physics) -> Int\n\nPayload rows of one batch, samples_per_segment(sample_rate, segment_duration_sec) · batch_size, from the [physics] settings (physics_settings) or any object with those three fields. The descriptor, the hole feed, the gap table and the run adapter use it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.points_per_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.physics_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"physics_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [physics] parameters: data_source (\"synthetic\" or \"external\"), external_data_path (as configured; consumers resolve it against the package root), external_data_columns, sample_rate > 0, segment_duration_sec > 0 and a whole number of milliseconds (the resolution of the content clock), and batch_size ≥ 1, with at least one sample per segment. The four core keys are required.\n\nexternal_data_columns lists unique, non-empty column names of the external file, ingested in that order, and is rejected with the synthetic source; left empty, the Amplitude column is ingested, or else the first column, under the name Amplitude.\n\nRetired keys and the existence of the external file are checked by validate_config only: post-processing reads the snapshot of a finished run after a key was retired or the input file removed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.physics_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_whole_milliseconds","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"is_whole_milliseconds(seconds::Real) -> Bool\n\nWhether seconds is a whole number of milliseconds, at least one, to within the rounding of a decimal literal (0.07 * 1000 is not exactly 70). The content clock is a DateTime, which resolves milliseconds.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_whole_milliseconds"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.segment_period","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"segment_period(segment_duration_sec::Real) -> Millisecond\n\nContent span of one segment on the mission clock. ArgumentError unless the duration is a whole number of milliseconds (is_whole_milliseconds).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.segment_period"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.supervision_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"supervision_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [supervision] parameters: on_component_failure (\"abort\", \"continue\", or \"restart\", lower-cased; default \"abort\"), and max_restarts ≥ 0 (default 3).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.supervision_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.visibility_model","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"visibility_model(cfg::AbstractDict) -> VisibilityModel\n\nThe VisibilityModel described by [telemetry] and [contacts], built from telemetry_settings and contacts_settings. Low-latency periods enter only when contacts.low_latency_enabled is set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.visibility_model"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.capacity_balance","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"capacity_balance(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nCapacity of one nominal pass against the daily production, from telemetry_settings, physics_settings, and the pass profile: rate_form (the capacity is given as the physical rate pair), profile_mean (profile_mean of the pass profile), pass_hours (telemetry.session_duration_hours), capacity_per_pass (batches, max_batches_per_hour × profile_mean × pass_hours), and produced_per_day (batches, 86 400 / (batch_size × segment_duration_sec)). Seasonal extensions, exceptions, and low-latency periods are not included: the figure is the balance of the nominal daily pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.capacity_balance"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.BANDWIDTH_PROFILES","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"BANDWIDTH_PROFILES\n\nCapacity profiles of a nominal pass accepted in telemetry.bandwidth_profile and by VisibilityModel; profile_factor defines each.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.BANDWIDTH_PROFILES"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.hours_period","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"hours_period(hours::Float64) -> Millisecond\n\nThe Millisecond period of a duration given in hours, rounded to the millisecond (contact-window and low-latency-period lengths).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.hours_period"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ContactWindow","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"ContactWindow\n\nOne ground-contact interval on the mission timeline: start and stop (DateTime), the link capacity relative to peak (1.0 for a nominal pass; the station-availability fraction of a low-latency period), the low_latency flag, and a free-text label (empty for nominal passes). The window is half-open, [start, stop). ContactWindow(start, stop) builds a nominal pass.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ContactWindow"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ContactsSettings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"ContactsSettings\n\nValidated [contacts] section as returned by contacts_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ContactsSettings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.contacts_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"contacts_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> ContactsSettings\n\nValidated [contacts] section: the ground-contact schedule layered on the daily window of [telemetry]. Every key is optional; an absent section gives the plain daily window.\n\nseasonal_extension_hours ≥ 0 (default 0): peak extension of the daily window, cosine-modulated over season_period_days > 0 (default 365.25) and peaking at season_peak_day_of_year ∈ [1, 366] (default 172); telemetry.session_duration_hours plus the extension may not exceed 24 h.\n[[contacts.passes]] (start datetime, duration_hours > 0) or schedule_csv (columns Start, DurationHours; relative paths resolve against the current directory, then the project root) — an explicit, non-overlapping pass list that replaces the daily generator. The two forms are mutually exclusive.\n[[contacts.exceptions]] (date, optional start, duration_hours ∈ [0, 24], 0 = missed pass): the window of that date verbatim, in place of the generated one; incompatible with an explicit schedule.\nlow_latency_enabled (default true), low_latency_capacity_fraction ∈ (0, 1] (default 1), and [[contacts.low_latency_periods]] (start, duration_hours > 0, optional capacity_fraction, label): extra contact windows at constant capacity; a nominal pass takes precedence where they overlap. Periods triggered by [[events.markers]] (event_marker_settings) are appended, labeled by the marker.\n\nMalformed entries raise a [CONFIG] error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.contacts_settings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.EventMarker","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"EventMarker\n\nOne [[events.markers]] entry: the mission instant time of an event of interest (a transient, a glitch), its label, and an optional triggered low-latency period — low_latency_after_hours after the marker, lasting low_latency_duration_hours (0 = none) at low_latency_capacity_fraction of peak capacity (NaN = the [contacts] default). EventMarker(time, label) builds a marker without a triggered period.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.EventMarker"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.event_marker_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"event_marker_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Vector{EventMarker}\n\nValidated [[events.markers]] entries sorted by time: time (datetime, required), label (default marker <i>), low_latency_after_hours ≥ 0, low_latency_duration_hours ≥ 0, and low_latency_capacity_fraction ∈ (0, 1] when given. Labels must be unique. A malformed marker raises a [CONFIG] error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.event_marker_settings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.LabelSpan","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"LabelSpan\n\nA labeled span of payload rows declared by the scenario ([[labels]]): its label and the inclusive payload rows row_first … row_last. A span with row_first > row_last is empty (an event without labeled samples) and is dropped from the label-span table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.LabelSpan"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.label_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"label_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Vector{LabelSpan}\n\nValidated [[labels]] entries in file order: label (default label <i>), label_start_index and label_end_index (integers ≥ 1, both required). A malformed entry raises a [CONFIG] error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.label_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ground_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"ground_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [ground] section: processing_latency_hours ≥ 0 (default 1, the low-latency alert pipeline budget added to every alert latency).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.ground_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.publication_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"publication_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [post_processing.publication]: enabled (default false), format (\"pdf\" | \"svg\", default \"pdf\"), column_width_mm ∈ [100, 400] (default 178; the standard layout is scaled as a whole to this printed width, PlotTheme.style_for_width), and export_dir (default \"\" = <run_dir>/publication; relative paths resolve against the current directory). Unrecognized keys warn.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.publication_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.post_processing_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"post_processing_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [post_processing] section: generate_mask_timeline (default true), alert_lookback_hours > 0 (72), delivery_requirement_hours > 0 (24), hdf5_export (false), session_figures (true, the per-contact session figures), session_figures_max ≥ 0 (0; the cap on their number, 0 for no cap), and figures, a NamedTuple of booleans keyed by the flags of FIGURE_PRODUCTS with their defaults; [post_processing.publication] is read by publication_settings.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> pp = TelemetryCore.post_processing_settings(Dict{String,Any}());\n\njulia> pp.generate_mask_timeline, pp.hdf5_export, pp.alert_lookback_hours\n(true, false, 72.0)\n\njulia> pp.figures\n(alert_latency = true, delivery_delay = true, state_raster = true)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.post_processing_settings"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.FigureProduct","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"FigureProduct\n\nA figure of the post-processing that a [post_processing] flag switches: flag (the configuration key, also the stem of the figure file), default, announcement (the console line printed before rendering), and title (the subject of the failure record). render_figure_product draws it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.FigureProduct"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.FIGURE_PRODUCTS","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"FIGURE_PRODUCTS\n\nThe flagged figure products, in rendering order. The schema, the artifact estimator, the post-processing and the publication export iterate this table; a new product is one entry here and one render_figure_product method.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.FIGURE_PRODUCTS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.render_figure_product","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"render_figure_product(product::Val, run_dir::String, post_processing::NamedTuple, ground::NamedTuple; kwargs...) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRenders the figure product Val(key) for the run in run_dir and returns the figure path, or nothing when the run holds nothing to draw. The keys are the flags of FIGURE_PRODUCTS, :mission_summary (the mission and session figures), and :publication (the journal-width export). post_processing and ground are the results of post_processing_settings and ground_settings. The keywords are those of the plotting functions (style, plots_dir, formats, suffix; format, column_width_mm, export_dir for :publication). The methods live in the main package, in the modules that own the figures (MissionFigures, MetrologyFigures, Publication); the supervisor of the headless package calls them when that package is loaded.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.render_figure_product"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_markers","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_markers(markers::Vector{EventMarker}, epoch::DateTime, stop::DateTime) -> Vector{EventMarker}\n\nThe markers whose instant lies in the content span [epoch, stop) of a batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_markers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_row_time","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"payload_row_time(origin::DateTime, sample_rate::Real, row::Integer) -> DateTime\n\nThe mission instant of payload row row: origin + (row − 1) / sample_rate on the millisecond grid.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_row_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_time_row","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"payload_time_row(origin::DateTime, sample_rate::Real, t::DateTime) -> Int\n\nThe payload row at the mission instant t, the inverse of payload_row_time.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_time_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_epoch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mission_epoch(cfg::AbstractDict) -> DateTime\n\nThe mission epoch simulation.start_sim_time of a configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_epoch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_end","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"mission_end(cfg::AbstractDict) -> DateTime\n\nThe nominal end of the mission, mission_epoch(cfg) plus mission_span_days rounded to the millisecond; the gap table closes a generation gap still open at the end of the run at this instant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.mission_end"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_origin","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"payload_origin(cfg::AbstractDict) -> DateTime\n\nThe content instant of payload row 1: provenance.payload_origin of a run snapshot when present, otherwise mission_epoch(cfg) less simulation.initial_downtime_days (clamped at zero days), the rule of Emitter.instrument_epoch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_origin"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_markers","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_markers(run_dir::String, markers::Vector{EventMarker};\n             origin = nothing, sample_rate = 0.0)\n\nWrites the run's event instants to <run_dir>/markers.csv (SimTime, Label, PayloadRow). PayloadRow, the payload row of the marker's instant, is written when origin is given and sample_rate > 0. Nothing is written when there are no markers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_markers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_markers","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_markers(run_dir::String) -> Vector{EventMarker}\n\nThe markers recorded in <run_dir>/markers.csv (empty when absent), as plain EventMarkers without triggered periods.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_markers"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_datetime","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"parsed_datetime(v, name::String) -> DateTime\n\nCoerces a config value to DateTime: TOML local datetimes and dates arrive already parsed, strings are read as ISO-8601; anything else is rejected with a [CONFIG] error naming name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_datetime"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_date","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"parsed_date(v, name::String) -> Date\n\nCoerces a config value to Date (TOML local date, datetime, or ISO-8601 string) with a [CONFIG] error naming name otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_date"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_time","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"parsed_time(v, name::String) -> Time\n\nCoerces a config value to Time (TOML local time or HH:MM:SS string) with a [CONFIG] error naming name otherwise.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parsed_time"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.loss_channel_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"loss_channel_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nValidated [packet_loss] parameters: enabled, model (\"bernoulli\" or \"gilbert_elliott\", lower-cased), the five probabilities p_loss, p_good_to_bad, p_bad_to_good, p_loss_good, p_loss_bad (each in [0, 1]), on_loss (\"retransmit\" or \"drop\"), and max_retries ≥ 0. Types, enumerations, and bounds are enforced also when the channel is disabled.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.loss_channel_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.gilbert_elliott_loss_rate","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"gilbert_elliott_loss_rate(p_good_to_bad, p_bad_to_good, p_loss_good, p_loss_bad;\n                          in_bad_state = false) -> Float64\n\nStationary loss probability of the two-state Gilbert–Elliott channel, π_bad·p_loss_bad + (1 − π_bad)·p_loss_good with the stationary occupancy π_bad = p_good_to_bad / (p_good_to_bad + p_bad_to_good). A channel with both transition probabilities zero keeps the loss probability of the state it is in (in_bad_state; a channel starts in the good state).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.gilbert_elliott_loss_rate"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DisruptionEventSettings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DisruptionEventSettings\n\nOne validated [[disruption.events]] entry as returned by disruption_event_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DisruptionEventSettings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.disruption_event_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"disruption_event_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> Vector{DisruptionEventSettings}\n\nValidated [[disruption.events]] entries in file order: type, label, start_day ≥ 0 (required), duration_hours > 0, recovery_hours ≥ 0, severity ∈ [0, 1], loss_multiplier, and affects — \"link\" (the default: a capacity and loss disruption) or \"generation\" (a scheduled gap in data production of duration_hours; severity, recovery, and loss keys are ignored). type = \"antenna_repointing\" defaults to \"generation\". A malformed event raises a [CONFIG] error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.disruption_event_settings"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.onboard_capacity","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"onboard_capacity(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe on-board recorder ceiling from telemetry.onboard_capacity_days > 0 (storage.onboard_capacity_days in a snapshot written before 2.11): days; batches, the production over that span in whole batches (at least one), which the emitter enforces; and gigabit, the physical volume when the link is given as a rate pair, else NaN. The default of 14 days is the autonomy without ground contact of the Definition Study Report (Colpi et al. 2024, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2402.07571).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.onboard_capacity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.open_recorder_gap_start","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"open_recorder_gap_start(run_dir::String) -> Union{DateTime,Nothing}\n\nThe SimTime of the RECORDER gap_start row of events_tx.csv not yet matched by a gap_end, or nothing when no recorder gap is open. A re-entering emitter closes that gap at its first finalization that finds room; the declared-hole feed declares the hole from this start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.open_recorder_gap_start"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.open_recorder_gap","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"open_recorder_gap(run_dir::String) -> Bool\n\nWhether the last recorder-overflow gap in events_tx.csv (rows with Batch = RECORDER) is still open — a gap_start without its gap_end — so a re-attaching emitter closes it when the buffer has room again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.open_recorder_gap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.validate_config","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"validate_config(cfg::AbstractDict)\n\nValidates a live configuration before any directory is created or any computation starts, and returns cfg; the safe intervals are documented in config.toml, the scenario files and the manual. A value that would break the pipeline raises a [CONFIG] ArgumentError; a runnable but suspicious one emits a @warn.\n\nErrors:\n\na missing required key: simulation.start_sim_time (with speed_up and mission_wall_seconds in wall mode, mission_days in tick mode), telemetry.session_start and session_duration_hours, the four core [physics] keys, and a link capacity\na span key of the other clock mode, and dashboard.receiver_status_panel = true in tick mode\nevery type, bound, and enumeration enforced by the accessors it calls: simulation_settings (with simulation_mode, mission_wall_seconds, mission_days_setting), storage_budget, retention_settings, telemetry_settings, physics_settings, loss_channel_settings, disruption_event_settings, contacts_settings (with event_marker_settings), label_settings, ground_settings, publication_settings, post_processing_settings, dashboard_settings, supervision_settings, and onboard_capacity\na non-positive speed_up or [storage] value, a negative initial_downtime_days, a non-integer rng_seed, a non-boolean retention.enabled or non-positive [retention] value, and a retention.high_watermark_gb above the storage budget\ndata_source = \"external\" with a missing file or with an external_data_columns entry absent from its header\nthe retired keys of REMOVED_CONFIG_KEYS, rejected with the replacement named\n\nWarnings:\n\nthe rate pair with a shaped bandwidth_profile (the profile mean scales a rate the pass sustains; the warning states the capacity of one nominal pass against the daily production)\na non-integer sample_rate × segment_duration_sec (rounded)\ninitial_downtime_days, or the longest interval without ground contact, beyond the on-board recorder capacity\nwith packet loss enabled, Gilbert–Elliott p_bad_to_good = 0 (the channel never recovers), and loss saturation: the worst-state per-attempt loss times the largest loss_multiplier reaches 1, so every transfer fails in that regime\ndisruption events that start at or after mission end (they never fire), black out the link to mission end, or have their blackout + recovery tail truncated by it; overlapping events (capacity composes as the minimum, the loss multiplier as the maximum); loss_multiplier < 1 (no effect) or NaN (no loss while the event is active)\npost_processing.state_raster with generate_mask_timeline disabled (the raster is drawn from the mask timeline and is skipped)\nunrecognized sections and keys, which would otherwise fall back to their defaults unnoticed\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.validate_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.check_storage_limits","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"check_storage_limits(cfg::AbstractDict)\n\nPre-run storage safety gate. Logs the per-class artifact estimate (estimate_artifacts), then enforces the [storage] budgets with mitigation awareness:\n\nretention disabled: abort when the projected total exceeds the budget (the error names [retention] as the mitigation); warn within 10 % of it.\nretention enabled: abort only when even the steady-state footprint (non-prunable classes + payload capped at the watermark) exceeds the budget; otherwise warn if the unbounded projection exceeds the budget and proceed. Additionally warns when the payload generated within one grace_hours window alone exceeds the watermark (the custodian could never satisfy both constraints simultaneously).\n\nThe same logic gates storage.max_file_count, and the replay and the figure-stage RAM estimates are each gated against storage.max_ram_gb (a mitigation-free hard budget). Every abort is a StorageBudgetError. Returns nothing; called before any run directory is created.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.check_storage_limits"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.estimate_artifacts","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"estimate_artifacts(cfg::AbstractDict) -> NamedTuple\n\nClosed-form per-class footprint of a run, from the configuration alone; counts that depend on stochastic outcomes are upper bounds. The classes are payload segment CSVs, batch metadata, event logs, metrics, the 2D mask timeline, the HDF5 export, plots (the session and summary figures and those of FIGURE_PRODUCTS, each as PNG and PDF; the optional GIF is a manual product and is excluded), and text logs. The calibration constants are measured (STORAGE_CALIBRATION).\n\nReturns the counts n_segments, n_batches, n_points, n_columns (payload columns per segment file: one for a synthetic run, else the length of physics.external_data_columns, at least one), mission_days, and metrics_rows; the per-class byte fields, total_bytes, and file_count; replay_ram_bytes and figure_ram_bytes, the RAM of the post-processing replay and of the figure stage (one figure at a time), both gated against storage.max_ram_gb; and the retention-prunable subset prunable_bytes, prunable_files: the payload scaled by the expected delivered fraction under the configured loss model. Metadata, event logs, metrics, masks, and lost/, where terminally lost batches land, are not prunable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.estimate_artifacts"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.storage_budget","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"storage_budget(cfg::AbstractDict) -> (max_gb, max_files, max_ram_gb)\n\nThe run-directory budgets of [storage]: disk max_storage_gb [GB] (default 5.0), file count max_file_count (default 1000000), and post-processing RAM max_ram_gb [GB] (default 8.0) for the replay and the figure stage. Every budget is > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.storage_budget"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.StorageBudgetError","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"StorageBudgetError(msg::String)\n\nRaised by check_storage_limits when the projected disk volume, file count, or post-processing RAM (replay or figure stage) exceeds its [storage] budget and no configured mitigation bounds it. msg carries the [STORAGE] diagnosis: the estimate, the budget, and the remedies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.StorageBudgetError"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.RetentionPolicy","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"RetentionPolicy\n\nImmutable operating parameters of the retention custodian: enabled, grace (the mission-time availability guarantee for delivered payloads, as a Millisecond period), watermark_bytes (prunable-payload size that triggers pruning), and log_rotate_bytes (size-capped log rotation, active regardless of enabled). Constructed by retention_settings.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.RetentionPolicy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.retention_settings","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"retention_settings(cfg::AbstractDict) -> RetentionPolicy\n\nParses the [retention] section into a RetentionPolicy: retention.grace_hours converts to a Millisecond period at parse time, and high_watermark_gb defaults to 75 % of the storage budget and may not exceed it, since the custodian prunes only above the watermark. grace_hours, high_watermark_gb and log_rotate_mb are > 0.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.retention_settings"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS\n\nWall-clock interval [ms] at which the tick driver (run_ticks!) refreshes the components' liveness files.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.STORAGE_CALIBRATION","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"STORAGE_CALIBRATION\n\nMeasured calibration constants of estimate_artifacts: per-artifact disk sizes [bytes], and the RAM of the replay (bytes_replay_cell, per metrics row × batch membership) and of the figure stage (bytes_figure_stage, bytes_figure_row).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.STORAGE_CALIBRATION"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.config_sha256","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"config_sha256(cfg::AbstractDict) -> String\n\nSHA-256, as 64 hex digits, of the configuration printed as sorted TOML without its provenance section: the identity of the parameters alone, the same on every host and for every run of one configuration.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.config_sha256"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.generate_run_id","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"generate_run_id(cfg::AbstractDict) -> String\n\nRun identifier RUN_cfg=<8 hex>_pid=<pid>_t=<yyyymmdd_HHMMSS>: the first eight hex digits of config_sha256, the process id, and the wall-clock instant — key=value fields in alphabetical order, the layout of DrWatson's savename. The hash makes the runs of one configuration recognizable by name; the process id and the instant keep the identifier unique.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.generate_run_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.package_version","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"package_version() -> String\n\nVersion of the package at PROJECT_ROOT, read from its Project.toml: the producer of a run, which is DeepSpaceTelemetry when it hosts the reader and the reader package itself otherwise. Falls back to the reader's own version when that file is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.package_version"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.platform_provenance","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"platform_provenance() -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nHardware and runtime fingerprint stamped into every run's config_snapshot.toml under [provenance.platform]: hostname, OS kernel and architecture, CPU model and logical core count, total memory, Julia version and versioninfo() report, thread and BLAS-thread counts, the package version, and the source identity of source_identity (identity_source, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty, repo_url, repo_rev), and reader_tree_sha1, the tree hash of the reader package's directory. The manifest is recorded beside the snapshot by save_manifest_snapshot. There are no GPU fields: the pipeline is I/O- and event-loop-bound and uses no GPU backend.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.platform_provenance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.source_identity","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"source_identity(root = PROJECT_ROOT[]; manifest = active_manifest_path()) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe identity of the package source at root, stamped into [provenance.platform] and copied into descriptor.toml:\n\nidentity_source — how it was determined: \"work_tree\" when root is the top level of a git work tree (git_work_tree) or a directory tracked at HEAD of one (the headless package of a clone); \"manifest\" for an installed copy whose package UUID has an entry with a git-tree-sha1 in the manifest manifest (the active environment's by default, active_manifest_path); \"directory\" otherwise.\ngit_commit — the HEAD commit of the work tree; for an installed copy the entry's repo-rev when it is a full 40-digit commit hash, as in a [sources] pin by commit; empty otherwise (a branch or tag, a registry install, a bare directory).\ngit_tree_sha1 — the git tree hash of the source: HEAD^{tree} of the work tree (HEAD:<path> for a tracked directory), the entry's git-tree-sha1, or the hash of the directory itself (git_tree_sha1). Never empty; for a source that is not dirty, the same value for the same content however it was obtained.\ngit_dirty — whether the code at root differs from that identity: uncommitted changes to tracked files of the work tree (anywhere in it for a tracked directory, whose path dependencies lie beside it), or an installed directory whose tree hash differs from the entry's (with a warning). false for \"directory\", whose identity is the content itself.\nrepo_url, repo_rev — the entry's repo-url and repo-rev; empty unless identity_source == \"manifest\" and the copy was added by URL.\n\nThe directory hash leaves out .git and, under the data root (DATA_ROOT) when it lies inside root, everything but the .gitkeep placeholders the repository tracks there: run outputs stay out of the hash and a pristine installed copy hashes to its manifest entry whatever the data root is set to. It also leaves out the manifests (Manifest.toml, JuliaManifest.toml, and their -vX.Y forms), which the repository does not track, so a copied directory with its environment instantiated hashes to its committed tree.\n\nExample\n\nid = TelemetryCore.source_identity()\nid.git_tree_sha1   # 40 hexadecimal digits, for a checkout and an installed copy alike\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.source_identity"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.active_manifest_path","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"active_manifest_path(project = Base.active_project()) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nThe manifest of the environment whose project file is project, resolved as Julia's code loading resolves it (a workspace root, a manifest key, the version-specific Manifest-vX.Y.toml before Manifest.toml), or nothing when there is no project or no manifest. On a Julia without the internal Base.project_file_manifest_path it falls back to the manifest next to the project file (manifest_beside).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.active_manifest_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.manifest_beside","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"manifest_beside(project::AbstractString) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nThe manifest next to the project file project, the version-specific Manifest-v<major>.<minor>.toml first, then Manifest.toml; nothing when neither exists. The fallback of active_manifest_path for a Julia without Base.project_file_manifest_path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.manifest_beside"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.manifest_entry","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"manifest_entry(root, manifest) -> Union{Dict{String,Any},Nothing}\n\nThe entry of the manifest file manifest for the package whose project file lies at root, matched by name and UUID, or nothing when either file or the entry is absent. Reads the manifest format of Julia 1.7 and later ([[deps.Name]]); an older manifest yields nothing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.manifest_entry"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_sha1","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_tree_sha1(dir::AbstractString; exclude = String[], skip_manifests = false) -> String\n\nThe git tree hash (40 hexadecimal digits) of the directory dir as git would record it if every file under it were committed: entries in git's order, modes 100644, 100755 (owner-executable), 120000 (symbolic link, hashed as its target) and 40000 (directory), empty directories and .git entries left out, as git write-tree and Pkg do. The hash of an unmodified installed copy equals the git-tree-sha1 of its manifest entry and git rev-parse <commit>^{tree} of the commit it was installed from. Under the paths in exclude only the .gitkeep placeholders are hashed (PLACEHOLDER_NAME): a directory the repository tracks as empty keeps its placeholder and loses the content written into it. With skip_manifests, files named Manifest.toml, JuliaManifest.toml, or a -vX.Y form of either are left out at every level.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_sha1"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.reader_tree_sha1","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"reader_tree_sha1() -> String\n\nThe git tree hash of the reader package's directory: HEAD:<path> when it is tracked in a clone of this repository without uncommitted changes to tracked files, the hash of the directory as it is otherwise (git_tree_sha1, manifests left out), so that a modified reader never records a committed tree; empty when the reader's directory is unknown. Without git, files git would ignore enter the directory hash.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.reader_tree_sha1"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_entries","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_tree_entries(dir, excluded) -> Vector{Tuple{String,String,Vector{UInt8}}}\n\nThe (mode, name, object hash) entries of the git tree of dir, in git's order (a directory sorts as its name followed by /). Under a path in excluded, or with placeholders_only, only files named PLACEHOLDER_NAME are hashed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_entries"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PLACEHOLDER_NAME","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"The file the repository tracks as a placeholder of a directory kept empty of source (data/runs/.gitkeep); the only content hashed under an excluded path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PLACEHOLDER_NAME"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_digest","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_tree_digest(entries) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nThe SHA-1 of the git tree object made of entries (git_tree_entries).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_tree_digest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_blob_digest","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_blob_digest(source) -> Vector{UInt8}\n\nThe SHA-1 of the git blob object holding source: the bytes of a file path, read in chunks, or a byte vector (the target of a symbolic link).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_blob_digest"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_work_tree","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_work_tree(root = PROJECT_ROOT[]) -> Bool\n\nWhether root is the top level of a git work tree with a commit at HEAD; false when git is not installed. A directory inside another repository does not count, since an installed copy under a version-controlled depot would otherwise be given that repository's commit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_work_tree"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_commit","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_commit(root = PROJECT_ROOT[]) -> String\n\nThe HEAD commit of the git work tree at root, or \"\" when root is not one (git_work_tree). source_identity covers installed copies as well.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_commit"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_dirty","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_dirty(root = PROJECT_ROOT[]) -> Bool\n\nWhether the git work tree at root carries uncommitted changes to tracked files (git status --porcelain --untracked-files=no); false when root is not a work tree (git_work_tree). A run made from a dirty tree is not reproducible from its recorded commit alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_dirty"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_output","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"git_output(arguments::Cmd; root = PROJECT_ROOT[]) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nStandard output of git -C <root> <arguments>, stripped, or nothing when git is not installed or the command fails (no repository).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.git_output"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_manifest_snapshot","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_manifest_snapshot(run_dir::String) -> Union{String,Nothing}\n\nCopies the manifest of the active environment (active_manifest_path) to <run_dir>/manifest_snapshot.toml, so the run records the dependency versions it was resolved on; manifests are not tracked in the repository. Returns the snapshot path, or nothing with a warning when the active environment has no manifest.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_manifest_snapshot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.setup_run_dir","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"setup_run_dir(run_id::String; cfg = nothing, data_root = DATA_ROOT[]) -> String\n\nCreates and returns the run directory of run_id under data_root (run_directory) with its subdirectories onboard, link, ground, lost, plots, and masks; a directory under the same run ID that holds anything but dotfiles is rejected with an ArgumentError. Given the parsed configuration cfg, it writes config_snapshot.toml, the run's own copy of its parameters (rotating an existing one, backup_existing). The snapshot adds the platform_provenance fingerprint under [provenance.platform] and the parameter hash config_sha256 at provenance.config_sha256; the manifest of the active environment is copied beside it (save_manifest_snapshot).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.setup_run_dir"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.backup_existing","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"backup_existing(path::String) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nIf path exists, renames it to <name>#<k><ext> with the smallest unused k, so #1 is the oldest backup. DrWatson's safesave numbers the other way (<name>_#1<ext> is the newest); the manual's \"Provenance conventions\" table lists the correspondences. Returns the backup path, or nothing if path did not exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.backup_existing"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.backup_existing_dir","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"backup_existing_dir(path::String) -> Union{String, Nothing}\n\nDirectory counterpart of backup_existing: renames an existing directory to <name>#<k> (smallest unused k) with a [SAFESAVE] warning, before a same-named arrival takes its place. Returns the backup path, or nothing when path did not exist.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.backup_existing_dir"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TIMESTAMP_COLUMNS","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"TIMESTAMP_COLUMNS\n\nColumns of the run-directory tables that hold instants. read_table reads them as DateTime whatever the CSV parser would infer, and write_table writes every DateTime column in one fixed form.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TIMESTAMP_COLUMNS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.timestamp_text","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"timestamp_text(t) -> Union{String,Missing}\n\nThe lexical form of an instant in the run-directory tables: yyyy-mm-ddTHH:MM:SS.s, the seconds with at least one and at most three decimals (2035-01-04T12:00:36.0, 2026-09-24T02:01:17.051). missing stays missing.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.timestamp_text"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_table","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"read_table(path::AbstractString; kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nReads a table of the run directory with stated types: the columns of TIMESTAMP_COLUMNS present in its header as DateTime (missing where a field is empty) and the text columns of the contract (Batch, Event, Cause, Class, Type, Label, Component) as String, whatever their content; the other columns are inferred. The inference of the CSV parser is not part of the file contract: CSV.jl 1.x reads an ISO instant as a nanosecond timestamp type and text as a string type of its own. kwargs are passed to CSV.read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parse_table","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"parse_table(header::AbstractString, body::AbstractVector{UInt8}; kwargs...) -> DataFrame\n\nThe rows of body, complete CSV lines without a header, under the header line header, typed as read_table types a file. kwargs are passed to CSV.read.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.parse_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.write_table","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"write_table(path::AbstractString, table; append = false) -> String\n\nWrites a table of the run directory, every DateTime column as text in the form of timestamp_text, which is part of the file contract. The default of the CSV writer is not, and it changed between CSV.jl 0.10 and 1.x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.write_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.safe_csv_write","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"safe_csv_write(path::String, table) -> String\n\nWrites table to path as CSV, first rotating any pre-existing file to a #k-suffixed backup via backup_existing. Instants are written in the fixed form of timestamp_text. Returns path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.safe_csv_write"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.MissionClock","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"MissionClock\n\nThe clock a mission runs on, with one subtype, TickClock. The components read the current instant through get_current_sim_time, wait through wait_until!, wait_until_or_change!, and wait_for_change!, and stop at horizon_reached.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.MissionClock"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.get_current_sim_time","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"get_current_sim_time(clock::TickClock) -> DateTime\n\nThe current logical instant of the tick clock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.get_current_sim_time"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TickClock","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"TickClock(start_sim_time::DateTime, horizon::DateTime;\n          wall_epoch = now(), pace = nothing) <: MissionClock\n\nThe mission clock. Mission time advances only while every component task is parked, jumping to the earliest requested wakeup, so a mission is a deterministic sequence of events for a given seed. now_sim is the current instant, horizon the mission end (start_sim_time plus mission_span_days), and wall_epoch the wall instant of the start. pace (> 0, stored as Float64) is the speed-up of a paced run (simulation.mode = \"wall\") and nothing for an unpaced one (--tick). run_ticks! states the pacing rule and drives the components that tick_spawn creates.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TickClock"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TickWakeup","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"TickWakeup\n\nA parked component of the TickClock: the mission instant at it asked to resume at, its component priority (the emitter before the receiver at an equal instant), the registration sequence breaking the remaining ties, and the release channel the driver signals.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.TickWakeup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.horizon_reached","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"horizon_reached(clock::TickClock) -> Bool\n\nWhether the clock's instant has reached its horizon.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.horizon_reached"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.loop_should_stop","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"loop_should_stop(clock::TickClock, stop, halt_path::String, tag::String) -> Bool\n\nThe exit test of a component loop: the cooperative stop flag raised (nothing for none), the HALT sentinel at halt_path present, or the horizon reached (horizon_reached); the cause is logged under tag (e.g. \"[EMITTER]\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.loop_should_stop"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.EMITTER_PRIORITY","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"EMITTER_PRIORITY\n\nWakeup priority of the emitter under the TickClock: at an equal instant generation precedes service, as the instrument delivers a segment after observing it and the ground serves what is on the link.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.EMITTER_PRIORITY"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.RECEIVER_PRIORITY","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"RECEIVER_PRIORITY\n\nWakeup priority of the receiver under the TickClock, after the emitter (EMITTER_PRIORITY).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.RECEIVER_PRIORITY"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_until!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"wait_until!(clock::TickClock, sim_time::DateTime; priority = 0)\n\nBlocks the calling component until the mission clock reaches sim_time: the task parks with a wakeup at sim_time (or now, if that is later) and priority, and resumes once the driver has advanced the clock to it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_until!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_until_or_change!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"wait_until_or_change!(clock::TickClock, sim_time::DateTime; priority = 0)\n\nLike wait_until!, but a wait that another component's activity may cut short: the task resumes at sim_time or at the next wakeup of another component, whichever comes first. The receiver waits this way for a deferred retransmission, so a batch placed on the link in the meantime is served without waiting out the round trip.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_until_or_change!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_for_change!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"wait_for_change!(clock::TickClock; priority = 0)\n\nParks the calling task at the next wakeup of another component (or the horizon, when none is parked). The task resumes after that component has run when its priority value is not smaller than the component's. The receiver's idle wait: the link changes only when the emitter runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.wait_for_change!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.park!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"park!(clock::TickClock, at::DateTime, priority::Int)\n\nRegisters a wakeup of the calling task at at with priority, tells the driver the task has parked, and blocks until the driver releases it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.park!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.earliest_wakeup","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"earliest_wakeup(clock::TickClock) -> DateTime\n\nThe earliest instant a parked task asked to resume at, or the horizon when none is parked.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.earliest_wakeup"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.tick_spawn","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"tick_spawn(clock::TickClock, priority::Int, f) -> Task\n\nCreates the task of a component under the tick clock: f() starts when the driver first releases the task, at the current instant with priority, and the driver is told when the task parks or ends. The task runs on the driver's thread, so the components interleave only at their waits.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.tick_spawn"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.run_ticks!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"run_ticks!(clock::TickClock, tasks::Dict{Symbol,Task};\n           on_task_failed = (name, task) -> throw(TaskFailedException(task)),\n           heartbeats = Dict{Symbol,String}(), should_stop = () -> false)\n\nThe driver of the tick clock. It advances the clock to the earliest wakeup (ties broken by component priority, then by registration order), releases that task, waits until it parks again or ends, and repeats until no task is parked. The clock stops at the horizon: a later wakeup resumes there, where the component sees horizon_reached and leaves. A task that ended with an exception is handed to on_task_failed(name, task) once; the callback returns a replacement Task created by tick_spawn, which takes the name in tasks, or nothing. Without a callback the driver ends with the task's exception. Runs on the calling task's thread.\n\nOn a paced clock (clock.pace set, simulation.mode = \"wall\") the driver sleeps, before each release, until the wall instant wall_epoch + (t − start_sim_time) / pace of the logical instant t, in chunks of at most 0.5 s; a host that lags releases at once and catches up. should_stop() (the HALT sentinel or the stop flag) is tested between chunks and ends the sleep, so the components, which check HALT at every wakeup, exit within 0.5 s. An unpaced clock never sleeps.\n\nheartbeats maps component names to liveness files. The driver touches every file at the start, then at most once per HEARTBEAT_INTERVAL_MS, after a release or between sleep chunks, and removes them when it returns normally.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.run_ticks!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_clock_anchor","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_clock_anchor(run_dir::String, clock::TickClock)\n\nPersists the anchor of a mission into <run_dir>/clock_anchor.toml, rotating an existing file (backup_existing): mode (\"wall\" for a paced clock, \"tick\" otherwise), wall_epoch, the mission epoch start_sim_time, and the horizon horizon_sim; a paced clock adds speed_up (its pace) and deadline_wall, the nominal wall instant of the horizon. Written once at mission start and read back by load_clock_anchor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_clock_anchor"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DESCRIPTOR_PRODUCTS","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DESCRIPTOR_PRODUCTS\n\nThe tabular products write_run_descriptor lists with their size and SHA-256, those present in the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DESCRIPTOR_PRODUCTS"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.write_run_descriptor","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"write_run_descriptor(run_dir::String) -> String\n\nWrites <run_dir>/descriptor.toml, the run's identity for a downstream collection to copy verbatim, and returns its path. Fields: schema, run_id, the producer's package_version and source identity (identity_source, git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty, repo_url, repo_rev; source_identity) and reader_tree_sha1, config_sha256, the clock mode, mission_days, start_sim_time, payload_origin, rng_seed, the payload geometry (sample_rate, segment_duration_sec, batch_size, points_per_batch), payload_columns (those stamped into the provenance of an external run, else [\"Amplitude\"]), external_data_sha256 and external_data_rows when recorded, written_at, and under [products] the size and SHA-256 of every table of DESCRIPTOR_PRODUCTS present. The source-identity fields added in 2.6.1 are empty for an earlier run.\n\nWritten last among the post-processing products, so the hashes are final. The file is written as descriptor.toml.partial and renamed into place, an existing descriptor being rotated first (backup_existing), so a reader finds the whole descriptor or none.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.write_run_descriptor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_clock_anchor","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_clock_anchor(run_dir::String) -> NamedTuple\n\nReads the anchor persisted by save_clock_anchor as (mode, start_sim_time, wall_epoch, horizon, speed_up, deadline_wall): mode is \"wall\" (also for anchors written before the key existed) or \"tick\"; start_sim_time and wall_epoch are the mission and wall instants of the start; horizon is the mission end horizon_sim, or nothing when the file has none (the anchors of wall runs made before 2.8.0 carry no horizon); speed_up is the pace of a wall run (NaN when absent); and deadline_wall is the nominal wall end of a wall run (nothing when absent). Throws an ArgumentError when the anchor file is absent.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_clock_anchor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.max_logged_batch_id","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"max_logged_batch_id(run_dir::String) -> Int\n\nHighest batch ID recorded in events_tx.csv, 0 when the log is absent or empty. A restarted emitter numbers its batches above it and above those still in onboard/, which delivered batches have left.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.max_logged_batch_id"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT\n\nThe name of the one payload column of a synthetic run and of an external run without physics.external_data_columns: Amplitude.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DataSegment","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DataSegment(id::Int, timestamp::DateTime, data::Matrix{Float32}, columns::Vector{String})\nDataSegment(id::Integer, timestamp::DateTime, data::AbstractVector{<:Real})\n\nOne segment_duration_sec span of the observed time series: the Float32 samples data, one row per sample and one column per payload column named in columns, the first sample at the mission instant timestamp. id is the segment's position on the payload grid, (timestamp − origin) / segment_duration_sec + 1 (DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_id), unique within a run. The vector form builds a one-column segment named PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT. Throws a DimensionMismatch when data does not have length(columns) columns and an ArgumentError when columns is empty.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DataSegment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_column_index","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"payload_column_index(columns, column::Union{Integer,AbstractString}) -> Union{Int,Nothing}\n\nIndex of a payload column in columns, given by index or by name; nothing when column is not one of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.payload_column_index"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.segment_column","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"segment_column(seg::DataSegment, column::Union{Integer,AbstractString}) -> Vector{Float32}\n\nThe samples of one payload column of seg, by index or by name; an ArgumentError names the columns when column is not one of them.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.segment_column"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DataBatch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"DataBatch(id::Int, segments::Vector{DataSegment}, created_at::DateTime)\n\nA collection of DataSegments prepared for bulk transmission over the DSN: id is the batch counter, segments the payload in content order, and created_at the mission instant of finalization (when the batch became transmittable; the content epoch is segments[1].timestamp).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.DataBatch"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.MissionMetrics","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"MissionMetrics\n\nA snapshot of the mission state including queue sizes, effective and nominal bandwidth, packet-loss counters, and the disruption flag. bandwidth_pct is the effective link capacity (visibility × disruption factor); nominal_bandwidth_pct is the visibility profile alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.MissionMetrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_metrics","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_metrics(run_dir::String, m::MissionMetrics)\n\nAppends a new metrics snapshot to mission_profile.csv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_metrics"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.log_tx_event","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"log_tx_event(run_dir::String, sim_t::DateTime, batch::String, event::String)\n\nAppends one row to events_tx.csv. Events: \"gen\" (batch finalized onboard), \"tx\" (batch placed on the downlink), \"marker\" (sim_t an event-marker instant, batch the batch holding it), and the \"gap_start\"/\"gap_end\" pair bounding a generation gap (batch = SCHEDULED, RECORDER, or STREAM); marker and gap rows preserve the batch states. With log_rx_event it records the per-batch state history that the mask and animation reconstruction replays. The emitter writes the file; the supervisor appends the STREAM pair only while no emitter is live, so there is one writer at a time and no lock.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.log_tx_event"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.log_rx_event","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"log_rx_event(run_dir::String, sim_t::DateTime, batch::String, event::String, attempt::Int)\n\nAppends one receiver-side batch milestone to events_rx.csv. Events: \"ingested\" (batch reached the ground archive), \"retry\" (transfer attempt lost, batch remains on the link), \"lost\" (retry budget exhausted, batch moved to lost/), and \"pruned\" (retention custodian deleted the delivered payload CSVs after the grace window; state-preserving for the mask replay). attempt counts failed transfer attempts so far (0 for \"pruned\"). The receiver task alone writes this file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.log_rx_event"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HOLE_FEED_COLUMNS","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"HOLE_FEED_COLUMNS\n\nThe columns of holes.csv, the declared-hole feed: DeclaredAt (the mission instant the hole was declared at), then the columns of the gap table, Cause, Batch, RowFirst, RowLast, ContentStart, ContentEnd.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HOLE_FEED_COLUMNS"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HoleFeed","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"HoleFeed(run_dir, origin::DateTime, sample_rate::Real, points_per_batch::Integer)\n\nThe writer of <run_dir>/holes.csv, the declared-hole feed: one row per hole of the delivered record, appended when its producer declares it. The receiver declares lost and pruned batches, the emitter scheduled and recorder gaps, and the supervisor emitter outages; after the mission phase Masks.settle_hole_feed! appends the end-of-run holes, so that the feed of a finished run and masks/gaps.csv hold the same holes. One instance is shared by the components of a run, and lock serializes their appends. The payload grid (origin, sample_rate) and points_per_batch let a batch name alone fix its rows. Consumers tail the file directly or through read_hole_feed and RunAdapter.declared_holes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.HoleFeed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.hole_feed","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"hole_feed(run_dir::String) -> HoleFeed\n\nThe feed of a run from its configuration snapshot: payload_origin(cfg), physics.sample_rate, and points_per_batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.hole_feed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_hole!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"declare_hole!(feed::HoleFeed, declared_at::DateTime, cause::AbstractString,\n              batch::Union{AbstractString,Missing}, rows::UnitRange{Int}) -> Bool\n\nAppends one row to the feed: declared_at, cause, batch (missing for a generation gap or the unreached tail), the inclusive rows, and the content interval [payload_row_time(first), payload_row_time(last + 1)). Returns false and writes nothing when rows is empty. The header is written with the first row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_hole!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_hole_rows","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_hole_rows(first_row, name::AbstractString,\n                points_per_batch::Integer) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nThe points_per_batch payload rows of batch name from its payload_row stamp first_row; an ArgumentError when the batch carries none (a run of a producer before 2.1.0, which this version does not read). Used by the hole feed, the gap table and the run adapter.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_hole_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.gap_hole_rows","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"gap_hole_rows(origin::DateTime, sample_rate::Real,\n              start::DateTime, stop::DateTime) -> UnitRange{Int}\n\nThe payload rows of the generation gap [start, stop): payload_time_row(origin, sample_rate, start) to payload_time_row(origin, sample_rate, stop) − 1, empty when the gap holds no row. Used where batch_hole_rows is.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.gap_hole_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_batch_hole!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"declare_batch_hole!(feed::HoleFeed, declared_at::DateTime, cause::AbstractString, batch_dir::AbstractString) -> Bool\n\nThe hole of the batch at batch_dir (its name is the batch), its rows by batch_hole_rows from the payload_row stamp of its metadata; cause is lost or pruned.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_batch_hole!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_gap_hole!","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"declare_gap_hole!(feed::HoleFeed, declared_at::DateTime, cause::AbstractString, start::DateTime, stop::DateTime) -> Bool\n\nThe hole of the generation gap [start, stop), its rows by gap_hole_rows; cause is scheduled, recorder, or stream. Nothing is written for an empty gap.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.declare_gap_hole!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_hole_feed","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"read_hole_feed(run_dir::String; after::Integer = 0) -> DataFrame\n\nThe rows of <run_dir>/holes.csv beyond the first after, typed as written (DeclaredAt, ContentStart, ContentEnd as DateTime); an empty table with the feed's columns when the file is absent or holds no further rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_hole_feed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_batch","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_batch(path::String, batch::DataBatch; markers = String[], payload_row = nothing)\n\nWrites batch into the directory path: metadata.json and one seg_<id>.csv per segment (save_segment), one column per payload column. The metadata carries batch_id, segment_count, created_at (the mission instant the batch was finalized and became transmittable), content_epoch (the mission timestamp of its first sample, the physical epoch of the data), and, when given, payload_row (the 1-based row of that sample in the payload series starting at the payload origin) and markers (the labels of the event markers whose instant lies in the payload).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_batch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_batch_metadata","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"read_batch_metadata(batch_dir::String) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nParses <batch_dir>/metadata.json. Returns an empty dictionary when the file is absent, since a directory without it is not a batch. An unparsable file (a truncated or corrupted batch) is an ArgumentError naming the directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.read_batch_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_metadata_values","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_metadata_values(parse_value, run_dir::String, key::String, T::Type) -> Dict{String,T}\n\nBatch name → parse_value(meta[key]) for every batch directory under onboard/, link/, ground/, and lost/ whose metadata.json records key; batches without the key, or whose value parse_value maps to nothing, are omitted.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_metadata_values"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_content_epochs","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_content_epochs(run_dir::String) -> Dict{String,DateTime}\n\nBatch name → content epoch (first-sample mission timestamp) for every batch directory under onboard/, link/, ground/, and lost/ whose metadata.json records a content_epoch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_content_epochs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_payload_rows","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"batch_payload_rows(run_dir::String) -> Dict{String,Int}\n\nBatch name → payload row of the first sample for every batch directory under onboard/, link/, ground/, and lost/ whose metadata.json records a payload_row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.batch_payload_rows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_segment","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"save_segment(path::String, seg::DataSegment)\n\nWrites the samples of seg to the CSV at path, one column per payload column under its name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.save_segment"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_segment","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"load_segment(path::String; timestamp::DateTime = DateTime(0)) -> DataSegment\n\nReads a segment CSV written by save_segment back into a DataSegment, one payload column per file column under its header name; the identifier comes from the file name seg_<id>.csv (0 when it does not conform). A non-numeric column raises an ArgumentError naming the file and the column. The file holds samples only, so a caller that knows the mission timestamp passes it as timestamp (the batch's content_epoch in metadata.json is that of its first segment); the default DateTime(0) marks it unknown.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.load_segment"},{"category":"type","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"VisibilityModel\n\nGround-contact model of the downlink. The nominal daily window opens at session_start for session_duration with the capacity profile (one of BANDWIDTH_PROFILES, shaped by sigmoid_steepness or gaussian_sigma; any other is an ArgumentError). seasonal_extension widens it symmetrically about its center, cosine-modulated with period season_period_days and peaking at season_peak_day_of_year. exceptions (date => (start, duration), zero duration for a missed pass) replace the window of a date verbatim, and a non-empty schedule of explicit passes replaces the daily generator. low_latency periods are additional windows at constant capacity; where one overlaps a nominal pass, the pass profile applies. The three- to five-argument constructors build the plain daily model.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel(Time(8), Second(8 * 3600), \"sine\");\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.is_visible(model, DateTime(2035, 1, 1, 10)), TelemetryCore.is_visible(model, DateTime(2035, 1, 1, 3))\n(true, false)\n\njulia> round(TelemetryCore.profile_mean(model); digits = 3)\n0.5\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.seasonal_window_duration","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"seasonal_window_duration(model::VisibilityModel, date::Date) -> Second\n\nDuration of the generated window on date: session_duration plus the seasonal extension modulated as ½ [1 + cos(2π (d − d_peak) / P)], where d is the day of year of date, the pass's own date. The full extension applies at season_peak_day_of_year and none half a period away. Over a long mission the duration follows the season pass by pass. The day of year restarts on 1 January, so the term repeats from year to year only for a period of one year.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.seasonal_window_duration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.nominal_window","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"nominal_window(model::VisibilityModel, date::Date) -> Union{Nothing,ContactWindow}\n\nThe nominal pass anchored on date under the daily generator: the exception of that date when one exists (nothing for a missed pass), otherwise the configured window extended symmetrically by the seasonal term. nothing in explicit-schedule mode.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.nominal_window"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.contact_windows","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"contact_windows(model::VisibilityModel, t_lo::DateTime, t_hi::DateTime) -> Vector{ContactWindow}\n\nThe contact windows, nominal passes and low-latency periods, that overlap [t_lo, t_hi], sorted by start.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.contact_windows"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.active_window","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"active_window(model::VisibilityModel, t::DateTime) -> Union{Nothing,ContactWindow}\n\nThe contact window containing t (windows are half-open, [start, stop)), or nothing when the spacecraft is out of contact; a nominal pass takes precedence over a low-latency period. Under the daily generator only the windows anchored on the date of t and on its two neighbouring dates can contain t: a pass crossing midnight, or one whose seasonal half-extension opens it the day before.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.active_window"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_visible","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"is_visible(model::VisibilityModel, t::DateTime) -> Bool\n\nWhether the spacecraft is in ground contact at t: inside a nominal pass or a low-latency period (active_window).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.is_visible"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.profile_factor","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"profile_factor(model::VisibilityModel, progress::Float64) -> Float64\n\nThe capacity profile of a nominal pass at the normalized position progress ∈ [0, 1] within the window: sin²(π p) for sine, [tanh(k p) + tanh(k (1 − p))] / 2 for sigmoid, exp(−(p − ½)² / (2σ²)) for gaussian, and 1 for flat. The sigmoid plateau is tanh(k/2), below 1 for a small steepness.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.profile_factor"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.profile_mean","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"profile_mean(model::VisibilityModel) -> Float64\n\nMean of the capacity profile over one nominal pass, ∫₀¹ profile_factor(model, p) dp, by composite Simpson quadrature on PROFILE_MEAN_SUBINTERVALS: 1 for flat, 1/2 for sine, ln(cosh k)/k for sigmoid with steepness k, and σ √(2π) erf(1/(2√2 σ)) for gaussian. Multiplied by the full-capacity rate and the pass length it gives the capacity of one pass in batches (capacity_balance).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.profile_mean"},{"category":"function","location":"api/telemetrycore/#DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor","page":"TelemetryCore","text":"get_bandwidth_factor(model::VisibilityModel, t::DateTime) -> Float64\n\nEffective link capacity in [0, 1] at t: the pass profile evaluated at the position of t within the active nominal window, the constant capacity fraction inside a low-latency period, and 0 out of contact.\n\nExamples\n\njulia> model = TelemetryCore.VisibilityModel(Time(8), Second(8 * 3600), \"sine\");\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor(model, DateTime(2035, 1, 1, 12))\n1.0\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor(model, DateTime(2035, 1, 1, 8))\n0.0\n\njulia> TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor(model, DateTime(2035, 1, 1, 20))\n0.0\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryReader.TelemetryCore.get_bandwidth_factor"},{"category":"section","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#MetrologyFigures","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"","title":"MetrologyFigures"},{"category":"module","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"MetrologyFigures\n\nThe figures of the metrology tables (Metrology): the alert-latency curve (plot_alert_latency) and the delivery-delay distribution against the requirement (plot_delivery_delay).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.plot_alert_latency","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"plot_alert_latency(run_dir::String; lookback_hours = 72.0, processing_latency_hours = 1.0, style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRenders <plots_dir>/alert_latency.png with a vector twin from alert_latency_table and marker_latency_table: the Kaplan–Meier median window-completeness latency and its interquartile band against the look-back, drawn where reached, realized in the archive color (solid) and counterfactual FIFO in PlotTheme.COLOR_COUNTERFACTUAL (dotted), one PlotTheme.COLOR_MARKER curve per event marker, and the medians at the largest look-back where both are reached annotated with the censored counts and the ground processing budget processing_latency_hours. Returns the PNG path, or nothing when the run holds no delivered live batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.plot_alert_latency"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.plot_delivery_delay","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"plot_delivery_delay(run_dir::String; requirement_hours = 24.0, style, plots_dir, formats, suffix) -> Union{Nothing,String}\n\nRenders <plots_dir>/delivery_delay.png with a vector twin from delivery_delay_table: the empirical distribution of the measurement-to-ground delay as the fraction of generated batches on the ground within a given delay, one curve per batch family and, when both exist, one for all batches, with the requirement rule and the compliance summary. The legend is omitted for a single curve. Returns the PNG path, or nothing when the run generated no batch.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.plot_delivery_delay"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.QUARTILE_BAND_ALPHA","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Fill alpha of the alert-latency interquartile bands and of their legend patches.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.QUARTILE_BAND_ALPHA"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.SINGLE_PANEL_SHARE","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Axis height of the single-panel metrology figures [PlotTheme.PlotStyle.panel_height].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.SINGLE_PANEL_SHARE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.Y_HEADROOM","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Upper y-limit of the delivery-delay figure: the unit fraction plus the headroom that keeps the top of the curves clear of the frame.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.Y_HEADROOM"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.ANNOTATION_BLOCK_BOTTOM","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Relative height at which the delivery-delay figure's corner annotation starts.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.ANNOTATION_BLOCK_BOTTOM"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.annotation_block_top","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"annotation_block_top(style::PlotTheme.PlotStyle, n_lines::Int) -> Float64\n\nRelative height the delivery-delay figure's corner annotation reaches: its bottom offset plus n_lines line advances, against the axis height of the figure, plus a margin of 0.02. The requirement rule stops here, and its label is placed above it by requirement_label_anchor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.annotation_block_top"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.REQUIREMENT_LABEL_MARGIN","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Relative clearance the requirement label of the delivery-delay figure keeps from the frame, from the curves across its footprint, and from the corner annotation.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.REQUIREMENT_LABEL_MARGIN"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.MIN_CURVE_SHARE","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Smallest fraction of the alert-latency figure's upper limit at which its curves may peak below the corner annotation (latency_axis_top).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.MIN_CURVE_SHARE"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.latency_axis_top","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"latency_axis_top(y_max::Real, n_lines::Integer, style::PlotTheme.PlotStyle) -> Float64\n\nUpper y-limit [h] of the alert-latency figure, whose lower limit is −0.05 of it, for curves peaking at y_max [h] below a corner annotation of n_lines lines. The annotation hangs from 0.97 of the axis height, one line advance per line; the curves peak 0.02 of the axis height below its bottom. With limits [-0.05 T, T], a height y sits at (y / T + 0.05) / 1.05 of the axis. Throws an ArgumentError when y_max is not finite and positive, when n_lines < 1, or when the annotation would leave the curves to peak below MIN_CURVE_SHARE of the upper limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.latency_axis_top"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_occupancy","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"requirement_label_occupancy(curves, xa::Real, xb::Real) -> Vector{Tuple{Float64,Float64}}\n\nRelative height intervals the drawn step curves of the delivery-delay figure cover over the abscissa band [xa, xb], one per curve present there. curves holds (x, y, drawn) triples of non-decreasing step data starting at the origin, each value holding from its abscissa to the next (the :post step of the drawn stairs); a curve whose last abscissa precedes the band is absent, one ending inside it covers up to its last value. Heights are divided by Y_HEADROOM, the figure's upper limit.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_occupancy"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_slot","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"requirement_label_slot(intervals, floor::Real, extent::Real) -> Union{Nothing,Tuple{Float64,Symbol}}\n\nFree slot for the delivery-delay figure's requirement label on one side of its rule: the relative ordinate of the anchor and the horizontal alignment of the rotated text (:right, the label hangs from the anchor; :left, it rises from it; :center), or nothing when the side holds no slot. intervals are the relative height intervals the curves occupy across the label's footprint on that side (requirement_label_occupancy), floor the relative height below which the rule is hidden (the corner annotation's top, or 0), extent the label length as a fraction of the axis height. The slot is above the highest interval when the room up to the frame holds the label with REQUIREMENT_LABEL_MARGIN at both ends, else below the lowest when the room down to floor does, else the middle of the widest gap between two intervals when it does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_slot"},{"category":"function","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_anchor","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"requirement_label_anchor(left, right, floor_left::Real, floor_right::Real, extent::Real) -> Tuple{Float64,Symbol,Symbol}\n\nAnchor of the delivery-delay figure's vertical requirement label: the relative ordinate, the horizontal alignment of the rotated text, and the side of the rule it stands on (:left or :right). left and right hold the height intervals the curves occupy across the label's footprint on each side (requirement_label_occupancy), floor_left and floor_right the heights below which the rule is hidden there, extent the label length as a fraction of the axis height. The label takes the first slot requirement_label_slot finds, on the left of the rule before the right, and the top of the left side when neither holds one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.requirement_label_anchor"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/metrologyfigures/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.ANNOTATION_BLOCK_LEFT","page":"MetrologyFigures","text":"Relative abscissa the delivery-delay figure's corner annotation reaches left to. A requirement rule beyond it would pass behind the block (annotation_block_top).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.MetrologyFigures.ANNOTATION_BLOCK_LEFT"},{"category":"section","location":"api/export/#Export","page":"Export","text":"","title":"Export"},{"category":"module","location":"api/export/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export","page":"Export","text":"Export\n\nHDF5 product export: the consumer-facing products of a run (event logs, hole feed, metrics profile, batch-state timeline, batch epochs, gap table, metrology tables, markers, label spans, component events) in one self-describing file with the run's provenance as attributes, for analysis pipelines that read HDF5 rather than a directory of CSV files. The CSV products remain the primary interface; the export is a derived, regenerable view of them (export_hdf5). The writer is an extension of the package, loaded with HDF5 (hdf5_available), so that the dependency closure of the package holds no HDF5 binary.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export"},{"category":"function","location":"api/export/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.export_hdf5","page":"Export","text":"export_hdf5(run_dir::String; path = joinpath(run_dir, \"products.h5\")) -> String\n\nWrites the run's products into one HDF5 file (default <run_dir>/products.h5, an existing file is rotated to products#k.h5): the tabular products of TABLE_PRODUCTS as column datasets, the batch-state timeline, and the provenance attributes. Times are seconds since the run's start_sim_time with ISO-8601 twins. Returns the file path. Throws an ArgumentError when HDF5 is not loaded (hdf5_available).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.export_hdf5"},{"category":"function","location":"api/export/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.hdf5_available","page":"Export","text":"hdf5_available() -> Bool\n\nWhether the HDF5 writer is loaded: the extension DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadlessHDF5Ext, which Julia loads with the HDF5 package. DeepSpaceTelemetry loads HDF5; the headless package alone does not.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.hdf5_available"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/export/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.FORMAT_VERSION","page":"Export","text":"FORMAT_VERSION\n\nLayout version of the exported file, stored as the root attribute format_version; incremented when a group or dataset changes meaning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.FORMAT_VERSION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/export/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.TABLE_PRODUCTS","page":"Export","text":"TABLE_PRODUCTS\n\n(group, relative path) pairs of the tabular CSV products written by export_hdf5, one group each; absent files are skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Export.TABLE_PRODUCTS"},{"category":"section","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#VirtualInstrument","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"","title":"VirtualInstrument"},{"category":"module","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"VirtualInstrument\n\nScience-payload data source. The synthetic payload is a binary flag series: 0 on a segment holding noise only, 1 on a segment holding a flagged signal, the flagged segments being those whose content span holds an event marker. The alternative source is an external CSV time series whose row r is the sample at origin + (r − 1) / sample_rate. An external file may carry several columns; those configured are ingested as one payload, each segment a samples × columns matrix, under one link accounting: one batch, one transfer, and one loss draw for all columns.\n\nThe instrument samples on a fixed grid anchored at the payload origin, the content instant of payload row 1. A generation gap suppresses whole segments of that grid and a restarted instrument resumes on it, so segment identifiers and payload rows follow from the content epoch alone.\n\nThe telemetry layers do not read the payload. The flag series states which delivered samples belong to an event; a physical noise or waveform model is supplied through the external source.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument"},{"category":"type","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.PayloadSource","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"PayloadSource\n\nOrigin of the samples of an InstrumentState. A source implements segment_samples(source, epoch, stop, first_row, n_samples) -> Matrix{Float32}, the samples × columns matrix of the segment with content span [epoch, stop) whose first sample is payload row first_row. Both addresses describe the same segment; a source reads the one it is indexed by.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.PayloadSource"},{"category":"type","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.FlaggedSignal","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"FlaggedSignal(instants::Vector{DateTime})\n\nSynthetic binary payload: a segment is 1 throughout when its content span [epoch, stop) holds one of instants, and 0 otherwise. The instants are the event markers of the scenario, so the series is declared by the configuration and involves no random draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.FlaggedSignal"},{"category":"type","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.ExternalSeries","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"ExternalSeries(samples::Matrix{Float32})\n\nExternally supplied time series addressed by payload row: row r of samples is payload row r, the sample at origin + (r − 1) / sample_rate of the InstrumentState that reads it, with one matrix column per payload column. Rows past the end read as zeros.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.ExternalSeries"},{"category":"type","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.InstrumentState","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"InstrumentState(origin, sample_rate, segment_duration_sec, data_source, ext_path;\n                markers = EventMarker[], columns = String[])\n\nState of the science payload: the payload origin origin (the content instant of payload row 1 and of segment 1), the content instant last_t of the next segment, the segment geometry, the PayloadSource the samples come from, and the names columns of the payload columns its segments carry.\n\nSegments open on the grid origin + k · segment_duration_sec, k ≥ 0: the segment opening at epoch has the identifier (epoch − origin) / segment_duration_sec + 1 (segment_id) and starts at payload row payload_row. The content clock starts at the origin and stays on the grid across a generation gap (advance_to!) and in the instrument of a restarted emitter (resumed), so identifiers do not repeat within a run and the payload continues at the rows of the resumed content instant.\n\ndata_source = \"synthetic\" builds a FlaggedSignal from the instants of markers, one column named TelemetryCore.PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT; a non-empty columns is then an ArgumentError. \"external\" reads the CSV at ext_path into an ExternalSeries through read_external_series, which states the column selection and the [CONFIG] errors. segment_duration_sec must be a whole number of milliseconds (TelemetryCore.segment_period) and hold at least one sample.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.InstrumentState"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.read_external_series","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"read_external_series(ext_path::String, columns::Vector{String} = String[]) -> Tuple{Matrix{Float32},Vector{String}}\n\nThe external payload series of the CSV at ext_path, relative paths resolved against the project root as in validate_config, as a samples × columns matrix and the names of its columns. An empty columns takes the column Amplitude when present and the first column otherwise, named TelemetryCore.PAYLOAD_COLUMN_DEFAULT; otherwise the listed columns are taken in that order under their own names. Every failure — an absent file, an unparsable or empty table, an absent column, a column that is not numeric or holds missing values — is a [CONFIG] error naming the file.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.read_external_series"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_boundary","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"segment_boundary(origin::DateTime, period::Millisecond, t::DateTime) -> DateTime\n\nThe first instant of the grid origin + k · period (k integer) at or after t.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_boundary"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_samples","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"segment_samples(source::PayloadSource, epoch, stop, first_row, n_samples) -> Matrix{Float32}\n\nThe n_samples samples of the segment with content span [epoch, stop), whose first sample is payload row first_row, as an n_samples × columns matrix: one column for a FlaggedSignal, one per payload column for an ExternalSeries. A FlaggedSignal is addressed by the span, an ExternalSeries by the rows first_row … first_row + n_samples − 1, rows past its end read as zeros in every column; both are stateless, so the samples of a segment depend on its position on the payload grid alone.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_samples"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_id","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"segment_id(vi::InstrumentState, epoch::DateTime) -> Int\n\nIdentifier of the segment opening at epoch, (epoch − origin) / segment_duration_sec + 1: segment 1 opens at the payload origin. ArgumentError when epoch precedes the origin or is not a grid instant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.segment_id"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.payload_row","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"payload_row(vi::InstrumentState, segment_id::Integer) -> Int\n\nPayload row of the first sample of segment segment_id, (segment_id − 1) · n + 1 with n = TelemetryCore.samples_per_segment. When sample_rate × segment_duration_sec is an integer this is (epoch − origin) · sample_rate + 1, the row whose instant is the segment's content epoch; otherwise (a configuration warned about at validation) consecutive segments still read consecutive, disjoint rows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.payload_row"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.advance_to!","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"advance_to!(vi::InstrumentState, t::DateTime) -> DateTime\n\nResumes the content clock at the first grid instant at or after t after a generation gap. The sampling phase of the payload origin is kept: a gap suppresses whole segments and never shifts the grid. A t at or before the current content instant leaves the clock unchanged. Returns the new content instant.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.advance_to!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.resumed","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"resumed(vi::InstrumentState, t::DateTime) -> InstrumentState\n\nThe instrument of a restarted emitter: the payload origin, segment geometry, and source of vi — an external series is not read again — with the content clock at the first grid instant at or after t, and never before the content instant of vi, so the replacement cannot repeat a segment of its predecessor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.resumed"},{"category":"function","location":"api/virtualinstrument/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.next_segment!","page":"VirtualInstrument","text":"next_segment!(vi::InstrumentState) -> DataSegment\n\nThe next segment of science data, stamped with its content epoch and its segment_id; advances the content clock by one segment period.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.VirtualInstrument.next_segment!"},{"category":"section","location":"api/sweeps/#Sweeps","page":"Sweeps","text":"","title":"Sweeps"},{"category":"module","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps","page":"Sweeps","text":"Sweeps\n\nEnsembles and sweeps on the tick clock: the same configuration run over a set of seeds, with optional overrides of any key, and the realized totals of every run gathered into one table. run_sweep drives the runs, serially or several at once on Julia threads; summarize_run reads one run's totals. The products land under <data root>/sweeps/<prefix>/.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.FIGURE_FREE_POST_PROCESSING","page":"Sweeps","text":"FIGURE_FREE_POST_PROCESSING\n\nThe post-processing switches a sweep run sets: no mask timeline, no batch-state raster or session figures, no HDF5 export. The publication export is switched off by sweep_configuration, and run_one runs the mission with figures = false, so no figure is drawn. The gap table and the metrology tables, which the summary reads, are written for every run.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.FIGURE_FREE_POST_PROCESSING"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.apply_override!","page":"Sweeps","text":"apply_override!(cfg::AbstractDict, key::AbstractString, value) -> AbstractDict\n\nSets the dotted key (section.key, any depth) of cfg to value, creating the sections on the way. A string value is converted by parse_override: true, 3, 0.25, or a quoted or bare string.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.apply_override!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.parse_override","page":"Sweeps","text":"parse_override(text::AbstractString)\n\nThe value of an override given as text: a boolean, an integer, a float, or the string itself (quotes stripped).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.parse_override"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.sweep_configuration","page":"Sweeps","text":"sweep_configuration(cfg::AbstractDict, seed::Integer; overrides = String[]) -> Dict{String,Any}\n\nThe configuration of one sweep run: cfg on the tick clock (TelemetryCore.tick_configuration), every \"section.key=value\" override applied, simulation.rng_seed = seed, the switches of FIGURE_FREE_POST_PROCESSING, and the publication export off. The figures themselves are skipped by run_one.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.sweep_configuration"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.split_override","page":"Sweeps","text":"split_override(item::AbstractString) -> Tuple{String,String}\n\n\"section.key=value\" into its key and its value text.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.split_override"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.summarize_run","page":"Sweeps","text":"summarize_run(run_dir::String) -> NamedTuple\n\nThe realized totals of one run: the counters of the last row of mission_profile.csv, the generated count and the share within the delivery requirement from delivery_delay.csv, the alert-latency medians at 24 h and 72 h (realized and counterfactual) from alert_latency.csv, the hole count of masks/gaps.csv, then pending (undelivered batches not yet due, from the Due column of delivery_delay.csv), discarded (Metrology.discarded_batches), within_requirement_resolved (batches within the requirement over generated − pending + discarded), and the Kaplan–Meier medians alert24_realized_km, alert24_fifo_km, alert72_realized_km, alert72_fifo_km from alert_latency.csv. An absent delay, latency, or gap table, a delay table without Due, or a latency table without the Kaplan–Meier columns leaves its fields missing; a 24 h or 72 h row on which no window completed under both doctrines gives NaN complete-case medians. mission_profile.csv, events_tx.csv, and the run's configuration snapshot are required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.summarize_run"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.run_one","page":"Sweeps","text":"run_one(cfg::Dict{String,Any}, run_id::String, data_root::String) -> String\n\nOne sweep run, the body of every member task: the mission headless under data_root, with the console and the global logger silenced; the run's own logs still go to its directory. Returns the run directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.run_one"},{"category":"function","location":"api/sweeps/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.run_sweep","page":"Sweeps","text":"run_sweep(cfg::AbstractDict; prefix::AbstractString, seeds, overrides = String[], workers = 0, data_root = TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT[]) -> NamedTuple\n\nRuns cfg on the tick clock once per seed in seeds, as <prefix>_seed<seed> under <data_root>/runs/, with the overrides applied to every run (sweep_configuration); with workers > 0 at most that many runs proceed at once as tasks on the Julia threads, and a warning says so when the process has fewer threads; otherwise they run one after another. A run shares no mutable state with its siblings, so its products do not depend on workers. Writes <data_root>/sweeps/<prefix>/summary.csv, one row per run (summarize_run, with the seed first), and sweep.toml, the sweep's provenance: the base configuration's hash, the overrides, the seeds, the package version and source identity (TelemetryCore.source_identity), the date. Returns (summary, dir).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Sweeps.run_sweep"},{"category":"section","location":"headless/#Headless-Package-and-Containers","page":"Headless Package and Containers","text":"The simulator without its figures is a package of its own, DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless, in the headless/ directory of the repository. It holds the channel and instrument models, the emitter and the receiver on the tick driver, the post-processing tables, the mission supervisor and sweeps. DeepSpaceTelemetry depends on it, re-exports its modules under the same names, and adds the figures; a mission run through the headless package alone writes every table and no figure. The reader package (reader/) holds the configuration and run-directory layer that both use.","title":"Headless Package and Containers"},{"category":"section","location":"headless/#Licences","page":"Headless Package and Containers","text":"The code of all three packages is MIT-licensed. Their dependencies differ:\n\nReader and headless package. Every registered package of their dependency closure is under a permissive licence: MIT, apart from DataStrings, a dependency of CSV, which is Apache-2.0 with MIT-licensed parts and ships a NOTICE file. headless/test/licences.toml lists them with the licences their licence files state. The tests of the headless package read those files and fail when a package of the closure is missing from the list, states another licence, or is not permissive, so a new dependency cannot pass unread.\nHDF5 export. It is an extension of the headless package, loaded with the HDF5 package. The binaries HDF5 brings (HDF5_jll, the MPI libraries it links, OpenSSL) are under permissive licences, apart from libiconv (LGPL-2.1), which XML2_jll links through the MPI library's hardware locality layer.\nFigures. The main package draws its figures with CairoMakie. Through Makie, its closure holds FFmpeg built with GPL and non-free codecs (FFMPEG_jll with x264_jll, x265_jll and libfdk_aac_jll) and Rmath (Rmath_jll, GPL-2.0), as well as the LGPL stack of Cairo, Pango and GLib.\n\nPkg downloads these binaries to the user's depot; the licence of this code is unaffected. A system image, an application bundle or a container image that ships them inherits their terms, and the non-free FFmpeg build cannot be redistributed. A bundle built from the headless package without the HDF5 extension holds no GPL-licensed or non-free package binary; the Apache-2.0 terms of DataStrings ask that its NOTICE travel with it.\n\nEvery bundle also carries the Julia runtime, whose libraries have licences of their own (THIRDPARTY.md of the Julia distribution): GMP (LGPL-3.0 or GPL-2.0) and MPFR (LGPL-3.0), which every Julia process loads, the GCC runtime libraries (GPL-3.0 with the GCC Runtime Library Exception), libgit2 (GPL-2.0 with a linking exception), the SuiteSparse modules (several GPL-2.0 or LGPL-2.1), which the headless package does not load, and the CA certificates of MozillaCACerts_jll (MPL-2.0). A redistributor meets these terms by passing the licences on and offering the sources, which the non-free FFmpeg build does not allow. A container image adds the licences of its base image, the Debian userland of julia:1.13.","title":"Licences"},{"category":"section","location":"headless/#Installation","page":"Headless Package and Containers","text":"From a clone, the headless runner environment deploy/ uses the two packages of the checkout:\n\njulia deploy/run_mission.jl scenarios/smoke_1d.toml --tick SMOKE\n\nA project that depends on the headless package adds it by URL, together with the reader in one call on every Julia version: its [sources] entry points at the sibling directory ../reader, which Pkg does not follow from a package added by subdir (on Julia 1.13.4 the headless package alone fails to resolve the reader):\n\nusing Pkg\nurl = \"https://github.com/PaulGoG/DeepSpaceTelemetry.jl\"\nPkg.add([Pkg.PackageSpec(; url, subdir = \"reader\"), Pkg.PackageSpec(; url, subdir = \"headless\")])\n\nIn a session, Supervisor.run_mission(cfg; figures = false) runs a mission; Supervisor.figure_backend_loaded() tells whether the figures of the main package are available, and Export.hdf5_available() whether the HDF5 export is. A configuration that asks for the HDF5 export without the HDF5 package loaded is refused before the run starts.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"headless/#Data-root","page":"Headless Package and Containers","text":"Runs are written under <data root>/runs/<RUN_ID>. The data root is the directory named by the environment variable DEEPSPACETELEMETRY_DATA when it is set, else data/ beside the package (data/ of the clone for the main package). A container or a cluster job sets the variable to a mounted volume; Supervisor.run_mission(cfg; data_root) overrides both.\n\nA relative path in a configuration (physics.external_data_path, contacts.schedule_csv) resolves against the package directory, which for the headless package is headless/ and in the image /opt/deepspacetelemetry/headless. A configuration run in a container therefore names its inputs by absolute path, under a mounted volume.","title":"Data root"},{"category":"section","location":"headless/#Container-image","page":"Headless Package and Containers","text":"deploy/Dockerfile builds an image of the headless package from the repository root, with its environment resolved from the Project.toml files and their [compat] bounds and precompiled into the image, run by a non-root user:\n\npodman build -f deploy/Dockerfile -t deepspacetelemetry-headless .\nmkdir -p data\npodman run --rm --userns=keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000 \\\n    -v ./data:/data:Z -v ./scenarios:/scenarios:ro,Z \\\n    deepspacetelemetry-headless /scenarios/smoke_1d.toml --tick SMOKE\n\nThe image runs as the user telemetry (uid 1000), which must be able to write to the volume at /data. Rootless Podman maps the host user to root in the container; --userns=keep-id:uid=1000,gid=1000 maps it to the image's user instead. With Docker, the host directory is made writable by uid 1000 (chown 1000 data); a Kubernetes pod sets runAsUser: 1000 and fsGroup: 1000 in its security context.\n\nThe image's entry point is deploy/run_mission.jl: a configuration path, --tick, and a run ID, as for scripts/run_full_sim.jl. Its data root is the volume mounted at /data. In a Kubernetes cluster a mission is a Job whose pod mounts a persistent volume claim at /data and the scenario at a read-only path, with one run ID per pod (for example the seed of an ensemble member); the run directory carries its own provenance.\n\nA run in the image records the source identity of the headless/ directory as copied into it (identity_source = \"directory\"): its git tree hash equals git rev-parse <commit>:headless of the commit the image was built from, and reader_tree_sha1 equals git rev-parse <commit>:reader, when the image is built from a clean checkout: an untracked file under headless/ or reader/ that .dockerignore does not exclude enters the hash. A run of the headless package in a clone records the commit itself (identity_source = \"work_tree\"), with git_dirty set by any change to a tracked file of the clone. The resolved environment is the run's manifest_snapshot.toml.\n\nThe image compiles its package images for the CPU targets of the official Julia binaries on x86-64 (JULIA_CPU_TARGET in the Dockerfile), so a pod on another x86-64 processor loads them without recompiling; an image for another architecture is built with --build-arg JULIA_CPU_TARGET=....","title":"Container image"},{"category":"section","location":"api/emitter/#Emitter","page":"Emitter","text":"","title":"Emitter"},{"category":"module","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter","page":"Emitter","text":"Emitter\n\nThe satellite-side loop: payload generation and batching, onboard queue management (live FIFO with absolute priority, archive LIFO backfill), link-gated transmission under the in-flight cap, and ground-truth gen/tx event logging. Re-entrant: a restarted emitter reconstructs its queues and counter from the run directory and event log.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.instrument_epoch","page":"Emitter","text":"instrument_epoch(start_sim_time::DateTime, initial_downtime_days::Real) -> DateTime\n\nAnchor of the instrument that pre-populates the onboard buffer: the mission epoch start_sim_time less the initial downtime, clamped at the epoch for a non-positive downtime.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.instrument_epoch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.pre_populate","page":"Emitter","text":"pre_populate(instrument, start_sim_time, run_dir; batch_size, kwargs...) -> (instrument, pending_segments)\n\nFills the on-board recorder (<run_dir>/onboard/) with the ARCH_ batches produced during the initial downtime, the backlog the active mission window starts with. run_dir is the absolute path of the run directory (TelemetryCore.run_directory).\n\ninstrument is the VirtualInstrument.InstrumentState anchored at the start of the blind spot (instrument_epoch); the pre-population runs from that anchor to start_sim_time, and an instrument anchored at or after start_sim_time returns at once with no batch written.\n\nReturns the instrument together with any trailing segments that did not fill a complete batch. Both must be handed to run_emitter, which completes the partial batch in the mission phase.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbatch_size: segments per batch (required).\nmarkers: event markers, stamped into the batch holding their instant.\ngeneration_gaps: scheduled (start, stop) intervals without data production (skip_generation_gaps!).\nonboard_capacity_batches: recorder ceiling; data beyond it is discarded.\nhole_feed: the declared-hole feed the emitter appends its scheduled and recorder gaps to, nothing for none. A scheduled gap inside the blind spot is declared at start_sim_time; a recorder gap opened here is declared by run_emitter when it closes.\nprogress: draw the progress bar on a terminal stderr; off for runs without a console, such as the members of a sweep.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.pre_populate"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.skip_generation_gaps!","page":"Emitter","text":"skip_generation_gaps!(vi, pending, gaps, run_dir; hole_feed = nothing, declared_at = nothing) -> Bool\n\nScheduled generation gap: when the instrument's next content instant lies inside one of gaps ((start, stop) intervals), the segments of the incomplete batch are discarded, which keeps every batch at batch_size segments, and the content clock resumes at the first grid instant at or after the gap's end (VirtualInstrument.advance_to!). The gap is bounded in events_tx.csv (Batch = SCHEDULED) by gap_start at the first discarded epoch (or the content end when nothing was pending) and gap_end at the resumption instant, so [gap_start, gap_end) is exactly the content not recorded. With a hole_feed, the same interval is appended to the declared-hole feed as a scheduled hole declared at declared_at (the resumption instant when nothing). Returns true when a gap was skipped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.skip_generation_gaps!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.stamp_markers!","page":"Emitter","text":"stamp_markers!(batch_dir, batch, batch_name, run_dir, markers, content_end; payload_row)\n\nSaves batch (TelemetryCore.save_batch) with its payload_row and the labels of the event markers whose instant lies in its content span, and appends one marker row per hit to events_tx.csv (SimTime = the marker instant, Batch = the containing batch) so live consumers learn which batch holds the event the moment it becomes transmittable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.stamp_markers!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.finalize_batch!","page":"Emitter","text":"finalize_batch!(run_dir, id, pending, finalized_at, is_live, markers, vi;\n                onboard_count = nothing) -> String\n\nWrites the segments of pending as batch id (LIVE_ when is_live, else ARCH_) into <run_dir>/onboard/ with its markers (stamp_markers!), counts it in onboard_count, appends its gen row at finalized_at, the finalization instant on the mission timeline, and empties pending. Returns the batch name.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.finalize_batch!"},{"category":"type","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.RecorderGap","page":"Emitter","text":"RecorderGap\n\nThe open recorder-overflow gap: start, the content instant of the first discarded segment, or nothing while the recorder has room.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.RecorderGap"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.discard_at_ceiling!","page":"Emitter","text":"discard_at_ceiling!(gap::RecorderGap, run_dir, pending, held::Int)\n\nDiscards the full batch in pending at the recorder ceiling of held batches. No batch is evicted: new data is lost until a batch leaves for the link. The first discard opens the gap with a RECORDER gap_start row in events_tx.csv.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.discard_at_ceiling!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.close_recorder_gap!","page":"Emitter","text":"close_recorder_gap!(gap::RecorderGap, run_dir, resume::DateTime, declared_at::DateTime;\n                    hole_feed = nothing)\n\nCloses an open recorder gap at resume, the first content instant recorded again: a RECORDER gap_end row, and with a hole_feed a recorder hole declared at declared_at. Does nothing while no gap is open.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.close_recorder_gap!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.transmit_onboard!","page":"Emitter","text":"transmit_onboard!(live_queue, arch_queue, link, sim_t, run_dir; max_inflight_batches,\n                  onboard_count = nothing) -> Int\n\nPlaces on-board batches on the downlink while link is transmittable at sim_t (visibility and no blackout), until link/ holds max_inflight_batches: first from live_queue (FIFO, absolute priority), then from arch_queue (held newest first, so the archive drains LIFO). Each move from onboard/ to link/ is appended to events_tx.csv as a tx row and taken off onboard_count. Returns the number of batches placed.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.transmit_onboard!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/emitter/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.run_emitter","page":"Emitter","text":"run_emitter(clock, link, run_dir, instrument; batch_size, kwargs...)\n\nThe spacecraft loop: segments from instrument (synthetic, or read from an external CSV) are batched and placed on the downlink, live FIFO with absolute priority over the archive LIFO (transmit_onboard!). link is the composite ChannelEffects.LinkModel (visibility × disruption timeline): a batch finalized while the link is transmittable is stamped LIVE_, any other ARCH_, so the batches generated during a disruption blackout accumulate onboard. run_dir is the absolute path of the run directory.\n\ninstrument is the VirtualInstrument.InstrumentState the stream continues from: on the first start the one returned by pre_populate; on a restart the instrument VirtualInstrument.resumed at the first segment boundary at or after the current mission time, with the payload origin of the first start, which leaves a genuine generation gap.\n\nclock is the TelemetryCore.TickClock of the mission. A segment is produced once the clock has passed the end of its content interval (vi.last_t + segment_duration_sec). While no segment is due the loop serves the downlink and parks the task (TelemetryCore.wait_until!) until that instant or the next boundary of the payload grid, whichever comes first, so a scheduled generation gap interrupts production but not transmission. The loop ends at the horizon, on the stop flag, or on the HALT sentinel (TelemetryCore.loop_should_stop).\n\nKeyword arguments\n\nbatch_size: segments per batch (required).\npending_segments: the partial batch returned by pre_populate.\nstop: cooperative stop flag raised by the supervisor.\nmax_inflight_batches: cap on batches simultaneously on the link.\nmarkers: event markers, stamped into the batch holding their instant. The synthetic payload flags them through the instrument (VirtualInstrument.FlaggedSignal), not through this argument.\ngeneration_gaps: scheduled (start, stop) intervals without data production (skip_generation_gaps!).\nonboard_capacity_batches: recorder ceiling; new data is discarded while the buffer holds that many batches.\nhole_feed: the declared-hole feed for the scheduled and recorder gaps, nothing for none. A scheduled gap is declared at the mission instant it is skipped at, a recorder gap at the finalization that closes it.\nonboard_count: the number of batches on board, set from the census at start and kept in step with every write to and move out of onboard/; the receiver reads it for the metrics instead of listing the directory.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Emitter.run_emitter"},{"category":"section","location":"#DeepSpaceTelemetry","page":"Home","text":"A Julia framework that simulates the telemetry environment of deep-space science missions: contact windows, link physics, queuing, loss, and disruption. The shipped scenarios, a library under scenarios/, model the LISA (Laser Interferometer Space Antenna) mission. Missions run on a wall-paced clock for rehearsals with live dashboards, or unpaced on a logical clock for analysis; both produce the same record, to the byte, for a given seed.","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry"},{"category":"section","location":"#Overview","page":"Home","text":"Deep-space missions communicate on asymmetric duty cycles: a daily ground-station contact window followed by a long blind spot in which science data accumulates onboard. The shipped configuration models LISA, which orbits the Sun 50 million kilometers behind Earth, with an 8-hour DSN window against a 16-hour blind spot (12 h passes near the seasonal peak in the default configuration). The telemetry, channel, and queuing layers remain mission-agnostic. \"DSN\" denotes throughout a deep-space ground-station network in the generic sense; the LISA passes are ESA ESTRACK 35 m antenna passes.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the stress scenario)\n\nOne week of scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml: 8 h daily passes on the physical link, a bursty Gilbert–Elliott channel, an 18 h blackout after a solar particle event with a 12 h recovery ramp on day 2.5, a partial ground-station outage on day 5, and a scheduled generation gap on day 1.5. The onboard buffer grows from 288 to 533 batches over the week, and retransmission recovers every rejected transfer. The Scenario Gallery shows the other products of this run, nine further scenarios, and the totals of all ten.","title":"Overview"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"Requires Julia ≥ 1.12. The package is not registered in the General registry and is used from a clone of the repository, which carries the scripts, the scenario library, and the benchmarks:\n\ngit clone https://github.com/PaulGoG/DeepSpaceTelemetry.jl.git\ncd DeepSpaceTelemetry.jl\n\nA first run on the default configuration takes about three minutes:\n\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl\n\nRuns are written under data/runs/ of the clone; data/ is the data root. The environment variable DEEPSPACETELEMETRY_DATA names another data root when it is set as the package loads. A copy installed by Pkg writes under its own directory unless that variable is set, the caller passes data_root to Supervisor.run_mission, or the default data root, TelemetryCore.DATA_ROOT[], is redirected.\n\nA project that depends on the package adds it by URL. Julia 1.13 resolves the reader and the headless package through the package's own [sources] entries; the Pkg of Julia 1.12 does not follow the [sources] of a dependency, so there the three packages are added in one call:\n\nusing Pkg\nurl = \"https://github.com/PaulGoG/DeepSpaceTelemetry.jl\"\nPkg.add([\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(; url, subdir = \"reader\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(; url, subdir = \"headless\"),\n    Pkg.PackageSpec(; url),\n])\n\nThe simulator without its figures is the package DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless, whose dependency closure carries permissive licences only; Headless Package and Containers describes it and its container image.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Capabilities","page":"Home","text":"Payload: a binary flag series marking the segments that hold a declared event (0 noise only, 1 flagged signal), or ingestion of an external continuous CSV time series, one or several of its columns as one payload.\nLink capacity: either the batches-per-hour abstraction or the physical rate pair downlink_kbps / onboard_data_rate_kbps, with the catch-up ratio and the capacity of one nominal pass against the daily production reported at start-up.\nPass profiles: sine, sigmoid, gaussian, or flat capacity over the pass; the validator states the profile mean when a shaped profile meets a physical rate.\nContact schedule: seasonal pass-duration modulation, per-date exceptions, explicit pass lists (TOML or CSV), and low-latency periods at a station-availability capacity fraction.\nFIFO/LIFO routing: live transmission first (FIFO), then the archive backfill in strict LIFO order, so alert pipelines extend a live event's waveform backward in time without gaps.\nStochastic packet loss: per-transfer loss from a memoryless Bernoulli or a bursty Gilbert–Elliott channel, a retransmit/drop policy, and retransmissions deferred by the round-trip light time of the configured range; retry-exhausted batches land in lost/ (data preserved, mask state 4).\nDisruption events: scheduled link disruptions (full or partial blackout, linear recovery ramp, elevated loss) and scheduled generation gaps such as antenna repointing; the on-board recorder ceiling discards data at capacity and records the loss as a gap.\nEvent markers: declared instants stamped into the batch metadata and the transmit log; the alert-latency metric is evaluated at each marker, with an optional triggered low-latency period.\nTwo clocks: one driver advances mission time from one component wakeup to the next on one thread, with liveness files and the [supervision] failure policy (abort, continue or restart). Wall mode paces it against the wall clock at speed_up and serves the live dashboards; tick mode (simulation.mode = \"tick\", or --tick on any scenario) runs it unpaced. Both give the same record for the same configuration (Mission Clocks: Wall and Tick).\nPost-processing products: the exact batch-state history replayed from the event logs (telemetry_mask_timeline.csv), the delivered payload rows at any instant (RunAdapter.delivered_rows), the gap table of every hole in the delivered record with its cause (masks/gaps.csv), metrology tables, mission and session figures, an HDF5 export of every product with provenance attributes, and a publication export at a declared printed width with a provenance sidecar.\nScenario library: twelve complete configurations under scenarios/, from a half-day smoke run to a 30-day seasonal mission, among them one mission day on the tick clock, each validated by the test suite.\nValidated configuration: every tunable has a documented safe interval. At start-up validate_config rejects code-breaking values and retired keys with a precise [CONFIG] message and warns on suspicious ones. simulation.rng_seed seeds the packet-loss channel, the only random stream.","title":"Capabilities"},{"category":"section","location":"api/publication/#Publication","page":"Publication","text":"","title":"Publication"},{"category":"module","location":"api/publication/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication","page":"Publication","text":"Publication\n\nPublication figure export: every figure of a run re-rendered at a declared printed width in a vector format, into one directory with a provenance sidecar, so a manuscript's figure directory is populated from the run that produced the numbers (export_publication_figures). The figures are drawn by the post-processing's own functions; the export changes only the print scale (PlotTheme.style_for_width), the format, the destination, and the file-name suffix carrying the run ID.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication"},{"category":"function","location":"api/publication/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication.export_publication_figures","page":"Publication","text":"export_publication_figures(run_dir::String; format = \"pdf\", column_width_mm = 178.0, export_dir = \"\") -> Vector{String}\n\nRenders the mission summary, the session figures (as the run's configuration switches and caps them), and the figure products flagged in post_processing.figures (the metrology figures, the batch-state raster) at column_width_mm, the standard layout scaled as a whole to this printed width, in format (\"pdf\" or \"svg\") into export_dir (default <run_dir>/publication). Each file is named <stem>__<run_id>.<format>, and PROVENANCE.toml is written beside them (write_provenance). The metrology tables of the run are not rewritten. Returns the figure paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication.export_publication_figures"},{"category":"function","location":"api/publication/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication.write_provenance","page":"Publication","text":"write_provenance(dir, run_dir, cfg, paths, column_width_mm, format) -> String\n\nWrites <dir>/PROVENANCE.toml (an existing file is rotated): the run ID and directory, the export instant, width and format, the package version and the source identity recorded in the run snapshot (git_commit, git_tree_sha1, git_dirty), the SHA-256 of the snapshot, and the exported file names.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.Publication.write_provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"api/plottheme/#PlotTheme","page":"PlotTheme","text":"","title":"PlotTheme"},{"category":"module","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme","page":"PlotTheme","text":"PlotTheme\n\nPlotting standards shared by every figure: the Okabe–Ito semantic palette, the standard figure layout with its uniform print scaling (PlotStyle), the measured legend and figure sizing (figure_legend!, size_to_panels!), and the CairoMakie theme (telemetry_theme). Lengths are Makie units (1/96 inch) at the standard layout, which PlotStyle scales.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme"},{"category":"type","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.PlotStyle","page":"PlotTheme","text":"PlotStyle\n\nLengths of one figure set, all in Makie units: scale relative to the standard layout, the figure width, the axis heights panel_height and strip_height, the font sizes (fontsize for axis labels and legends, fontsize_tick, fontsize_annotation), the line widths (linewidth for data, linewidth_guide for reference lines and window edges, linewidth_edge for the edge of an area fill), and markersize. PlotStyle(scale) multiplies the standard layout by scale throughout; PlotStyle() is the standard layout itself.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.PlotStyle"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.style_for_width","page":"PlotTheme","text":"style_for_width(column_width_mm::Real) -> PlotStyle\n\nThe PlotStyle of a figure printed column_width_mm wide (Makie units are 1/96 inch). At 178 mm the axis labels print at ≈ 11 pt and the tick labels at ≈ 9 pt; at the 100 mm floor of the publication export, at ≈ 6 pt and ≈ 5 pt.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.style_for_width"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.scaled","page":"PlotTheme","text":"scaled(style::PlotStyle, length::Real) -> Float64\n\nlength, given in Makie units at the standard layout, at the scale of style, for lengths a figure sets by hand: a reserved tick-label width, a gap, an offset.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.scaled"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_fraction","page":"PlotTheme","text":"annotation_fraction(style::PlotStyle, text) -> Float64\n\nWidth of the single-line annotation text as a fraction of the axis it sits in, at half an em per character (the mean advance of the text face) against AXIS_WIDTH_SHARE of the figure width, capped at 0.45 — beyond that neither end of the axis is free. Independent of the scale of style. Used by annotation_side and annotation_anchor.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_fraction"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_side","page":"PlotTheme","text":"annotation_side(occupied, x_lo, x_hi, fraction) -> Symbol\n\nEnd of the axis for an in-axis annotation block of relative width fraction (annotation_fraction): :left when one of the occupied x positions (upright rules, shaded edges) lies within fraction of the right edge and none within fraction of the left edge, else :right.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_side"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.ANNOTATION_EDGE_INSET","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Relative distance between an in-axis annotation block and the axis frame at the end it is aligned to (annotation_anchor).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.ANNOTATION_EDGE_INSET"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_anchor","page":"PlotTheme","text":"annotation_anchor(occupied, x_lo, x_hi, fraction) -> Tuple{Float64,Symbol}\n\nRelative x position and horizontal alignment of an in-axis annotation block of relative width fraction (annotation_fraction) on an axis spanning [x_lo, x_hi], clear of the occupied x positions (upright rules and shaded edges; those outside the axis are ignored). The block with its two insets (ANNOTATION_EDGE_INSET) takes the right end, else the left end, else the middle of the widest interval between consecutive positions; it stays at the right end when no interval holds it.\n\njulia> DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_anchor([0.5, 9.5], 0.0, 10.0, 0.2)\n(0.5, :center)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.annotation_anchor"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.POWER_OF_TEN_THRESHOLD","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Magnitude from which the tick labels of a count axis are written with a power of ten (power_of_ten_labels, count_tickformat).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.POWER_OF_TEN_THRESHOLD"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.power_of_ten_labels","page":"PlotTheme","text":"power_of_ten_labels(values) -> Vector{String}\n\nTick labels with one common power of ten, that of the largest magnitude among values: 1×10ⁿ is written 10ⁿ, zero is 0, a mantissa carries at most three significant digits without trailing zeros, and a negative value takes the typographic minus.\n\njulia> DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.power_of_ten_labels([0, 10_000, 20_000, 30_000])\n4-element Vector{String}:\n \"0\"\n \"10⁴\"\n \"2×10⁴\"\n \"3×10⁴\"\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.power_of_ten_labels"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.power_of_ten_label","page":"PlotTheme","text":"power_of_ten_label(mantissa::Real, exponent::Integer) -> String\n\nOne label of power_of_ten_labels: mantissa × 10^exponent in the house style.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.power_of_ten_label"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.count_tickformat","page":"PlotTheme","text":"count_tickformat(top::Real)\n\nTick format of a count axis that reaches top: power_of_ten_labels from POWER_OF_TEN_THRESHOLD on, Makie's own format below it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.count_tickformat"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.TICK_PRUNE_FRACTION","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Fraction of the axis range below the upper limit within which UpperPrunedTicks discards a tick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.TICK_PRUNE_FRACTION"},{"category":"type","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.UpperPrunedTicks","page":"PlotTheme","text":"UpperPrunedTicks()\n\nTick locator of the lower panel of a stacked pair: the ticks of the default locator on the axis range, without those within TICK_PRUNE_FRACTION of the upper limit. A tick label at the upper limit of the lower panel meets the 0 of the panel above across the row gap; pruning it is the stacked-axes rule (matplotlib's prune = \"upper\"). Passed as yticks = UpperPrunedTicks().\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.UpperPrunedTicks"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.line_advance","page":"PlotTheme","text":"line_advance(style::PlotStyle) -> Float64\n\nVertical advance between two lines of in-axis annotation text in Makie units: the line height of the theme's text face (height / units_per_EM, the multiplier Makie applies to multi-line text) times the annotation font size. Places a second text primitive directly under a first, e.g. a plain block below a LaTeX headline.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.line_advance"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.fit_legend!","page":"PlotTheme","text":"fit_legend!(legend, available_width::Real) -> Int\n\nSets legend.nbanks to the smallest row count at which the legend, as Makie itself measures it, is no wider than available_width, and returns that count. A legend whose longest group cannot fit keeps one entry per row.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.fit_legend!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.figure_legend!","page":"PlotTheme","text":"figure_legend!(fig, style::PlotStyle, elements, labels) -> Legend\nfigure_legend!(fig, style::PlotStyle, groups) -> Legend\n\nThe frameless horizontal legend strip above the panels of fig, in as many rows as the figure width requires (fit_legend!). groups is a vector of (title, elements, labels) tuples, one per series family, drawn side by side under bold headers; groups without entries are dropped.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.figure_legend!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.size_to_panels!","page":"PlotTheme","text":"size_to_panels!(fig, rows::Pair{Int,<:Real}...) -> fig\n\nFixes the axis height of each listed layout row of fig (row => height in Makie units, e.g. 1 => style.panel_height) and resizes the figure to the height its layout then measures — legend, panels, decorations, gaps, and padding — at the width it already has. Called last, once the legend and every axis are in place.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.size_to_panels!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.save_figure","page":"PlotTheme","text":"save_figure(fig, dir::String, stem::String; formats = (\"png\", \"pdf\"), suffix = \"\") -> String\n\nSaves fig as <dir>/<stem><suffix>.<ext> for every extension in formats — PNG at px_per_unit = 4 (≈ 380 dpi at native size), vector formats at native size — and returns the path of the first one. The figure is emptied after the last save: Makie keeps the color buffer of a saved figure, about 70 MiB for a standard PNG, reachable through its scene, so a caller that still needs the figure draws it again.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.save_figure"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.figure_scope","page":"PlotTheme","text":"figure_scope(f, style::PlotStyle = PlotStyle())\n\nRuns f(), the drawing and saving of one figure, inside a telemetry theme scope of its own (telemetry_theme), runs the garbage collector, and returns the value of f. Makie keeps every figure drawn in a theme scope reachable until the scope ends, so a loop of figures takes one scope per figure; save_figure releases the buffers. Must not be nested in another theme scope, which would hold the figures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.figure_scope"},{"category":"function","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.telemetry_theme","page":"PlotTheme","text":"telemetry_theme(style::PlotStyle = PlotStyle())\n\nThe CairoMakie theme of every figure at the scale of style: boxed axes with inward ticks, no titles or minor ticks, a faint dashed grid, guide-weight rules, stroked markers, and frameless legends with top-aligned bold group headers. Faces are (New) Computer Modern through MathTeXEngine, since Makie ignores a plain font = \"Computer Modern\" and falls back to DejaVu. Tick labels are rotated per axis (session HH:MM).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.telemetry_theme"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_LIVE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Near-real-time (live, FIFO-priority) batches: Okabe–Ito sky blue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_LIVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_ARCHIVE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Blind-spot archive (LIFO-backfilled) batches: Okabe–Ito bluish green.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_ARCHIVE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_BANDWIDTH","page":"PlotTheme","text":"DSN link-capacity (bandwidth) curve: Okabe–Ito blue.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_BANDWIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_ONBOARD","page":"PlotTheme","text":"On-board recorder backlog curve, dashed so it survives grayscale: Okabe–Ito orange.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_ONBOARD"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_LOST","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Permanently lost (retry-exhausted) batches, drawn as ✕: Okabe–Ito vermillion.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_LOST"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_COUNTERFACTUAL","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Counterfactual FIFO drain of the alert-latency figure, dotted: Okabe–Ito reddish purple.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_COUNTERFACTUAL"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_DISRUPTION","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Neutral base of the disruption shading (blackout wash fading to zero alpha over the recovery ramp, dashed edges), kept apart from the series palette.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_DISRUPTION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_OUTAGE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Neutral base of the shading of unscheduled component outages (low-alpha wash, dotted edges), apart from the series palette and, by line style, from the disruption shading.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_OUTAGE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_MARKER","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Event markers: their upright rules in the mission figures and their realized latency curves in the alert-latency figure, apart from the population bands and told apart by cycling line styles.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_MARKER"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_GUIDE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Neutral gray of population aggregates and reference guides: the all-batches delivery curve and the delivery-requirement rule with its label.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_GUIDE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_FUTURE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Not-yet-generated cells of the batch-state raster: a near-white that reads as absence, so the generation front is the edge of the drawn area.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.COLOR_FUTURE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_WIDTH","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Width of every figure, shared by the stacked time-series dashboards of a mission so that sibling figures print with identical type.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_WIDTH"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.PANEL_HEIGHT","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Axis height of a main panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.PANEL_HEIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.STRIP_HEIGHT","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Axis height of an auxiliary strip (the Lost strip of the mission summary).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.STRIP_HEIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_PADDING","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Outer padding of every figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_PADDING"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_PADDING_RIGHT","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Right-hand padding of every figure: the outer padding plus half a tick label, which an x tick on the axis frame pushes past it.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FIGURE_PADDING_RIGHT"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.AXIS_WIDTH_SHARE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Estimated share of the figure width left to a stacked panel's axis by the y-axis decorations of both sides and the padding; used only by annotation_fraction.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.AXIS_WIDTH_SHARE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Font size of axis labels, legends, and legend headers.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE_TICK","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Tick-label font size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE_TICK"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE_ANNOTATION","page":"PlotTheme","text":"In-axis annotation font size.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FONTSIZE_ANNOTATION"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_DATA","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Data-series line width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_DATA"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_GUIDE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Width of guide lines: thresholds, requirement and event-marker rules, window edges.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_GUIDE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_EDGE","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Width of the same-hue edge of an area fill.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.LINEWIDTH_EDGE"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.MARKERSIZE_DATA","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Marker size of event pins and legend glyphs, the same in every figure.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.MARKERSIZE_DATA"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/plottheme/#DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FILL_ALPHA","page":"PlotTheme","text":"Alpha of the fills under cumulative curves, edged in full hue (LINEWIDTH_EDGE).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetry.PlotTheme.FILL_ALPHA"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Scenario-Gallery","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"Ten of the twelve shipped scenarios, each run once. The figures are those the run wrote to its plots/ directory, reduced to the page width and otherwise untouched. The smoke scenario and the deterministic one-day scenario exist for the test suite and are left out.\n\nAny scenario runs by path from the package root, on the wall clock or, with --tick, on the tick clock:\n\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl <RUN_ID> scenarios/<file>.toml\njulia scripts/run_full_sim.jl --tick <RUN_ID> scenarios/<file>.toml\n\nOne command regenerates the figures and the totals of this page. It runs the ten scenarios one after another on the tick clock, reduces the figures, and records every run and every file in docs/src/assets/PROVENANCE.toml:\n\njulia docs/render_assets.jl\n\nThe totals below reproduce from the scenario, the seed, the commit, the platform, and the Julia version recorded with each run; the record also identifies the resolved dependencies by the digest of the run's manifest. The regime each scenario is built to reach follows from its link budget and is derived in the coverage matrix of scenarios/README.md.","title":"Scenario Gallery"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Realized-Totals","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"Scenario Days Batches On the ground Live Archive Lost Failed transfers Buffer, first → last Within 24 h\nnominal_8h 7 1007 923 336 587 0 8 0 → 84 91.5 %\nstress_8h_bursty 7 1294 756 329 427 0 49 288 → 533 57.9 %\nrecovery_12h_seasonal 7 1294 1137 483 654 0 66 283 → 153 67.5 %\nabstraction_gaussian_peak 7 1294 960 321 639 0 57 288 → 329 64.4 %\nbacklog_recovery_sine 7 1439 1350 331 1019 0 142 432 → 84 71.1 %\ndrop_policy 3 503 336 117 219 83 83 72 → 84 66.8 %\nexplicit_schedule 7 1150 860 299 561 0 60 144 → 286 70.2 %\nlong_30d_seasonal 30 4196 2196 805 1391 1 129 432 → 1994 45.9 %\nlong_segments_2400s 7 95 85 28 57 0 0 12 → 7 82.1 %\nexternal_ingest 2 359 275 96 179 0 1 72 → 84 76.6 %\n\nRuns of 2026-11-16 at commit e98be87, Julia 1.13.4.\n\n\n\nBatches counts every batch of the run, the pre-populated backlog included. The buffer column gives the onboard buffer at the first and at the last row of the metrics profile, and the last column the share of all batches on the ground within the 24-hour delivery requirement.","title":"Realized Totals"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#The-Mission-Summary","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"Each scenario opens with its mission summary. The top panel carries the link capacity in percent of its peak and, on the right-hand axis, the onboard buffer; where a disruption lowers the link, the nominal capacity remains as a dotted curve behind the effective one. The middle panel accumulates the batches on the ground, the archive share in green under the total. The bottom strip accumulates the lost batches and is present whenever the loss channel is enabled. Shaded bands mark events on all three panels (a blackout with its fading recovery ramp, a generation gap, an interval with the recorder full, a low-latency period), and a solid upright rule marks a declared event marker.","title":"The Mission Summary"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Nominal-Operations","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/nominal_8h.toml: seven days of 8-hour passes on the physical link, 230 kbit/s down against 75 kbit/s produced, flat within the pass; no backlog, no disruption, 1 % Bernoulli loss.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the nominal scenario: seven rectangular passes, the onboard buffer returning to zero at the end of each)\n\nOne pass carries about 146 batches after the 1 % loss, against 144 produced per day: each pass ends with the buffer empty, and each blind spot refills it to 96 batches, sixteen hours of production. Within a pass the live batches cross as they are generated and the remaining capacity drains the archive; on the ground the archive grows at about twice the live rate.","title":"Nominal Operations"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Growth-under-Disruptions","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml: the same link and passes with a two-day backlog, a bursty Gilbert–Elliott channel (stationary loss 6.25 %), three scheduled events, and one event marker.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the stress scenario: seven daily passes against a growing onboard buffer, a blackout after a solar particle event with its recovery ramp, a partial ground-station outage, a generation gap, and an event marker with its low-latency period)\n\nAfter retransmissions the link delivers fewer batches per pass than a day produces, so the backlog cannot shrink, and each disruption adds to it. The 18-hour blackout after the solar particle event on day 2.5 removes one pass except for what the first hours of its 12-hour recovery ramp let through, and the partial ground-station outage on day 5 holds a pass at a fifth of its capacity. The 15-minute antenna repointing on day 1.5 is a generation gap. The marker on day 4 opens, six hours later, a three-hour low-latency period at half capacity. Retransmission recovers every rejected transfer, and the lost strip stays at zero. The buffer ends the week near twice its initial level.\n\n(Image: Batch-routing animation of the stress scenario: one row per stage, sky blue for live batches and green for archive ones, with the mission clock and the buffer counters in every frame)\n\nThe same run, batch by batch (GIF Animation), read frame by frame under Queuing Theory: LIFO vs FIFO.\n\n(Image: Batch-state raster of the stress scenario: one column per batch, mission time upward, each pass turning a green archive block and a sky-blue live block to the ground colors)\n\nThe batch-state raster (Batch-State Raster) shows the routing doctrine in one panel, with one column per batch, mission time upward, and one color per state. The diagonal is generation. Each pass turns two blocks of columns to the ground colors: the sky-blue block of the batches generated during the pass, and to its left the green block of archive batches, whose lower edge slopes because the newest archive batch goes first. The columns still in the onboard color at the top are the backlog the week never cleared; the blackout and the outage appear as passes whose blocks stay narrow.\n\n(Image: Delivery-delay distribution of the stress scenario: cumulative fraction of generated batches against the measurement-to-ground delay, for all batches, the live family, and the archive family, with the 24-hour requirement)\n\nThe delivery delay is the time from the end of a batch's measurement to its arrival on the ground. Every live batch arrives within the requirement. Under the LIFO backfill an archive batch either arrives within about a day or stays behind for the rest of the week, so the archive curve and the curve of all batches level off at the requirement; the level of the latter is the compliance fraction of the run.\n\n(Image: Alert-latency curves of the stress scenario: time until a look-back window before a live event is complete on the ground, under the realized doctrine and under a counterfactual first-in, first-out drain)\n\nThe alert latency (Alert-Latency Metrology) is the time after a live event until the data of the preceding hours are complete on the ground. Under the realized routing the Kaplan–Meier median stays below an hour for look-backs up to six hours, reaches about 3 h at twelve hours and about two days at seventeen; beyond that more than half of the windows are still incomplete at the end of the week, and the median is not reached within the run. A first-in, first-out drain of the same service completions would need about 71 h at every look-back. The complete-case medians, over the windows delivered in full under both doctrines, put the realized latency at about 2 h for 18 h of look-back: they leave out the windows still open at the end of the run (Alert-Latency Metrology).\n\n(Image: Session figure of the low-latency period of the stress scenario: capacity at 50 %, the received batches as a staircase)\n\nEvery contact window receives a session figure with the mission clock on the time axis and the counts starting at zero. This one is the low-latency period the marker triggered: three hours at half capacity, about nine batches per hour.","title":"Growth under Disruptions"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Recovery-at-the-Seasonal-Peak","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/recovery_12h_seasonal.toml, the reference scenario and the content of config.toml: the event timeline of the stress scenario from 30 March, the peak day of the seasonal term, when the extension widens the passes to 12 hours; evaluated on the date of each pass, it stays within minutes of its maximum over the week.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the recovery scenario: 12-hour passes, the onboard buffer falling from 283 to 15 batches at day 2.5, then between about 75 and 220)\n\nA 12-hour pass carries about 210 batches against 144 produced per day, so an undisturbed day lowers the buffer by some 65 batches. The blackout and the outage each cost about one pass, and the week ends with the backlog below its initial level where the 8-hour passes of the stress scenario nearly double it.\n\n(Image: Delivery-delay distribution of the recovery scenario: the archive curve keeps rising beyond the 24-hour requirement)\n\nWith capacity to spare, the backfill reaches past the newest day of archive: the archive curve keeps rising beyond the requirement, in stages that are the successive passes working backward through the initial backlog.","title":"Recovery at the Seasonal Peak"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Shaped-Pass-Profiles","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/abstraction_gaussian_peak.toml and scenarios/backlog_recovery_sine.toml replace the physical rate pair by a peak capacity of 60 batches per hour under a profile of the window progress: Gaussian with σ = 0.15 (mean factor 0.38) and sine (mean factor 0.5).\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the Gaussian-profile scenario: bell-shaped passes under the event timeline of the stress scenario)\n\nThe Gaussian scenario carries the event timeline of the stress scenario. Capacity concentrates around the culmination, so the received count rises as an S-curve within each pass. The 18-hour blackout ends at one culmination and leaves that pass a few percent of its capacity.\n\n(Image: Session figure of the first pass of the Gaussian-profile scenario: the bell-shaped capacity and the S-shaped received count)\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the sine-profile scenario: sine-profile passes draining a three-day backlog)\n\nThe sine scenario starts from a three-day backlog under 10 % Bernoulli loss and no disruption. Each pass delivers more than a day produces, the buffer falls from pass to pass, and from the fifth pass on it reaches zero before the pass ends.","title":"Shaped Pass Profiles"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Drop-Policy","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/drop_policy.toml: three days on the physical link with 20 % Bernoulli loss and on_loss = \"drop\", so a rejected transfer is not repeated.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the drop-policy scenario: the lost strip rising during every pass)\n\nAbout one transfer in five is lost for good, and the lost strip rises through every pass. With no retransmission traffic the passes clear the buffer. The partial outage on day 1.5 falls into a blind spot and costs nothing.\n\n(Image: Batch-state raster of the drop-policy scenario: lost batches as vermilion columns inside the delivered blocks)\n\nIn the raster the lost batches are the vermilion columns scattered through the delivered blocks: mask state 4, rows that RunAdapter.delivered_rows leaves out.","title":"Drop Policy"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Explicit-Pass-Schedule","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/explicit_schedule.toml: the passes are listed one by one instead of being generated, with the pass of day 2 shortened to 6 hours, the pass of day 3 missed, and the pass of day 5 extended to 12 hours.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the explicit-schedule scenario: a shortened pass, a missing pass, and an extended pass)\n\nThe missed pass leaves a blind spot of 41 hours, across which the buffer grows by more than a day and a half of production; the extended pass two days later recovers part of it.","title":"Explicit Pass Schedule"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Thirty-Days-with-a-Contact-Gap","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/long_30d_seasonal.toml: thirty days from 20 July, on the declining side of the seasonal term (the passes shorten from about 9.3 h to about 8.5 h over the month, following the date of each pass), two missed passes and a shortened one, a 12-day interval without contact, five disruptions, and two event markers.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the 30-day scenario: the onboard buffer rising through a 12-day contact gap to the recorder capacity)\n\nA 36-hour safe mode on day 8 is a generation gap that coincides with two missed passes: the buffer stays level. From day 15 no pass arrives for twelve days, the buffer climbs to the 14-day capacity of the on-board recorder (the dotted level on the buffer axis), and from there until a pass lowers it again every new batch is discarded, the interval shaded as Recorder full. The second marker falls inside the gap and still opens its low-latency period.\n\n(Image: Batch-state raster of the 30-day scenario: the generation diagonal interrupted by the safe mode and by the recorder-full interval)\n\nIn the raster the generation diagonal has two vertical steps, the safe mode and the recorder-full interval, during which mission time advances without new batches.","title":"Thirty Days with a Contact Gap"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#Coarse-Batches","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/long_segments_2400s.toml: 2400-second segments at 0.5 Hz, three per batch, so a batch spans two hours and a day produces twelve.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the long-segment scenario: the received count as a visible staircase of single batches)\n\nWith one batch every forty minutes of contact, the counts are visible staircases at the scale of the week and the buffer moves in single batches.\n\n(Image: Batch-state raster of the long-segment scenario: single batches as cells, the archive block of each pass delivered newest first)\n\nAt this batch size the raster resolves single batches. The stepped lower edge of each green block is the LIFO order, one batch per forty minutes from the newest backward, and the dark-blue cells are batches in flight on the link. The three oldest batches stay there across the blind spots until a pass has delivered everything newer.","title":"Coarse Batches"},{"category":"section","location":"gallery/#External-Ingestion","page":"Scenario Gallery","text":"scenarios/external_ingest.toml: two days reading the generated example series (16 Hz) through the same pipeline; the series is written by scripts/maintenance/generate_example_strain.jl.\n\n(Image: Mission summary of the external-ingestion scenario: two passes draining a half-day backlog)\n\nThe telemetry layers do not distinguish the payload source: segmentation, batching, routing, and the figures are those of the synthetic runs.","title":"External Ingestion"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Physics-and-Queuing-Theory","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"DeepSpaceTelemetry models the telemetry environment of a deep-space science mission: a duty-cycled ground-station contact, a physical downlink with stochastic loss and scheduled disruptions, and the priority order in which data are sent to the ground. The telemetry, channel, and queuing layers are mission-agnostic; the shipped scenarios model the LISA mission.","title":"Physics & Queuing Theory"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Virtual-instrument-(payload)","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"The telemetry layers do not read the payload: routing, loss, and delay depend on the batch cadence alone. In synthetic mode I therefore reduce the payload to a binary flag series, 0 on a segment holding noise only and 1 on a segment holding a flagged signal. A segment is flagged when its content span [epoch, epoch + segment_duration_sec) holds the instant of an event marker ([[events.markers]]), so the series is declared by the configuration and involves no random draw. Restricted to the rows that RunAdapter.delivered_rows returns at an instant, it states which samples of an event had reached the ground by then.\n\nA physical noise or waveform model enters through physics.data_source = \"external\": the series of a CSV file is cut into the same segments and passes through the same pipeline. Both sources are sampled on one grid anchored at the start of the initial blind spot, the content instant of the first sample. A generation gap or an emitter restart suppresses whole segments of that grid without shifting it, as the sampling clock of an instrument keeps its phase through an interruption of recording. A file with several columns is ingested as one payload (physics.external_data_columns), every column on the same grid and under one link accounting.\n\nEvery timestamp of a run (content epochs, event instants, the exported seconds) is referred to simulation.start_sim_time, a calendar instant without a time-scale attribution; the simulator carries no conversion between TCB and UTC. The LISA science ground segment conventions refer every time grid to the epoch LISA_EPOCH_TCB = 2035-01-01 00:00 TCB (Baghi et al. 2026, §7.1). The shipped scenarios therefore place their missions in 2035: most start on that calendar day, the seasonal cases later in the year.","title":"Virtual instrument (payload)"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Bandwidth-Profiling","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"Satellite-to-ground communication is constrained by the ground station's line of sight. The daily contact window opens at telemetry.session_start for telemetry.session_duration_hours (sessions crossing midnight are handled); outside it the capacity is zero, and within it the fractional capacity follows a selectable profile of the window progress x ∈ [0, 1]:\n\nsine: sin²(πx), zero at both horizons, full capacity at culmination, 50 % on average.\nsigmoid: [tanh(kx) + tanh(k(1 − x))] / 2 with k = telemetry.sigmoid_steepness, a fast rise at either horizon to a plateau of tanh(k/2) (0.9999 at the default k = 10). The edges are centered on the window boundaries, so the capacity at the session edges is ≈ 0.5: the profile models an abrupt acquisition.\ngaussian: exp(−(x − ½)² / (2σ²)) with σ = telemetry.gaussian_sigma, a pass concentrated around culmination.\nflat: constant full capacity throughout the window.\n\nA link at a fixed data rate, as the Report describes for LISA, is designed to close at the lowest elevation and holds its rate through the pass, which is the flat profile. The shaped profiles are stress abstractions of a partially usable pass. The budget of an X-band link varies by only a few dB between zenith and a 10° elevation mask, through atmospheric attenuation and noise temperature (DSN 810-005, module 105), so a capacity that falls to zero at the horizons has no link-budget basis. With the LISA rate pair and 8-hour passes the sine profile delivers about half of the daily production.","title":"Bandwidth Profiling"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Link-Capacity-and-the-24-Hour-Requirement","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"The link capacity is configured either as telemetry.max_batches_per_hour, the scenario abstraction, or physically as the downlink data rate telemetry.downlink_kbps against the on-board production rate telemetry.onboard_data_rate_kbps. In the physical form one batch of content span D takes D · production / downlink seconds at full capacity, and the catch-up ratio downlink / production is the number of hours of production that one hour of contact drains. At a lower capacity a transfer lasts until the capacity integrated from its start reaches that time, ∫ c(t) dt = D · production / downlink; the capacity is zero outside the contact windows, so a transfer interrupted by the end of a pass or by a blackout resumes at the next contact. Contact windows are half-open, [start, stop). The LISA Definition Study Report gives ≈ 75 kbit/s of production (Colpi et al. 2024, §6.3.2) and a 230 kbit/s downlink with communication windows of 8 hours per day (Table 6.2). Its requirement that data reach the ground within 24 hours of measurement demands more than 225 kbit/s at 8 hours of contact per day (§6.3.2), so the ratio of ≈ 3.07 leaves a margin of about 2 %.\n\nThe delivery-delay metric (Metrology.delivery_delay_table) reports, per run, the distribution of the delay from the end of each generated batch's content span to its arrival, and the fraction within post_processing.delivery_requirement_hours. fraction_within counts every generated batch, delivered or not. A batch generated less than the requirement before the end of the run is not yet due, and its delay cannot be judged within the run; delivery_delay.csv marks the due batches (Due), and fraction_within_resolved counts the batches due or delivered by the end of the run together with the batches the on-board recorder discarded at its ceiling, which never reach the ground.","title":"Link Capacity and the 24-Hour Requirement"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Contact-Schedule-and-Low-Latency-Periods","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"The daily window of [telemetry] generates the nominal passes; [contacts] shapes it. A seasonal term widens the window symmetrically about its center by seasonal_extension_hours · ½ [1 + cos(2π (d − d_peak) / P)], with the day of year d, the peak day season_peak_day_of_year, and the period season_period_days. The term is evaluated on the calendar date of each pass. A mission spanning months sees its passes lengthen and shorten with the season, from the bare window half a period from the peak to the window plus the full extension at the peak; a year-long mission traverses the whole cycle. simulation.start_sim_time sets the phase at which the mission enters that cycle; with seasonal_extension_hours = 0 the date has no effect on the passes. The day of year restarts on 1 January, so the term repeats from year to year only for a period of one year.\n\nThe Definition Study Report quotes pass durations of 8 to 12 hours over the year for the ESTRACK 35 m antennas (Colpi et al. 2024, §6.3.2), which the scenarios model as an 8-hour window with a 4-hour extension. The Report gives no peak day. The shipped scenarios take the phase from a northern-hemisphere 35 m station (Cebreros). The shipped scenarios take the constellation trailing Earth by 20°, as ESA's LISA factsheet describes it (\"behind the Earth\"). The Report allows either sense (±20°, Table 6.2), and its §6.2.1 counts a positive angle as leading and gives the baseline as 20°, which read literally is a leading constellation; the phase below rests on the factsheet. Trailing by 20°, the constellation sits about 80° east of the Sun, its geocentric declination peaks near day 89 and bottoms near day 276, and a northern station's pass is longest at the maximum, hence season_peak_day_of_year = 89; a southern station (Malargüe, New Norcia) would peak near day 276. A constellation leading by 20° sits about 80° west of the Sun, and the peak of a northern station moves to about day 255. The model keeps the Report's range and takes only the phase from this geometry: above a 10° elevation mask Cebreros alone sees the constellation for 6.9 h at the declination minimum and 12.9 h at the maximum, so the 8-hour floor is the Report's operational allocation (Table 6.2, §9.1.1) and not the visibility of one station.\n\nOf the shipped scenarios, recovery_12h_seasonal.toml starts on 30 March (day 89: 12-hour passes, within minutes of the maximum throughout its week) and long_30d_seasonal.toml on 20 July (day 201, on the declining side: passes of about 9.3 h shortening to about 8.5 h over its thirty days). Every other scenario starts on 1 January 2035 with the term disabled, so the date does not act on its passes: 8 hours each, except the explicit pass list of explicit_schedule.toml.\n\n[[contacts.exceptions]] state the window of a date as given (a zero duration is a missed pass). An explicit pass list, [[contacts.passes]] or a schedule_csv with the columns Start, DurationHours, replaces the generator altogether for planned schedules. Batches finalized inside a contact window are LIVE unless a full blackout holds the link at zero (Link-Disruption Events), and the capacity profile is evaluated at the position within the actual window, so an extended or shortened pass keeps its horizon-to-horizon shape.\n\nLow-latency periods ([[contacts.low_latency_periods]]) are the extra contacts a mission arranges around a well-localized transient: quasi-real-time download outside the nominal pass, subject to station availability. Each period supplies a constant fraction of the peak capacity (capacity_fraction) for its duration and is an ordinary contact for the emitter (live classification) and the receiver (transfers). Where a period overlaps a nominal pass, the pass and its profile take precedence. The delivery-delay table flags batches that reached the ground inside a period, and the compliance summary counts them. A comparison with and without the periods takes a pair of runs differing only in contacts.low_latency_enabled, because the realized queue depends on the link history and cannot be replayed from one event log.","title":"Contact Schedule and Low-Latency Periods"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Packet-Loss-Channel-Models","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"Each batch transfer attempt on the downlink draws one loss realization from the configured [packet_loss] model:\n\nBernoulli: memoryless i.i.d. loss with probability p_loss, the baseline.\nGilbert–Elliott: the bursty-channel model of Gilbert (1960) and Elliott (1963). A two-state Markov chain alternates between a GOOD state (loss probability p_loss_good) and a BAD state (p_loss_bad). It starts in GOOD and makes one transition before each draw, at probabilities p_good_to_bad and p_bad_to_good. Its long-run loss rate is π_bad·p_loss_bad + π_good·p_loss_good, with π_bad = p_good_to_bad / (p_good_to_bad + p_bad_to_good) and π_good = 1 − π_bad; stationary_loss_rate returns it, and the test suite checks it against the sampled stream. The original models step the chain once per transmitted digit. Here it steps once per batch transfer attempt, so a burst lasts a number of attempts whatever their duration, and the state persists through the blind spots between passes. The shipped parameters are stress values and are not fitted to an X-band link.\n\nA lost transfer leaves the batch on the link, and the whole batch is sent again. The loss is detected on the ground when the transfer completes, as in CFDP's deferred lost-segment detection, which issues the negative acknowledgment on receipt of the end-of-file PDU (CCSDS 727.0-B-5, §4.6.4.6). The retransmission can be served no earlier than one round-trip light time 2 · range / c later, with the range from telemetry.range_million_km (≈ 334 s at 50 × 10⁶ km; 0 disables the delay). I model the other in-flight batches as served meanwhile, as concurrent CFDP transactions are; the link idles only when every in-flight batch is waiting for its round trip. After max_retries + 1 failed transfers (the first attempt and max_retries retransmissions) the batch moves to lost/ (data preserved, mask state 4), which frees its in-flight slot on the link. on_loss = \"drop\" is shorthand for a zero-retry budget.\n\nWhole-batch retransmission is an adaptation. CFDP requests only the missing segments, so the model charges each loss a full transfer time where the protocol would charge the lost frames. The delay statistics follow from the batch-level loss probability; the capacity consumed by retransmission is an upper bound. For scale, at the frame rejection rate of 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁵ that the DSN quotes as typical (DSN 820-100, §3.2.1.2), a 600 s LISA batch of about 5000 frames of 8920 bits holds a rejected frame with probability 0.4 to 0.05, while the rejected volume is 10⁻⁴ to 10⁻⁵ of the batch. With 2 % of margin at 8-hour passes, a batch-loss probability above that margin makes the backlog grow, which is the regime of the stress scenario.\n\nThe receiver records the outcome of a transfer (ingested, retry, lost) at its completion instant, and a transfer starts when the previous one has completed or later. The loss multiplier of a transfer is the largest of the disruption events active while it transmits; an event confined to an interruption of the transfer does not count. A transfer that would complete at or after the mission horizon has no outcome: its batch stays on the link. Two simplifications remain. The range is held fixed over the mission; on the Report's baseline orbit it drifts between about 45 and 60 × 10⁶ km (Colpi et al. 2024, Figure 6.2 and §6.3.2). And the one-way light time is not modelled, so delivery delays and L(δ) are one one-way light time (≈ 167 s at 50 × 10⁶ km) short of the physical values.","title":"Packet Loss Channel Models"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Link-Disruption-Events","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"[[disruption.events]] entries superimpose scheduled link-degrading events on the daily visibility schedule: a spacecraft safe mode, for instance after a solar energetic particle event, or a ground-station outage. During the blackout phase the link capacity is multiplied by 1 − severity; afterward it ramps linearly back to nominal over recovery_hours. Throughout blackout and recovery the stochastic loss probability is multiplied by loss_multiplier and clamped to [0, 1]; a multiplier below 1 has no effect. Overlapping events compose conservatively (minimum capacity, maximum loss multiplier).\n\nThe scenarios label their 18-hour outage a solar particle event, whose physical reading is the safe mode such an event can trigger: the Sun does not degrade this link through propagation, since solar-plasma scintillation raises X-band frame errors only below a Sun–Earth–probe angle of about 2.3° (DSN 810-005, module 106, §2.2.1), and LISA stays about 80° from the Sun. The elevated loss of the recovery phase is a scenario parameter without a propagation basis.\n\nThe emitter gates transmission on the effective link, the geometric visibility times the disruption factor. Data generated while the effective link is zero, as in a full blackout, is stamped ARCH_ and accumulates on board like blind-spot data, and the post-event drain follows the same LIVE-FIFO/ARCH-LIFO routing.","title":"Link-Disruption Events"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Scheduled-Generation-Gaps-and-the-On-Board-Recorder","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"A disruption event with affects = \"generation\" interrupts data production instead of the link. It is the default for type = \"antenna_repointing\": an antenna rotation is a planned interruption of science that degrades it for minutes afterward (Colpi et al. 2024, §6.3.2 and §9.1.1). The emitter produces nothing between the event start and start + duration_hours, discards the segments of the batch left incomplete at the gap start (as in an emitter outage, to keep the batch geometry uniform), and bounds the gap in events_tx.csv (gap_start / gap_end, Batch = SCHEDULED). Gap boundaries snap to segment boundaries; the severity, recovery, and loss keys of such an event are ignored. The downlink is unaffected: while the link is up, the emitter keeps placing buffered batches on it throughout the gap.\n\nThe on-board recorder holds telemetry.onboard_capacity_days of production (default 14 days, the Report's autonomy without ground contact; Colpi et al. 2024, §9.1.1), converted to a ceiling in batches through the batch content span. The emitter enforces it without eviction: a batch finalized while the buffer sits at the ceiling is discarded, the loss bounded by gap_start / gap_end rows with Batch = RECORDER (closed at the first finalization that finds room again). Configuration validation warns when the initial blind spot, or the longest interval without ground contact in the schedule, exceeds the capacity. The mission summary shades scheduled gaps and recorder overflows (a span narrower than 0.6 % of the plotted range is widened to that width so it stays visible) and draws the capacity on the buffer axis when it was reached.","title":"Scheduled Generation Gaps and the On-Board Recorder"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Queuing-Theory:-LIFO-vs-FIFO","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"Between contacts the spacecraft accumulates a backlog (16 blind hours per day under the 8 h passes of most shipped LISA scenarios). When the link opens, the satellite routes data as a two-class priority queue whose classes are served FIFO and LIFO (Kleinrock 1976, §3.5 and §3.6; non-preemptive priorities after Cobham 1954). Service is non-preemptive: a transfer in service completes before the next selection. The ordering is a design choice of this package. The LISA documents state a 24-hour delivery requirement and low-latency periods, and no downlink order.\n\nNear-real-time (live) data: highest priority, sent first-in, first-out (FIFO) so the ground sees the newest observations with the smallest latency.\nArchive backfill (LIFO): the remaining bandwidth drains the backlog last-in, first-out, newest archived data first. For a transient caught live (in the LISA case a massive black-hole binary merger), the archived data immediately preceding it are the most valuable: the sky localization of such a binary improves rapidly in the last hours before merger (Colpi et al. 2024, Figure 9.2). LIFO delivery lets alert pipelines extend the waveform backward from the live event without a gap.\n\nThe receiver selects among the batches on the link, and the emitter places a batch there only while fewer than max_inflight_batches are in flight. A live batch finalized while that window is full can therefore wait one archive transfer longer than head-of-the-line priority would allow.\n\n(Image: Batch-routing animation: one row per stage, sky blue for live batches and green for archive ones)\n\nThe doctrine batch by batch, over one run of scenarios/stress_8h_bursty.toml. Each row is a stage (buffered on the satellite, in flight on the link, delivered on the ground), and the color gives the routing family, sky blue for live (FIFO) and green for archive (LIFO); the marker shape repeats the distinction. Live batches cross to the ground as they are generated, so the sky-blue runs mark the passes; between them the archive segment grows backward in batch identifier, contiguous with the live tail, which is the LIFO backfill. Through the blackout the ground row stands still while the onboard block extends to the right. The status line carries the mission clock (Day N counts elapsed days, as on the summary axis, and the time of day is that of the session figures), the link state, and the buffer counters of that frame.","title":"Queuing Theory: LIFO vs FIFO"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Alert-Latency-Metrology","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"The alert-latency metric measures the effect of the LIFO backfill from the event logs (Metrology.alert_latency_table). Every live batch that reached the ground defines an alert: the event instant t_m is the content end of that batch (the moment the transient's samples exist on board). For a look-back δ, the window [t_m − δ, t_m) together with the alert batch itself is complete on the ground once every batch overlapping it has arrived; the latency L(δ) is that completion instant minus t_m, so L(δ ≤ D) is the delivery delay of the live batch itself and L(δ) is non-decreasing for each alert. Under the realized doctrine the arrival instants are the recorded ingested events. The counterfactual first-in, first-out drain assigns the same successful transfers (the sorted ingested instants, with their link, slots and losses) to the batches in content order: each goes to the oldest batch already generated and still undelivered, with no live priority. The table reports the median and the interquartile band of L(δ) over all alerts on the grid δ = 0, D, 2D, … (D = batch content span) up to post_processing.alert_lookback_hours.\n\nAn alert whose window reaches before the first batch is left out at that δ. A window that holds a batch that had not arrived by the end of the run is censored: its latency exceeds the time from t_m to the end of the run. A window that holds data the on-board recorder discarded never completes, nor, under the realized doctrine, one that holds a batch lost after its last retransmission; the counterfactual drain reassigns the realized transfers and may deliver that batch. The table carries two estimates. The complete-case quantiles (LIFO_Median_Hours and the like, population N_Alerts) use the alerts whose windows completed under both doctrines, which requires the counterfactual drain to reach the alert batch within the mission, and count a window over discarded data as complete, since none of its batches exists; the population shrinks with δ, so these medians need not be monotone, and in a run that ends with a backlog they understate the latency of the alerts they exclude. The product-limit (Kaplan–Meier) quantiles (LIFO_KM_Median_Hours and the like, population N_Alerts_All, censored counts N_Censored_LIFO and N_Censored_FIFO) use every live batch delivered under the realized doctrine (Kaplan & Meier 1958); a quantile that the estimate does not reach within the run is reported as NaN. The estimator assumes censoring independent of the latency, which a backlog at the end of the run violates in part, since late alerts are censored early. The figure draws the product-limit estimates.\n\nUnder LIFO the window fills backward from the live stream. For an alert τ hours into a pass, the pass has already delivered the live data since its start and archive data reaching about (c − 1)·τ further back, c the catch-up ratio net of retransmissions, so L(δ) stays at L(0) up to δ ≈ c·τ. Beyond that it grows with slope 1/(c − 1) (about 0.5 h per hour of look-back on the LISA link). Each time the backfill continues into a later pass it steps up by the blind spot B before that pass and by the time to drain it, since the newer blind-spot data go first under LIFO: B + B/(c − 1) = B·c/(c − 1), about 18 h for a 12-hour blind spot at c ≈ 3.07. Under FIFO the newest batches of the window arrive last, so L(δ) sits near the time needed to drain the whole backlog generated before the event.\n\nEvent markers ([[events.markers]]) are the instants of interest a scenario declares, such as a transient or a glitch. The alert latency is evaluated at each of them as well (Metrology.marker_latency_table), with the batch whose content span holds the marker as the alert batch and the marker instant as t_m; each marker's L(δ) is written to alert_latency_markers.csv and drawn over the population bands. The end-to-end alert latency adds the ground processing budget ground.processing_latency_hours, which the figure annotates. Its default of 1 h is the Report's requirement that the low-latency alert pipeline finish within one hour of the L0 data reaching the ground stations (Colpi et al. 2024, §8.5); the Report's end-to-end figure is a total latency below 1 h from data generation to updated source parameters inside a low-latency period (§9.1.1). A marker may also trigger a low-latency period low_latency_after_hours after its instant (Contact Schedule and Low-Latency Periods). The emitter stamps the labels of the markers held by a batch into its metadata.json and appends a marker row to events_tx.csv when that batch is finalized.","title":"Alert-Latency Metrology"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#Mission-Clocks:-Wall-and-Tick","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"simulation.mode selects one of two clocks. The physics (contact windows, capacity profile, loss channel, the round-trip deferral of retransmissions, the recorder ceiling, the routing doctrine) is written in mission time and is the same under both. The clocks differ only in how mission time advances.\n\nBoth modes run on one driver (TelemetryCore.run_ticks!). The emitter and the receiver are cooperative tasks on one thread: each task computes the mission instant it next needs (the end of the segment being observed, the completion of the transfer in flight, the earliest deferred retransmission) and parks; when every task is parked the clock jumps to the earliest request and releases that task alone. The record is therefore a deterministic function of the configuration and the seed in both modes. In both modes the driver refreshes the liveness files emitter_alive and receiver_alive and applies the [supervision] policy to a component that fails.\n\nWall mode (\"wall\", the default and the operational mode) paces the driver against the wall clock: before releasing a wakeup at mission instant t it sleeps until speed_up mission seconds per wall second have passed since the start, for a span of speed_up · mission_wall_seconds. A consumer developed against an accelerated run follows a real-time rehearsal (speed_up = 1) unchanged. The wall budget is nominal: a host that lags releases late and finishes later, with the same content.\n\nTick mode (\"tick\", the analysis mode) runs the driver unpaced: nothing sleeps and the run takes as long as its events take to process. The span is mission_days; speed_up and mission_wall_seconds have no meaning and are rejected, as is the console status panel, and the dashboard launcher refuses the mode. The run directory, the sentinels, and every product are those of a wall run; clock_anchor.toml records the mode and the horizon, and for a wall run the speed-up and the nominal wall deadline as well.\n\nAt an equal instant the emitter runs before the receiver (TelemetryCore.EMITTER_PRIORITY, RECEIVER_PRIORITY), because the instrument delivers a segment after observing it and the ground serves what is on the link once it is there. A task's own requests are released in registration order. These two rules make the schedule total. The receiver's idle wait resumes after the emitter's next wakeup, since the link changes only when the emitter runs. Its waits for the completion of a transfer and for a deferred retransmission also end at the emitter's next wakeup, so the receiver writes the profile throughout a transfer that spans a blind spot, and a batch placed on the link in the meantime is served without waiting out the round trip (TelemetryCore.wait_until_or_change!). At the horizon the clock stops: a segment whose content interval ends at the horizon is not generated, a transfer that would complete at or after the horizon has no outcome, and both tasks leave at their next iteration.\n\nOn one platform and one Julia version, two runs of the same configuration and seed produce the same events_tx.csv, events_rx.csv, mask timeline, batch epochs, and metrics profile apart from its WallTime column. A wall run gives the same record as the tick run of the same configuration with mission_days = speed_up · mission_wall_seconds / 86 400. The test suite asserts both, and asserts the production count from the span alone. Across platforms the record should agree as well, since routing, counts, and event order follow from integer arithmetic on the millisecond grid and from the Xoshiro stream of the loss channel.","title":"Mission Clocks: Wall and Tick"},{"category":"section","location":"physics/#References","page":"Physics & Queuing Theory","text":"Baghi, Q., Babak, S., Barack, L., et al., LISA science ground segment conventions, LISA-DDPC-SEG-TN-007, issue 1 revision 10 (2026), arXiv:2603.22377v1, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2603.22377.\nCCSDS, CCSDS File Delivery Protocol (CFDP), Recommended Standard CCSDS 727.0-B-5, Blue Book, Issue 5 (2020).\nCobham, A., Priority assignment in waiting line problems, Journal of the Operations Research Society of America 2, 70–76 (1954), doi:10.1287/opre.2.1.70.\nColpi, M., et al., LISA Definition Study Report, ESA-SCI-DIR-RP-002 (2024), arXiv:2402.07571, doi:10.48550/arXiv.2402.07571.\nElliott, E. O., Estimates of error rates for codes on burst-noise channels, Bell System Technical Journal 42, 1977–1997 (1963), doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1963.tb00955.x.\nESA, LISA factsheet, esa.int/ScienceExploration/SpaceScience/LISA_factsheet.\nGilbert, E. N., Capacity of a burst-noise channel, Bell System Technical Journal 39, 1253–1265 (1960), doi:10.1002/j.1538-7305.1960.tb03959.x.\nJet Propulsion Laboratory, Deep Space Network Services Catalog, DSN No. 820-100, Rev. H (2022), deepspace.jpl.nasa.gov/files/820-100-H.pdf.\nJet Propulsion Laboratory, DSN Telecommunications Link Design Handbook, DSN No. 810-005: module 105, Atmospheric and Environmental Effects; module 106, Rev. B, Solar Corona and Solar Wind Effects (2010), deepspace.jpl.nasa.gov/dsndocs/810-005.\nKaplan, E. L., Meier, P., Nonparametric estimation from incomplete observations, Journal of the American Statistical Association 53, 457–481 (1958), doi:10.1080/01621459.1958.10501452.\nKleinrock, L., Queueing Systems, Volume II: Computer Applications, Wiley, New York (1976), ISBN 0-471-49111-X.","title":"References"},{"category":"section","location":"api/receiver/#Receiver","page":"Receiver","text":"","title":"Receiver"},{"category":"module","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver","page":"Receiver","text":"Receiver\n\nThe ground-station loop: bandwidth-paced ingestion with stochastic loss and retransmission, the retention custodian, and the metrics and events_rx.csv recording. Re-entrant: a restarted receiver reseeds retry and custodial state from the event log. The derived products live in Masks and MissionFigures.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.idle_wait!","page":"Receiver","text":"idle_wait!(clock::TelemetryCore.TickClock, link_path::String)\n\nThe receiver's wait while nothing is on the link: a park on the tick clock until another component has run (TelemetryCore.wait_for_change!), since the link changes only when the emitter does.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.idle_wait!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.run_receiver","page":"Receiver","text":"run_receiver(clock, link, run_dir; ...)\n\nThe ground-station loop: serves the batches on <run_dir>/link/, each transfer lasting until the effective link capacity (visibility profile × disruption factor) integrated from its start reaches batch_transfer_sec (ChannelEffects.transfer_completion), so that a transfer interrupted by the end of a contact or a blackout resumes at the next contact; it draws one loss realization from loss_model per transfer attempt, with the largest loss multiplier of the events active during the transfer, and records the outcome at the completion instant. A transfer that would complete at or after the horizon has no outcome. The loop moves delivered batches to ground/ and retry-exhausted ones to lost/. Every milestone is appended to events_rx.csv, from which the post-processing reconstructs the run exactly. run_dir is the absolute path of the run directory.\n\nclock is the TelemetryCore.TickClock of the mission; every wait parks the task on it. The loop ends at the horizon, on the stop flag, or on the HALT sentinel (TelemetryCore.loop_should_stop).\n\nA lost transfer leaves the batch on the link; its retransmission is served no earlier than round_trip_light_time_sec after the loss was detected (deferred negative acknowledgment), while the other in-flight batches keep being served. A batch whose transfer fails max_retries + 1 times moves to lost/, data preserved, which frees its slot in the emitter's transmission window (in-flight occupancy is the link/ listing). A re-attaching receiver reseeds retry and custodial state from the event log (reattach_state).\n\nWith retention.enabled the loop also runs the retention custodian (custodian_step!): while the delivered-payload tally exceeds watermark_bytes, the oldest-ingested batches, once at least grace of mission time past their ingestion, lose their seg_*.csv payload files, keep metadata.json, gain a PRUNED marker, and are recorded as pruned in events_rx.csv. Event logs, metrics, masks, and lost/ are not pruned.\n\nKeyword arguments\n\norig_stdout: stream receiving the console status panel.\nstatus_panel: render the clear-screen console status panel (status_panel_text) to orig_stdout on every loop iteration; off by default, set by the supervisor from dashboard.receiver_status_panel.\nbatch_transfer_sec: transfer time of one batch at full link capacity [mission s] (telemetry_settings(cfg).nominal_batch_transfer_sec); at a lower capacity the transfer lasts until the integrated capacity reaches it.\nloss_model: stochastic packet-loss channel (ChannelEffects.LossModel).\nmax_retries: retransmissions allowed per batch; 0 moves a batch to lost/ at its first failed transfer.\nretention: the custodian's TelemetryCore.RetentionPolicy.\nstop: cooperative stop flag raised by the supervisor.\nmin_link_factor: capacity floor below which no transfer is attempted.\nround_trip_light_time_sec: earliest retransmission delay after the completion of a failed transfer [mission s]; 0.0 retries immediately.\nhole_feed: the declared-hole feed the receiver appends its lost and pruned batches to, nothing for none.\nonboard_count: the emitter's count of batches on board (Emitter.run_emitter), read for the metrics; nothing lists onboard/ on every iteration instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.run_receiver"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.reattach_state","page":"Receiver","text":"reattach_state(run_dir, link_path, ground_names, clock, retention) -> NamedTuple\n\nRetry and custodial state of a receiver attaching to a run, from its events_rx.csv: retry_counts, the failed attempts of each batch still on the link; total_retries, the run's retry rows; and payload_bytes, the delivered-payload tally (zero unless retention.enabled). A restarted receiver must not grant fresh retry budgets to in-flight batches nor forget custodial state. Batches of ground_names without an ingested record witness a crash between the delivery move and the log append; their record is synthesized at the current mission instant, as the delivery time is unrecoverable, so masks, custodian, and consumers agree the batch is delivered. A run without events yields the empty state.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.reattach_state"},{"category":"type","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.RetentionCustodian","page":"Receiver","text":"RetentionCustodian\n\nState of the retention custodian: queue, the delivered batches as (ingest instant, name, payload bytes), oldest-ingested first, materialized on watermark breach (delivered_payload_queue) and empty between breach episodes; and payload_bytes, the running tally of prunable payload compared with the watermark.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.RetentionCustodian"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.custodian_step!","page":"Receiver","text":"custodian_step!(custodian, retention, run_dir, sim_t; hole_feed = nothing)\n\nOne pass of the retention custodian at sim_t: while the tally exceeds retention.watermark_bytes, the oldest-ingested batch at least retention.grace past its ingestion loses its payload. Each prune appends the pruned event row and declares the hole first, then writes the PRUNED marker, then deletes the payload, so a consumer never sees a payload-less batch without its pruned row. An I/O failure is logged and leaves the tally unchanged, as the files are still on disk; the batch is not re-queued.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.custodian_step!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.status_panel_text","page":"Receiver","text":"status_panel_text(link, sim_t, nominal_factor, disruption_scale, hours_elapsed,\n                  ground_count, lost_count, total_retries) -> String\n\nThe clear-screen console status panel of the receiver: mission day, link state (dormant, active, or the active disruption with its recovery level), and the ground and lost tallies.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.status_panel_text"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.payload_bytes","page":"Receiver","text":"payload_bytes(batch_dir::String) -> Int\n\nSize of the seg_*.csv payload files of a batch directory [bytes].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.payload_bytes"},{"category":"function","location":"api/receiver/#DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.delivered_payload_queue","page":"Receiver","text":"delivered_payload_queue(run_dir::String, ground_path::String)\n    -> Vector{Tuple{DateTime,String,Int}}\n\nMaterializes the retention custodian's pruning queue from the run's ground census and events_rx.csv: delivered (ingested) batches whose payload has not been pruned, oldest-ingested first, each with its current seg_*.csv payload size in bytes. Consulted at startup for the payload tally and lazily on watermark breach, so the in-memory queue stays empty outside breach episodes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"DeepSpaceTelemetryHeadless.Receiver.delivered_payload_queue"}]
}
